hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"2401","2565 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Husayn, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Forgetfulness is to stand where one should sit, or sit where one should stand, or give salutation in the two rakahs; he gives salutation, then prostrates the two prostrations of forgetfulness, recites tashahhud, and gives salutation."
"2402","2566 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Ufayr narrated to us, saying: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Qurrah ibn Abd al-Rahman, he narrated to him from Amr ibn Dinar, he narrated to him from Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The two prostrations of forgetfulness are after the salutation."
"2403","2567 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah narrated to us, from Zayd, from Jabir, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I prayed behind Ibn al-Zubayr, and he gave salutation in the two rakahs, so the people said Subhan Allah, and he stood and completed the prayer; when he gave salutation, he prostrated two prostrations after the salutation. Ata said: I went to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and mentioned to him what Ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, did, and he said: He did well and correctly."
"2404","2568 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Yusuf ibn Mahak, he said: Ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, led us in prayer and stood in the first two rakahs of Zuhr, so we said Subhan Allah to him, and he said Subhan Allah and did not turn to them, and completed what was upon him, then prostrated two prostrations after giving salutation."
"2405","2569 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abu Bishr informed me, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2406","2570 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said regarding a man who is uncertain in his prayer, not knowing whether he added or decreased: He prostrates two prostrations after giving salutation."
"2407","2571 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Fulayh narrated to us, from Damrah ibn Said, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he prayed behind Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, and was made uncertain, so he prostrated two prostrations after the salutation."
"2408","2572 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Mamar narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb narrated to us, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that he stood in the second rakah, so the people said Subhan Allah to him, and he stood fully four, then prostrated two prostrations after giving salutation, then said: When you are uncertain, do thus. And this Imran ibn Husayn had attended the prostration of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of al-Khirbaq for the addition he had added in his prayer, after the salutation, then he himself said after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The prostration for forgetfulness is after the salutation, without distinguishing between what of that was for addition or deficiency. That indicates that the prostration he attended of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for the forgetfulness he had been inattentive in his prayer at that time was, in his view, that every prostration for every forgetfulness in prayer is likewise."
"2409","2573 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed me that Khalid al-Hadhdha informed them, from Abu Qilabah, from Imran ibn Husayn, he said: Regarding the two prostrations of forgetfulness: he gives salutation, then prostrates, then gives salutation. Al-Zuhri mentioned to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz the prostration of forgetfulness before the salutation, but he did not adopt it."
"2410","2574 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, he said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Said ibn Abd al-Aziz, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to me, he said: I said to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz: The prostration is before the salutation? But he did not adopt it. This is the aspect of this chapter by way of reports. As for its aspect by way of reasoning, we saw that a man when he is inattentive in his prayer is not commanded to prostrate for forgetfulness at the moment of forgetfulness, but is commanded to delay it. Some said: To after the salutation, and others said: To the end of his prayer before the salutation. One who recites a prostration verse in his prayer so prostration becomes due upon him by his recitation, or remembers while in his prayer that a prostration is upon him for what preceded of it, is commanded to perform it then, and is not commanded to delay it to other than that place of his prayer. So what becomes due of prostration in prayer is performed where it became due thereof, and is not delayed beyond that; and the prostration of forgetfulness is agreed to be delayed from the place of forgetfulness until all the prayer passes, except the salutation, for there is disagreement about placing it before the forgetfulness prostration or placing the forgetfulness prostration before it. So reasoning upon what we mentioned is that the ruling of the disputed salutation be the ruling of what precedes it of the agreed-upon prayer. Just as that is placed before the forgetfulness prostration, so likewise the salutation is placed before the forgetfulness prostration, by analogy and reasoning upon what we mentioned. And this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them. Chapter 65: Speech in prayer due to forgetfulness occurring therein."
"2411","2575 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: A shaykh, I think Abu Zayd al-Harawi, narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, he said: I heard Abu Qilabah narrate from his uncle Abu al-Muhallab, from Imran ibn Husayn, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Zuhr with them three rakahs, then gave salutation and left. Al-Khirbaq said to him: O Messenger of Allah, you prayed three. He said: So he came and prayed a rakah, then gave salutation, then prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness, then gave salutation."
"2412","2576 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he said: Al-Khirbaq stood to him, claiming it was the Asr prayer."
"2413","2577 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Mualla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Khalid, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Muhallab, from Imran ibn Husayn, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave salutation in three rakahs, then entered the chamber angry. Al-Khirbaq stood - a man with outspread hands - and said: O Messenger of Allah, has the prayer been shortened, or did you forget? He said: So he came out dragging his cloak, asked, was informed, prayed the rakah he had left, gave salutation, then prostrated two prostrations, then gave salutation."
"2414","2578 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakrah ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed two rakahs for the people, then was inattentive and gave salutation. Dhu al-Yadayn said to him, and he mentioned the like of the hadith of Ibn Awn and Hisham. Their hadith is that he said: Has the prayer been decreased, O Messenger of Allah? He said: No. So he prayed two other rakahs, then gave salutation, then prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness, then gave salutation."
"2415","2579 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in one of the two afternoon prayers, Zuhr or Asr, and my stronger thought is that he mentioned Zuhr, and he prayed the two rakahs, then stood to a wooden post in the front of the mosque and placed his hands upon it, one upon the other, anger recognized in his face. He said: The hasty people left and said: Has the prayer been shortened? Among the people were Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and Umar, but they were awed to speak to him. A man with long hands stood, whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had named Dhu al-Yadayn, and said: O Messenger of Allah, did you forget or has the prayer been shortened? He said: I did not forget, nor has the prayer been shortened. He said: Rather you forgot, O Messenger of Allah. So he turned to the people and said: Has Dhu al-Yadayn spoken truth? They said: Yes. So he came and prayed with us the remaining two rakahs, then gave salutation, then said takbir, then prostrated like his prostration or longer, then raised his head, said takbir, prostrated like his prostration or longer, then raised his head and said takbir."
"2416","2580 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Ayyub, Ibn Awn, and Salamah ibn Alqamah, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"2417","2581 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him, from Ayyub ibn Abi Tamimah, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished from two, and Dhu al-Yadayn said to him: Has the prayer been shortened? Then he mentioned similar to what follows that in the hadith of Hammad ibn Zayd. He did not mention in this hadith similar to what Hammad mentioned in his hadith, of the saying of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in prayer."
"2418","2582 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in prayer, then he mentioned the like of it."
"2419","2583 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated to us, he said: Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed one of the two afternoon prayers, then he mentioned similar to it; Abu Bakrah did not say in this hadith: He led us in prayer."
"2420","2584 - Muhammad ibn al-Numan narrated to us, he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Labid narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in prayer, then he mentioned the like of it."
"2421","2585 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Abu Sufyan, the freedman of Ibn Abi Ahmad, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in prayer, then he mentioned similar to it."
"2422","2586 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to us, he said: Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in prayer, then he mentioned similar to it."
"2423","2587 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us. (H) 2588 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave salutation in two rakahs, so it was said to him: O Messenger of Allah, has the prayer been shortened? He said: What is that? He was informed of what he did, so he prayed two rakahs, then gave salutation, then prostrated two prostrations while sitting."
"2424","2589 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Imran ibn Abi Anas, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed one day and gave salutation in two rakahs, then left, and Dhu al-Shimalayn caught up with him and said: O Messenger of Allah, has the prayer decreased or did you forget? He said: It has not decreased, nor did I forget. He said: Yes, by the One who sent you with the truth. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Has Dhu al-Yadayn spoken truth? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. So he prayed two rakahs for the people."
"2425","2590 - Ibrahim ibn Munqidh narrated to us, he said: Idris narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Ayyash, from Ibn Hurmuz, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it, with the addition: And he prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness after the salutation."
"2426","2591 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished from two rakahs, and he mentioned similar to that, except that he did not mention the salutation before the prostration. Abu Jafar said: Some people held that speech in prayer from the followers to their imam for what occurred from him does not break the prayer, and that speech from the imam and the followers therein forgetfully does not break the prayer. They argued for their view regarding the speech of the follower to the imam for what he omitted of prayer with the speech of Dhu al-Yadayn to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in these reports we narrated, and for their view that forgetful speech does not break prayer with the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Dhu al-Yadayn: It has not been shortened, nor did I forget, while he thought he was not in prayer. They said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) built upon what he prayed, and that was not breaking for him nor for Dhu al-Yadayn, it is thus established that speech to correct prayer is permissible in prayer, and that forgetful speech in prayer does not break prayer. Others disagreed with them in that and said: Speech in prayer is not permissible except with takbir, tahlil, and recitation of the Quran, and one may not speak therein about anything occurring from the imam therein. They argued for that with:"
"2427","2592 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Hilal ibn Abi Maymunah, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Muawiyah ibn al-Hakam al-Sulami, he said: While I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in prayer, a man sneezed and I said: May Allah have mercy on you. The people stared at me, so I said: Woe to my mother! What is wrong with you looking at me? He said: So the people struck their hands upon their thighs. When I saw them silencing me, I fell silent. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his prayer, he called me; by my father and mother, I never saw a teacher before him nor after him better in teaching than him. By Allah, he did not strike me, nor rebuke me, nor revile me, but said to me: This prayer of ours, nothing of people's speech is proper in it; it is only takbir, tasbih, and recitation of the Quran."
"2428","2593 - Yunus and Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, they both said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to me, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2429","2594 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Fulayh ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Hilal ibn Ali, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Muawiyah ibn al-Hakam, then he mentioned similar to it, with the addition: When you are in it, let that be your practice. Do you not see that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught Muawiyah ibn al-Hakam, when he spoke in prayer, he said to him: This prayer of ours, nothing of people's speech is proper in it; it is only tasbih, takbir, and recitation of the Quran. Since he did not say to him: Or if something befalls you therein of what your imam omitted, speak of it, that indicates that speech in prayer other than tasbih, takbir, and recitation of the Quran breaks it. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught the people afterwards what to do for what befalls them in their prayer."
"2430","2595 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Whoever is faced with something in his prayer, let him say Subhan Allah; clapping is for women, and tasbih is for men."
"2431","2596 - Ibrahim ibn Munqidh narrated to us, he said: al-Muqri narrated to us, from al-Masudi, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went to some people of the Ansar to reconcile between them. The time of prayer came while he was not present, so Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, stepped forward. While he was thus, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, and the people clapped, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gestured to him to remain, but Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, refused until he stepped back, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stepped forward and prayed. When he finished his prayer, he said to Abu Bakr: What prevented you from remaining as I ordered you? He said: It was not for the son of Abu Quhafah to step forward in front of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: And you, why did you clap? They said: To inform Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: Clapping is for women, and tasbih is for men."
"2432","2597 - Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2433","2598 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah narrated to us, he said: al-Thawri narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is faced with something in his prayer, let him say tasbih; tasbih is for men, and clapping is for women."
"2434","2599 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Tasbih is for men and clapping is for women."
"2435","2600 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Yala ibn Ubayd narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Tasbih is for men and clapping is for women. Al-Amash said: I mentioned that to Ibrahim and he said: My mother used to do it."
"2436","2601 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Awf, he said: Muhammad narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2437","2602 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Bukayr informed me, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yaqub ibn Utbah, from Abu Ghatfan, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Jafar said: In these reports the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught them tasbih for every contingency befalling them in prayer, and did not permit them other than it. That indicates that the speech of Dhu al-Yadayn to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with what he spoke to him in the hadith of Imran, Ibn Umar, and Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, was before the prohibition of speech in prayer."
"2438","2603 - Of what also indicates that is that Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, that Suwayd ibn Qays informed him from Muawiyah ibn Hudayj, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed one day and left, while a rakah of the prayer remained; a man caught up with him and said: A rakah of the prayer remains. So he returned to the mosque and ordered Bilal, who called the iqamah, and he prayed a rakah for the people. I informed the people of that, and they said to me: Do you know the man? I said: No, unless I see him. He passed by me and I said: It is this one. They said: This is Talhah ibn Ubaydullah. In this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Bilal, who called the iqamah, then prayed what he had left of his prayer. His ordering Bilal with the iqamah was not breaking his prayer, nor was Bilal's calling of it breaking his prayer. They have agreed that if one did this now while in prayer, he would thereby break the prayer. That indicates that all that was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his prayer, in this hadith of Muawiyah ibn Hudayj, and in the hadith of Ibn Umar, Imran, and Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with them, was while speech was permissible in prayer, then it was abrogated by the abrogation of speech therein. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught the people afterwards what Muawiyah ibn al-Hakam, Abu Hurayrah, and Sahl ibn Sad, may Allah be pleased with them, reported from him. Of what indicates that is that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, had been with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Dhu al-Yadayn, then that incident occurred in his prayer after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he acted therein contrary to what was the act of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that day."
"2439","2604 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Uthman ibn al-Aswad, he said: I heard Ata say: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, led his companions in prayer and gave salutation in two rakahs, then left; it was said to him, and he said: I had prepared a caravan from Iraq with its loads and saddle-bags until it reached Madinah. Then he prayed four rakahs with them. The abandonment by Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, of what he knew of the act of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the like of this, and his acting contrary to it, indicates its abrogation in his view, and that the ruling in that incident in his time was contrary to what was on the day of Dhu al-Yadayn. This act of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was also in the presence of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), some of whom had attended the act of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Dhu al-Yadayn in his prayer, yet they did not object to him, nor say to him: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did on the day of Dhu al-Yadayn contrary to what you did. That also indicates that they knew of its abrogation what Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, knew. Of what also indicates that it is abrogated and that practice is contrary to it is that the ummah has agreed in practice that if a man's imam omits something of his prayer, he says Subhan Allah to him to inform his imam of what he omitted, so he performs it; whereas Dhu al-Yadayn did not say Subhan Allah to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that day, nor did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) object to his speaking to him. That also indicates that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught the people of tasbih for a contingency befalling them in their prayer was later than that. In the hadith of Abu Hurayrah and Imran, may Allah be pleased with them both, is also what indicates abrogation: Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave salutation in two rakahs, then went to a wooden post in the mosque. And Imran said: Then he went to his chamber. That indicates that he had turned his face from the qiblah and performed in prayer an act not of it, of walking and otherwise. Is it permissible for anyone today whom that befalls, while a remainder of his prayer remains upon him, that it does not take him out of prayer? If one says: Yes, it does not take him out of prayer, because he did it without seeing himself as in prayer, he must say: If he also ate or drank in that state, it would not take him out of prayer, and likewise if he bought or sold or had intercourse with his wife; the corruption of his saying suffices to bind its speaker to this. If anything we mentioned takes a man out of his prayer if he does it while seeing himself as not in it, likewise speech which is not of it takes him out of his prayer even if he spoke it while not seeing himself as in it. The proponent of the hadith of Dhu al-Yadayn claimed that the report of a single narrator establishes proof and obliges action; Dhu al-Yadayn informed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he informed him, while he is a trustworthy man among his Companions, yet after his informing him of that, he turned to his Companions and said: Has the prayer been shortened? So he was speaking with that after knowing he was in prayer, according to this opponent of ours, yet that did not take him out of prayer. Thus by this he is bound upon his principle that that speech was before the abrogation of speech in prayer. Another proof: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to the people and said: Has Dhu al-Yadayn spoken truth? They said: Yes. They could have gestured to him of that so he would know it from them, yet they spoke to him with what they spoke, knowing they were in prayer, and he did not object to them nor order them to repeat. That indicates that what we mentioned of what was in the hadith of Dhu al-Yadayn was before the abrogation of speech. If one says: How can this be before the abrogation of speech in prayer, when Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, had attended that, while the Islam of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, was only three years before the death of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? And he mentioned concerning that what they acted upon."
"2440","2605 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: We came to Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: Narrate to us. He said: I accompanied the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) three years. They said: Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, only accompanied the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) three years, while he attended that prayer, and the abrogation of speech in prayer was while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in Makkah. That indicates that what was in the hadith of Dhu al-Yadayn of speech in prayer is of what was not abrogated by the abrogation of speech in prayer, if it was later than that. It is said to him: As for what you mentioned of the time of the Islam of Abu Hurayrah, it is as you mentioned. As for your saying that the abrogation of speech in prayer was while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in Makkah, who narrated that to you, when you argue only with a supported chain and do not allow your opponent proof against you except with the like? Who supported that for you, and from whom did you narrate it? This Zayd ibn Arqam al-Ansari says: We used to speak in prayer until there was revealed: Stand before Allah devoutly, so we were ordered to be silent; we have narrated that from him elsewhere in this book of ours, and the companionship of Zayd with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was only in Madinah. Thus it is established by this hadith of his that the abrogation of speech in prayer was in Madinah after the coming of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Makkah, besides that Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, did not attend that prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at all, because Dhu al-Yadayn was killed on the day of Badr with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), being one of the martyrs. Muhammad ibn Ishaq and others mentioned that. And there has been narrated from Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, what agrees with that."
"2441","2606 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad informed me, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to me, from Abdullah al-Umari, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the hadith of Dhu al-Yadayn was mentioned to him, and he said: The Islam of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, was after Dhu al-Yadayn was killed. The saying of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, in our view - The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in prayer - means the Muslims, and this is permissible in language. And the like of this has been narrated from al-Nazzal ibn Sabrah."
"2442","2607 - Fahd and Abu Zurah al-Dimashqi narrated to us, they both said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Misar narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, from al-Nazzal ibn Sabrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us: We and you were called Banu Abd Manaf; today you are Banu Abdullah, and we are Banu Abdullah, meaning the people of al-Nazzal. This al-Nazzal says: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us, while he never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he means thereby: He said to our people. And there has been narrated from Tawus, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: Muadh ibn Jabal came to us and did not take anything from greens. Tawus did not witness that, because Muadh had only come to Yemen in the era of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while Tawus was not yet born then; the meaning of his saying Came to us is: Came to our land. And it was narrated from al-Hasan that he said: Utbah ibn Ghazwan addressed us, meaning his address in Basrah. Al-Hasan was not in Basrah then, because his coming to it was only a year before Siffin."
"2443","2608 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Idris narrated to us, from Shubah, from Abu Raja, he said: I said to al-Hasan: When did you come to Basrah? He said: A year before Siffin. So the meaning of the saying of al-Nazzal: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us, and the meaning of the saying of Tawus: Muadh came to us, and the meaning of the saying of al-Hasan: Utbah addressed us, is that they mean thereby their people and their land, because they neither attended that nor witnessed it. Likewise the saying of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, in the hadith of Dhu al-Yadayn: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in prayer, means: He prayed with the Muslims, not that he witnessed that nor attended it. Thus by what we mentioned is negated that in his saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in prayer, in the hadith of Dhu al-Yadayn, is anything indicating that it was after the abrogation of speech in prayer. Of what indicates what we mentioned, that the abrogation of speech in prayer was in Madinah, is:"
"2444","2609 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Muhammad ibn Ajlan narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: We used to return salutations in prayer until we were forbidden from that. Abu Said was perhaps in age much younger than Zayd ibn Arqam by a long time, and such is the case; here he is informing that he had witnessed the permissibility of speech in prayer."
"2445","2610 - There has also been narrated concerning that from Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Asim narrated to us, from Abu Wail, he said: Abdullah said: We used to speak in prayer and command needs; we came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Abyssinia while he was praying, so I greeted him and he did not return it to me. I was seized by old and new concern, and when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his prayer, I said: O Messenger of Allah, has something come down concerning me? He said: No, but Allah introduces of His command what He wills."
"2446","2611 - Ismail ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, with the addition: And of what He introduced, He decreed that you not speak in prayer. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed that Allah the Mighty and Majestic abrogated speech in prayer, without excepting anything of that. That indicates all the speech they used to speak in prayer. This is the aspect of this chapter by way of validating the meanings of the reports. As for its aspect by way of reasoning, we have seen things servants enter which prevent them from things. Of them: prayer prevents them from speech and acts not done in it. Of them: fasting prevents them from intercourse, food, and drink. Of them: Hajj and Umrah prevent them from intercourse, perfume, and clothing. Of them: itikaf prevents them from intercourse and activity. One who has intercourse in his fast, or eats or drinks forgetfully, is disputed in ruling. Some say: That does not take him out of his fast, following reports they narrated. Others say: That has taken him out of his fast. Everyone who has intercourse in his Hajj, Umrah, or itikaf, deliberately or forgetfully, has thereby left what he was in thereof. So what takes him out of these things when he does it deliberately also takes him out when he does it undeliberately; and speech in prayer breaks prayer when deliberate, so reasoning upon what we mentioned is that it also breaks it when forgetful, and the ruling of speech therein is the same for deliberate and forgetful, just as the ruling of intercourse in itikaf and Umrah is the same for deliberate and forgetful. This is also the reasoning in this chapter, and it agrees with what we validated of the meanings of the reports; it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them. If one asks about the meaning for which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not order Muawiyah ibn al-Hakam to repeat the prayer when he spoke therein, it is said to him: That is because proof had not been established with him before that of the prohibition of speech in prayer, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not order him to repeat the prayer for that. As for one who does the like after the establishment of proof of the abrogation of speech in prayer, he must repeat the prayer. It is also possible that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to repeat the prayer, but that was not transmitted in his hadith. Some people said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not prostrate on the day of Dhu al-Yadayn."
"2447","2612 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us of that, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: I asked the people of knowledge in Madinah, and none of them informed me that he prayed them, meaning the prostration of forgetfulness on the day of Dhu al-Yadayn. The meaning of this in our view, and Allah knows best, is that the prostration of forgetfulness in prayer is only due when one does therein what should not be done therein, like standing from sitting, or sitting where sitting is not due, or the like, of what if done deliberately its doer would be at fault. As for what is done therein of what is not disliked therein, there is no forgetfulness prostration in it; and the ruling of prayer on the day of Dhu al-Yadayn was that there was no harm in speech and activity therein. When he did that therein forgetfully, while its deliberate doer was not at fault, its forgetful doer incurs no forgetfulness prostration. This is the view of those who held that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not prostrate on that day. This is proof for the people of the view we explained in this chapter. The view of those who mentioned that he prostrated on that day was that speech and activity, though they were permissible in prayer on that day, it was not of the permissible on that day to give salutation in prayer before its time. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave therein a salutation intending thereby to leave it, assuming he had completed it, and that was of what if a doer did deliberately he would be at fault, when he did it forgetfully, the forgetfulness prostration became due therein. This is the view of the people of the view in this hadith."
"2448","Chapter 66: Gesturing in prayer. 2613 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Bukayr informed me, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed me, from Yaqub ibn Utbah, from Abu Ghatfan ibn Tarif, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Tasbih is for men, clapping is for women, and whoever gestures in his prayer a gesture understood from him, let him repeat it. Some people held that an understood gesture, when from a man in prayer, breaks his prayer, and they ruled for it the ruling of speech, arguing for that with this hadith. Others disagreed with them in that and said: Gesturing does not break prayer. They argued for that with:"
"2449","2614 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Nafi narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Sad, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Quba; the Ansar heard of him and came to him greeting him while he was praying, so he gestured to them with his hand, spreading his palm, while praying."
"2450","2615 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, from Hisham, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it, except that he said: I said to Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him, and Suhayb: How did you see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) return it to them while praying? He said: He gestures with his hand."
"2451","2616 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuh Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghazwan narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Sad informed me, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he said: I said to Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him: How did he return it to them?"
"2452","2617 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us. (H) 2618 - And Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Bukayr, from Nabil, the owner of the cloak, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, from Suhayb, he said: I passed by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was praying, so I greeted him, and he returned to me with a gesture. Ibn Marzuq said in his hadith: Layth said: I think he said: With his finger."
"2453","2619 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn Ajlan narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Said al-Khudri, that a man greeted the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he returned it with a gesture, and said: We used to return salutations in prayer, then we were forbidden from that. Abu Jafar said: In these reports is what indicates that gesturing does not break prayer, and they have come successively, unlike the opposing hadith, so they are more worthy than it. Gesturing in reasoning is not speech at all, because gesturing is movement of a limb, and we have seen movement of all other limbs besides the hand in prayer does not break prayer, so likewise movement of the hand. If one says: Since gesturing in prayer in your view is established to be unlike speech and does not break prayer as speech breaks it, and you argued for that with these reports you narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), why did you dislike the praying person's returning salutation by gesture, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did that in what you narrated in these reports? If that is proof for you that gesturing does not break prayer, it is proof against you that gesturing is unobjectionable in prayer. It is said to him: As for what we argued with these reports, that gesturing does not break prayer, that is established by these reports as we argued. As for what you mentioned of the permissibility of gesturing in prayer in returning salutation, there is in them no evidence for that. That is because what is in them is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gestured to them. Had the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us: That gesture I intended thereby to return salutation to whoever greeted me, it would thereby be established that likewise is the ruling of the praying person when greeted in prayer. But he said nothing of that, so it is possible that gesture was a return from him for salutation as you mentioned. And it is possible that it was from him deterring them from greeting him while praying. Since there is nothing of this in these reports, and they admit of the interpretation each of the two parties adopted, what one party interpreted is not more worthy than what the other interpreted except with a proof established against his opponent, either from the Book, or from the Sunnah, or from consensus. If one says: What is your evidence for disliking that?"
"2454","2620 - It is said to him: Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Asim narrated to us, from Abu Wail, he said: Abdullah said: We used to speak in prayer and command needs and say: Peace upon Jibril, peace upon him, and Mikail, and every righteous servant whose name is known in heaven and earth. I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Abyssinia while he was praying, so I greeted him and he did not return it to me. I was seized by old and new concern. When he finished his prayer I said: O Messenger of Allah, has something come down concerning me? He said: No, but Allah introduces of His command what He wills."
"2455","2621 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Israil informed me, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, he said: I went out for a need, while we greeted one another in prayer, then I returned and greeted, but he did not return it to me, and said: Prayer has occupation."
"2456","2622 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Masudi narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I came from Abyssinia, my last knowledge of them being that they greeted in prayer and fulfilled needs; I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and greeted him while he was praying, but he did not return it to me. When he finished his prayer he said: Allah introduces for the Prophet of His command what He wills, and He has introduced for you that you not speak in prayer. As for you, O Muslim, peace upon you and the mercy of Allah."
"2457","2623 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Mutarrif, from Abu al-Jahm, from Abu al-Radrad, from Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I used to greet the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in prayer and he would return it to me. One day I greeted him and he did not return it to me, so I felt distress in myself, and mentioned that to him, and he said: Allah introduces of His command what He wills. Abu Jafar said: In the hadith of Abu Bakrah from Abu Dawud is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned to the one who greeted him in prayer after finishing it; that is evidence that there was no returning of salutation from him in prayer, for had that been from him, it would have sufficed him from returning after finishing prayer, as says one who holds returning in prayer by gesture, that the praying person when he does that with one who greets him in his prayer need not return after finishing his prayer. In the hadith of Abu Bakrah also from Muammal: He did not return it to me, so I was seized by old and new concern. In that is evidence that he did not return at all, by gesture or otherwise, for had he returned to him by his gesture, he would not have said: He did not return it to me, but would have said: He returned to me by gesture, and what he reported befell him of old and new would not have befallen him. In the hadith of Ali ibn Shaybah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Prayer has occupation; that is evidence that the praying person is excused by that occupation from returning salutation to the one greeted, and a prohibition to others from greeting him."
"2458","There has been narrated from Abdullah of his saying after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what: 2624 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed me from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Abdullah, that he disliked greeting people while they are in prayer. And there has been narrated from Jabir ibn Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning that, the like of what was narrated from Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2459","2625 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abdullah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and he sent me for a need; I went to it, then returned to him while he was upon his mount, so I greeted him, but he did not return it to me, and I saw him bowing and prostrating; when he gave salutation, he returned it to me."
"2460","2626 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he did not say: He did not return it to me, and said: When he finished his prayer he said: Nothing prevented me from returning to you except that I was praying. This Jabir ibn Abdullah also informed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not return to him, and that when he finished his prayer he returned to him. The discussion in this is like the discussion in what we narrated before it from Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him. In the hadith of Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Nothing prevented me from returning to you except that I was praying. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed that he had not returned anything to him; that negates that he returned to him by gesture or otherwise."
"2461","2627 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him for some need; he came while he was praying upon his mount, so he greeted him and he was silent, then gestured with his hand, then he greeted him and he was silent three times; when he finished he said: Nothing prevented me from returning to you except that I was praying. This Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, informed in this hadith that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gestured to him with his hand when he greeted, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him after finishing prayer: Nothing prevented me from returning to you except that I was praying. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed that he had not returned to him in prayer. That indicates that gesture from him in prayer was not a return, but was a prohibition, and this is permissible. This has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as we mentioned. And there has been narrated from him what:"
"2462","2628 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, he said: Abu Sufyan narrated to me, he said: I heard Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, say: I do not like to greet a man while he is praying; if he greeted me, I would return it to him."
"2463","2629 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Ishkab, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. This Jabir ibn Abdullah disliked greeting the praying person, though he had greeted the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was praying, and he gestured to him. Had the gesture from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) been a return of salutation to him, he would not have disliked that, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not forbid him from it; but he disliked that only because the gesture of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in his view a prohibition to him from greeting him while praying. If one says: Jabir said in this hadith of yours: If he greeted me, I would return it. It is said to him: Did Jabir say: I would return in prayer? It is possible that by his saying I would return he meant after finishing prayer. What indicates that of his view is what:"
"2464","2630 - Ali ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa asked Ata: Did you ask Jabir about a man greeting you while you pray, and he said: Do not return to him until you finish your prayer? He said: Yes. Abu Jafar said: That indicates that the return Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, meant in the first hadith is the return after finishing prayer. That agrees with what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and indicates of its meaning what we mentioned. And there has been narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, in this similar to that."
"2465","2631 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh narrated to us, he said: Arim narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, from Qays, from Ata, that Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, was greeted by a man while praying, but did not return anything to him, and pinched him with his hand. This Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, also did not return in his prayer to the one who greeted him while in it, but pinched him with his hand out of dislike for what he did. Since Abdullah ibn Masud and Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them both, who had greeted the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was praying, disliked after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) greeting the praying person, it is thereby established that what was of the gesture of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which they knew from him was not a return but was a prohibition, because prayer is not a place of salutation, for salutation is speech, so its answer is likewise. Since prayer is not a place of speech, returning salutation, it is also not a place of salutation. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered stillness of limbs in prayer."
"2466","2632 - Fahd narrated to us thereof, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from al-A'mash, from al-Musayyib ibn Rafi', from Jabir ibn Samurah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the mosque and saw people praying while they had raised their hands. He said: What is the matter with me that I see you raising your hands as though they are the tails of restive horses; be still in prayer. So when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded stillness in prayer, and returning the salam by gesture is a departure from that, because it involves raising the hand and moving the fingers, it is established thereby that it entered into what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded of keeping the limbs still in prayer. And this view which we have explained in this chapter is the view of Abu Hanifah, and Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"2467","67 - Chapter: passing in front of one who is praying, does that cut off his prayer or not? 2633 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Yunus, and Mansur, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Nothing cuts off prayer if there is in front of him something like the back of a saddle, and he said: The woman, and the donkey, and the black dog cut off prayer. He said: I said: O Abu Dharr, what is with the black dog as distinct from the red and the white? He said: O son of my brother, you asked me about what I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about, and he said: The black dog is a devil."
"2468","2634 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, from Nafi' ibn Jubayr, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you prays toward a sutrah, let him draw close to it, lest Satan cut off his prayer."
"2469","2635 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Qatadah, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Zayd narrating from Ibn Abbas, Shu'bah raised it, he said: The menstruating woman and the dog cut off prayer."
"2470","2636 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Mu'adh ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Yahya, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: I think he ascribed it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The menstruating woman, and the dog, and the donkey, and the Jew, and the Christian, and the pig cut off prayer, and it suffices you if they are at the distance of a throw from you, they will not cut off your prayer."
"2471","2637 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Mu'adh ibn Mu'adh narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Abdullah ibn Mughaffal, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The dog, and the donkey, and the woman cut off prayer. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to these reports and said: The black dog, and the woman, and the donkey cut off prayer if they pass in front of one who is praying. And others opposed them in that and said: Nothing of this cuts off prayer. And they argued in that with what."
"2472","2638 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: I came, I and al-Fadl, while we were upon a she-donkey, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was praying with the people at Arafah, so we passed by part of the row, then we dismounted from it and left it grazing, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said nothing to us."
"2473","2639 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik and Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was praying with the people at Mina."
"2474","2640 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Amir, and Rawh, and Wahb narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Yahya ibn al-Jazzar, from Suhayb, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: I passed by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was praying, and I was upon a donkey, and with me was a boy from Banu Hashim, and he did not turn away. So in the hadith of Ubaydullah from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, is that they both passed by the row. So it is possible that they passed by those being led in prayer apart from the imam, and that was not cutting off for those being led, and there was in that no proof of the ruling of the passing of the donkey in front of the imam. But in the hadith of Suhayb, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, is that he passed by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he did not turn away. So that proved that the passing of the donkey in front of the imam also does not cut off prayer. And it has been narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, in the hadith which we mentioned from him in the first section from the hadith of Ibn Abi Dawud, that the donkey cuts off prayer among things he mentioned with it in that hadith, he said: and I think he ascribed it. So this hadith which we narrated from Ubaydullah and Suhayb, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, contradicts that, so we wanted to know which of them abrogated the matter. So we looked into that."
"2475","2641 - And Abu Bakrah had narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, from Sufyan, he said: Simak narrated to us, from Ikrimah, he said: Mentioned in the presence of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, was what cuts off prayer. They said: The dog and the donkey. So Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: To Him ascends the good word, and what cuts off this, but it is disliked. So this Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that the donkey does not cut off prayer, so that proved that what Ubaydullah and Suhayb narrated from him was later than what Ikrimah narrated from him thereof. And it has been narrated from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what indicates that the donkey also does not cut off prayer."
"2476","2642 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Muhammad ibn Umar, from Abbas ibn Ubaydullah, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited us in a desert land of ours, and we had a small dog and a donkey grazing, so he prayed Asr while they were in front of him, and they were neither driven away nor moved back."
"2477","2643 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Mu'adh ibn Fadalah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Umar ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with them, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2478","2644 - Muhammad ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Ayyub. 2645 - And Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, Abdullah ibn Salih said in his hadith, from Muhammad ibn Umar. 2646 - And Ibn Abi Maryam said in his hadith, he said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to me, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited Abbas. So this hadith agreed with the hadith of Suhayb and Ubaydullah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, which we presented in the preceding section. Then we returned to the ruling of the passing of the dog in front of one who is praying, how is it, and does it cut off prayer or not. One of those from whom it was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that it cuts off prayer was Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both; we narrated that from him at the beginning of this chapter. Then we narrated in the hadith of al-Fadl which we mentioned what contradicted it. Then we narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, afterward, from his statement after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Ikrimah from him, that the dog does not cut off prayer. So that proved the abrogation thereof in his view, and that what al-Fadl narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he distinguished the black dog from other dogs, making the black cut off prayer and making what is besides it otherwise, and that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about that and said: The black is a devil. So that proved that the meaning for which its cutting off was required is only because it is a devil. So we wanted to see whether anything opposed that."
"2479","2647 - And Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik informed him, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sa'id al-Khudri, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you is praying, let him not leave anyone to pass in front of him, and let him repel him as much as he can, and if he refuses then let him fight him, for he is only a devil."
"2480","2648 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Zafar narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abu Salih, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2481","2649 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, from Ata ibn Yasar, and from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sa'id, all of them, from Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. So in this hadith is that every passer in front of one who is praying is a devil, and he equated therein between the children of Adam and the black dog when they pass in front of one who is praying. And they narrated the like of that also from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2482","2650 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, from al-Dahhak ibn Uthman, from Sadaqah, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you is praying, let him not leave anyone to pass in front of him, and if he refuses then let him fight him, for with him is the companion, a devil. Abu Ja'far said: So the meaning of this is the meaning of the hadith of Abu Sa'id alike, and that the son of Adam in his passing in front of his praying brother is also a passing of his companion in front of him, and he is a devil. Then there is consensus that the passing of the children of Adam by one another in their prayer does not cut it off; that has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from more than one angle."
"2483","2651 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Kathir ibn Kathir, from one of his family, that he heard al-Muttalib saying: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying toward the side of the gate of Banu Sahm, and the people passing in front of him, and there was nothing between him and the qiblah."
"2484","2652 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn Jurayj narrating from Kathir ibn Kathir, from his father, from his grandfather al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wada'ah, and he mentioned the like of it, except that he said: There was no sutrah between him and the tawaf. Sufyan said: Then Kathir ibn Kathir narrated to us after I heard it from Ibn Jurayj, he said: One of my family informed me, and I did not hear it from my father."
"2485","2653 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hisham informed us, I think from the cousin of al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wada'ah, from Kathir ibn Kathir ibn al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wada'ah, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that."
"2486","2654 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja' ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Mihran, from Muslim ibn Subayh, from Masruq, that he said: Mentioned in the presence of Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, was what cuts off prayer, and they said: The dog and the donkey and the woman cut off prayer. So Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: You have equated us with dogs and donkeys, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray toward the middle of the bed while I was lying upon it, and the bed was between him and the qiblah, then a need would occur to me and I would dislike to sit in front of him lest I harm him, so I would slip away from the side of his feet, slipping."
"2487","2655 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb, and Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray while I was between him and the qiblah, and when I wanted to stand I disliked standing in front of him, so I would slip away, slipping."
"2488","2656 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Maslamah narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Abi al-Nadr. 2657 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb, and Ashhab informed us, from Malik, from Abi al-Nadr, from Abi Salamah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I would stretch my leg in the qiblah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was praying, and when he prostrated he would nudge me and I would lift them, and when he stood I would stretch them."
"2489","2658 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Za'idah informed us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abi Salamah, he said: Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray while she was lying across in front of him toward the qiblah, and when he wanted to pray witr he would nudge her with his foot and say: Move aside."
"2490","2659 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yunus al-Basri narrated to us, he said: al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from his uncle Iyas ibn Amir al-Ghafiqi, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to glorify Allah at night, and Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, was lying across between him and the qiblah."
"2491","2660 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray at night, while I was lying across between him and the qiblah, upon the bed upon which he and his family slept, and when he wanted to pray witr he would wake me and I would pray witr."
"2492","2661 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me, from Urwah, from Aishah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray while she was lying across in front of him."
"2493","2662 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, he said: Khalid narrated to us, from Abi Qilabah, from Zaynab bint Abi Salamah, from Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: A spread would be laid for me opposite the prayer place of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he would pray while I was opposite him."
"2494","2663 - Salih narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: al-Shaybani informed us, from Abdullah ibn Shaddad, he said: My maternal aunt Maymunah bint al-Harith narrated to me, she said: My bed was opposite the prayer place of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and his garment would sometimes fall upon me while he was praying. Abu Ja'far said: So these reports have come successively from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with what indicates that the children of Adam do not cut off prayer. And every passer in front of one who is praying was made a devil in the hadith of Ibn Umar and Abu Sa'id, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And Abu Dharr reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that the black dog cuts off prayer only because it is a devil. So the cause for which he made it cut off prayer was also ascribed to the children of Adam. And it is established from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that they do not cut off prayer, so that proved that every passer in front of one who is praying, of what is besides the children of Adam, is likewise, not cutting off prayer."
"2495","2664 - Of what came after him, Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, he said: It was said to Ibn Umar: That Abdullah ibn Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'ah says: The dog and the donkey cut off prayer. So Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: Nothing cuts off the prayer of the Muslim."
"2496","2665 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', and Salim, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Nothing cuts off prayer, and repel what you can."
"2497","2666 - Salih narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, the like of it. So this Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said this after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he had heard that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So this proved the abrogation of what he had heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), until what he held of this became worthier in his view than that. And as for the fighting mentioned in the hadith of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, and Abu Sa'id, from one praying, of one who wants to pass in front of him, it may be that it was permitted at a time when acts were permitted in prayer, then that was abrogated by the abrogation of acts in prayer. So this is the aspect of this chapter by way of verifying the meanings of the reports. And as for its aspect by way of reasoning, we saw them not differing regarding the dog other than black, that its passing in front of one who is praying does not cut off prayer. So we wanted to look into the ruling of the black, is it likewise or not. We saw all dogs, their flesh unlawful to eat, whether black or other than black, so the unlawfulness of their flesh was not for their colors but for their essences in themselves. And likewise all whose eating is forbidden, of every fanged beast and every taloned bird, and of domestic donkeys, the ruling of none of them differs for difference of colors, and likewise their leftovers all. So reasoning upon that is that the ruling of all dogs in their passing in front of one who is praying be alike; just as other than black does not cut off prayer, so likewise the black. And when there is established in dogs by reasoning what we mentioned, the donkey is worthier to be likewise, because there is difference over eating domestic donkey flesh, a group permitting it and others disliking it. So when what none of the Muslims eats of its flesh, its passing does not cut off prayer, what differs over eating its flesh is worthier not to cut off prayer by its passing. So this is the reasoning in this chapter, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, and Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them. And that has also been narrated from a group of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); we mentioned, after what was narrated from them in what preceded of this chapter."
"2498","2667 - And there has been narrated from them in that also what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah, and Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah, and Hisham ibn Abi Abdullah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, that Ali and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: Nothing cuts off the prayer of the Muslim, and repel from it what you can."
"2499","2668 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, from Isra'il, from Abi Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Neither the dog, nor the donkey, nor the woman, nor what is besides that of beasts cuts off the prayer of the Muslim, and repel what you can."
"2500","2669 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from his father, that he was praying, and a man passed in front of him. He said: So I prevented him and he overcame me except to pass in front of me, so I mentioned that to Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, and he was the maternal uncle of his father, and he said: It does not harm you."
"2501","2670 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to me, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Bukayr, that Busr ibn Sa'id, and Sulayman ibn Yasar narrated to him, that Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf narrated to them that he was in prayer, and Salit ibn Abi Salit passed by him, so Ibrahim pulled him and he fell and was wounded. So he went to Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, and he sent to me and said to me: What is this? I said: He passed in front of me, so I repelled him lest he cut off my prayer. He said: And does he cut off your prayer? I said: You know better. He said: He does not cut off your prayer."
"2502","2671 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, he said: al-Zibriqan ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Ka'b ibn Abdullah, he said: I heard Hudhayfah saying: Nothing cuts off prayer."
"2503","68 - Chapter: one who sleeps past prayer or forgets it, how does he make it up? 2672 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn Hafs al-Dari narrated to us, he said: Maslamah ibn Alqamah narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Rahman, freedman of Banu Hashim, from Dhu Mikhbar, nephew of al-Najashi, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and we slept and did not wake except by the heat of the sun, so we moved away from that place. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with us, then when it was the morrow, when the sun rose, that is it appeared, he commanded Bilal and he called the adhan, then he commanded him and he called the iqamah, and he prayed with us the prayer. When he completed the prayer he said: This is our prayer of yesterday."
"2504","2673 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Abi Mijlaz, from Samurah ibn Jundub, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever forgets a prayer, let him pray it when he remembers it, on the morrow at its time."
"2505","2674 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Shurayh ibn al-Nu'man al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Bishr ibn al-Harith, I heard Samurah ibn Jundub saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to this and said: Thus does one who sleeps past a prayer or forgets it do, and they argued in that with these two hadiths. And others opposed them in that and said: Rather he prays it with the next prescribed prayer, and nothing else is upon him. And they argued in that with what."
"2506","2675 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Marwan ibn Ja'far ibn Sa'd al-Sammari narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Habib ibn Sulayman ibn Samurah informed me, from Ja'far ibn Sa'd ibn Samurah, from Habib ibn Sulayman, from his father, from Samurah, that he wrote to his sons that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command them, when one of them was occupied from prayer or forgot it until its time in which it is prayed passed, to pray it with the next prescribed prayer. And others opposed them in that and said: Rather he prays it when he remembers it, even if that is before the entry of the time of the next, and nothing else is upon him. And they argued in that with the hadith of Abu Qatadah and Imran, and Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he slept past the dawn prayer until the sun rose, so he prayed it after it was high, and did not wait for the entry of the time of Zuhr, and we mentioned that with its chains in another place of this book."
"2507","2676 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, from Khalid, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Burayd ibn Abi Maryam, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions slept past the dawn prayer until the sun rose, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Bilal, and he called the adhan, then he prayed two rak'ahs, then he commanded him and he called the iqamah, and he prayed with them the prescribed prayer."
"2508","2677 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Zafir ibn Sulayman informed us, from Shu'bah, from Jami' ibn Shaddad, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Alqamah, from Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the expedition of Tabuk. When we were at a rugged land he, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: Who will guard us tonight? Bilal said: I. He said: Then you will sleep. So they slept until the sun rose, and so-and-so and so-and-so woke, and they said: Speak until he wakes. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) woke and said: Do what you used to do, and likewise does one who sleeps or forgets."
"2509","2678 - And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that also what Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever forgets a prayer, let him pray it when he remembers it. Hammam said: Then I heard Qatadah narrating it after that and he said: And establish prayer for My remembrance."
"2510","2679 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever forgets a prayer, let him pray it when he remembers it."
"2511","2680 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Thabit, from Abdullah ibn Rabah, from Abu Qatadah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. So in this hadith from the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is what indicates that nothing is upon him besides its make-up, because he mentioned one who forgets a prayer, then informed of what is upon him. And there has been narrated from him also in that in other than this hadith what adds upon this wording."
"2512","2681 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever forgets a prayer, let him pray it when he remembers it; there is no expiation for it except that. He said: Then I heard him narrating and adding therein: And establish prayer for My remembrance."
"2513","2682 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: Sa'id informed us, from Qatadah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever forgets a prayer or sleeps past it, its expiation is to pray it when he remembers it. So when he said: There is no expiation for it except that, it is impossible that there be upon him with that other than it, because had there been upon him with that other than it, that would not have been an expiation for it. And al-Hasan narrated from Imran ibn Husayn in the hadith of sleeping past prayer until the sun rose, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed it with them. He said: So we said: O Messenger of Allah, shall we not make it up at its time on the morrow? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Does Allah forbid you usury and accept it from you? And we mentioned that with its chain in another place of this book. So when they asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, and he answered them with what we mentioned, it is impossible that they knew to make it up on the morrow except by their witnessing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do that before, or his commanding them with a command; that proved the abrogation of what Dhu Mikhbar and Samurah narrated, and that this was later than it, so it is worthier than it, because it abrogates it. So this is the aspect of this chapter by way of reports. And as for by way of reasoning, we saw Allah, Mighty and Majestic, obligate prayer for its times, and obligate fasting for its appointed time in the month of Ramadan, then He placed upon one who did not fast the month of Ramadan a number of other days, making its make-up in other months, and did not place with its make-up by the number of its days a like make-up after that. So reasoning upon what we mentioned is that likewise prayer, when forgotten or missed, its make-up is required in what is after it, even if the time of its like has not entered. And there is not required with its make-up once, its make-up a second time, by analogy and reasoning upon what we mentioned of fasting which we described. And this is the view of Abu Hanifah, and Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them. And that has been narrated from a group of the early ones."
"2514","2683 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Whoever forgets a prayer and remembers it with the imam, let him pray it with him, then let him pray the one he forgot, then let him pray the other after that."
"2515","2684 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Abu Ibrahim al-Tarjuman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jumahi narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2516","2685 - Muhammad ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it and did not raise it. And his statement, let him pray it with him, that is possible in our view that he do that upon it being voluntary for him."
"2517","2686 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah informed us, from Ibrahim, regarding a man who forgot Zuhr and remembered it while he was in Asr. He said: He turns away and prays Zuhr, then prays Asr."
"2518","2687 - Salih narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mansur and Yunus informed us, from al-Hasan, that he used to say: He completes the Asr which he entered into, then prays Zuhr after that."
"2519","69 - Chapter: tanning of carrion, does it purify it or not? 2688 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir, and Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Abdullah ibn Ukaym, he said: Read to us was the letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while we were in the land of Juhaynah, and I was a young boy: Do not benefit from carrion by hide nor sinew."
"2520","2689 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja' narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Utbah, from al-Hakam, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Except that he said: The letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us."
"2521","2690 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to me, from al-Shaybani, from al-Hakam, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Except that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to us."
"2522","2691 - Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi Abu Zur'ah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Sadaqah ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Maryam, from al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah, from Abdullah ibn Ukaym, he said: Elders of Juhaynah narrated to me, they said: The letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us, or read to us was the letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Do not benefit from carrion by anything. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to that the hides of carrion are not purified even if tanned, and prayer upon them is not allowed, and they argued in that with this hadith. And others opposed them in that and said: When the hide of carrion or its sinew is tanned, it is purified, and there is no harm in selling it and benefiting by it, and praying upon it. And among the proof for them against the people of the first view in what they argued with against them, from the hadith of Ibn Abi Layla which we mentioned, is that the statement of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Do not benefit from carrion by hide nor sinew, it is possible that he meant thereby while it remains carrion untanned, for he had been asked about benefiting by the fat of carrion, and he answered the asker with the like of this."
"2523","2692 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Zam'ah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Abi al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: While I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) people came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, a ship of ours broke, and we found a fat carrion in need, and we wanted to oil our ship with it, and it is only wood, and it is upon water. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not benefit by anything of carrion."
"2524","2693 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Zam'ah narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. So Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, informed of the question which the statement of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Do not benefit by carrion, was an answer to, and that it was upon the prohibition of benefiting by its fats. As for what was tanned thereof until it exits the state of carrion and returns to other than the meaning of hides, it is purified thereby. And there have come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) successive reports, sound in transmission, clear in meaning, informing of the purity of that tanning."
"2525","Of what was narrated in that is what 2694 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Amr narrated to us, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a dead sheep belonging to Maymunah, may Allah be pleased with her, and said: Had they taken its hide and tanned it and benefited by it."
"2526","2695 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Usamah informed us, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the people of a sheep that died: Why did you not strip its skin and tan it and enjoy it."
"2527","2696 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, he said: Ata informed me long ago, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Maymunah informed me of a sheep that died, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Why did you not tan its hide and benefit by it."
"2528","2697 - Rabi' the mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth, and Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, they said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, that he said: I heard Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, saying: A sheep died, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to its people: Why did you not strip its skin and tan it and benefit by it."
"2529","2698 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ya'qub ibn Ata, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: A sheep belonging to Maymunah died, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Why did you not benefit by its hide? They said: It is carrion. So he said: The tanning of leather is its purification."
"2530","2699 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Wa'lah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whatever hide is tanned has been purified."
"2531","2700 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Malik informed us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ibn Wa'lah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When leather is tanned it has been purified."
"2532","2701 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan informed us, he said: Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Wa'lah, that he said: I said to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: We raid the land of the west, and our waterskins are only hides of carrion. So Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whatever skin is tanned has been purified."
"2533","2702 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah, that he heard Aba al-Khayr informing from Ibn Wa'lah that he asked Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and said: We raid this west, and they have waterskins in which is water, and they are idolaters. So Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: Tanning is purification. Ibn Wa'lah said to him: From your opinion, or something you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Rather I heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2534","2703 - Rabi' the mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abdah ibn Sulayman narrated to us. 2704 - And Isma'il ibn Ishaq ibn Sahl al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, they both said: From Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Amir, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, from Sawdah, may Allah be pleased with her, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: A sheep of ours died, so we tanned its skin and we kept making nabidh in it until it became worn."
"2535","2705 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The tanning of carrion is its purification. This is the wording of Muhammad. As for Fahd, he said: The tanning of carrion is its slaughtering."
"2536","2706 - Muhammad ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The tanning of carrion is its purification."
"2537","2707 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from al-A'mash, he said: Our companions narrated to us, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2538","2708 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, from Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, he said: I asked Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, about hides of carrion, and she said: Perhaps its tanning is its purification."
"2539","2709 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Kathir ibn Farqad, that Abdullah ibn Malik ibn Hudhafah narrated to him, from his mother al-Aliyah bint Subay', that Maymunah, may Allah be pleased with her, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), narrated to her that men of Quraysh passed by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), dragging a sheep of theirs like a donkey. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: Had you taken its hide. They said: It is carrion. He said: Water and qaraz purify it."
"2540","2710 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith and al-Layth informed me, from Kathir ibn Farqad, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2541","2711 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abdullah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Jawn ibn Qatadah, from Salamah ibn al-Muhabbaq, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for a waterskin from a woman in which was water, and she said: It is carrion. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did you tan it? She said: Yes. So he said: Its tanning is its slaughtering. So these reports have come successively regarding the purity of the hide of carrion by tanning, and they are clear in meaning. So they are worthier than the hadith of Abdullah ibn Ukaym which did not prove contrary to what these reports brought. If a speaker says: What was of the permissibility of tanning hides of carrion and their purity by that tanning was only before the prohibition of carrion, then the proof against him in that."
"2542","2712 - And the proof that that was after the prohibition of carrion, and that this was not included in what was forbidden thereof, it was said to him: Ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, he said: Simak ibn Harb narrated to us. 2713 - And Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Simak, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: A sheep belonging to Sawdah bint Zam'ah, may Allah be pleased with her, died, and she said: O Messenger of Allah, so-and-so died, meaning the sheep. He said: Why did you not take its skin? She said: We take the skin of a sheep that died? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah only said: Say, I do not find in what was revealed to me forbidden to one who eats it, the verse, for there is no harm that you tan it and benefit by it. She said: So she sent to it, and skinned its hide and tanned it, and took from it a waterskin, until it wore out. So in this hadith is that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when she asked him about that, recited to her the verse in which the prohibition of carrion came down. So he informed her thereby that what was forbidden to them by that verse of the sheep when it died is only what is eaten thereof when slaughtered, nothing else, and that benefiting by its hides when tanned is not included in that which was forbidden thereof. And Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah also narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, the like of that."
"2543","2714 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah narrated to me, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) found a dead sheep given to a freedwoman of Maymunah, may Allah be pleased with her, from charity, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Why did you not benefit by its skin? They said: It is carrion. He said: Only its eating was forbidden. So that proved that what was forbidden of the sheep by its death is what is intended thereof for eating, nothing else of its hides and sinews. So this is the aspect of this chapter by way of reports. And as for its aspect by way of reasoning, we saw the agreed-upon origin that juice, there is no harm in drinking it and benefiting by it, so long as the qualities of wine do not occur in it. When the qualities of wine occur in it, it is forbidden thereby, then it does not cease to be forbidden likewise until the qualities of vinegar occur in it. When the qualities of vinegar occur in it, it becomes lawful. So it becomes lawful by the occurrence of a quality, and forbidden by the occurrence of another quality, though it be one body. So reasoning upon that is that likewise be the hide of carrion, forbidden by the occurrence of the quality of death in it, and lawful by the occurrence of the quality of goods in it, of garments and other than them. When it is tanned and becomes like hides and goods, the quality of lawfulness has occurred in it. So reasoning upon what we mentioned is that it become lawful also by the occurrence of that quality in it. And another proof: We saw the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when they embraced Islam, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not command them to discard their sandals and boots and leather mats which they had taken in their time of ignorance, and that was only from carrion or from slaughter. And their slaughter then was only the slaughter of idolaters, so it is in its unlawfulness upon the people of Islam like the unlawfulness of carrion. So when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not command them to discard that and leave benefiting by it, it is established that that had exited the ruling of carrion and its impurity by tanning to the ruling of other goods and their purity. And likewise they were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when they conquered the lands of the polytheists, he did not command them to avoid their boots and sandals and leather mats and other hides, and not take anything of that; rather he did not forbid them anything of that. So that is a proof also of the purity of hides by tanning. And there has been narrated in this from Jabir ibn Abdullah what:"
"2544","2715 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Rashid narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Musa, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We used to obtain with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in our spoils from the polytheists waterskins, and we would divide them, and all of them were carrion, and we benefited by that. So that proved what we mentioned. And this Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, says this, and he narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Do not benefit from carrion by anything. So that was not in his view contradicting this. So it is established that the meaning of his hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Do not benefit from carrion by anything, is other than the meaning of his other hadith, and that the forbidden thing of carrion in that hadith is other than the permitted in this hadith. So likewise also what Abdullah ibn Ukaym narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of what was forbidden to benefit by of carrion, and it is other than what he permitted in these reports of its tanned hides, until these reports agree and none contradicts another. And this which we went to in this chapter, of the purity of hides of carrion by tanning, is the view of Abu Hanifah, and Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"2545","70 - Chapter: the thigh, is it of the private parts or not? 2716 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Khalid informed me, from Abdullah ibn Abi Sa'id al-Madini, he said: Hafsah bint Umar, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated to me, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was one day having placed his garment between his thighs, then Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, came and sought permission, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted him in his state, then Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, came likewise, then people of his companions came, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in his state, then Uthman came and sought permission of him, so he permitted him, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took his garment and wrapped himself, and they conversed, then they exited. I said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Bakr and Umar and Ali, may Allah be pleased with them, came, and people of your companions, and you were in your state, then when Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, came you wrapped your garment. He said: Shall I not be shy of one of whom the angels are shy? She said: And I heard my father and others narrating the like of this. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to that the thigh is not of the private parts, and they argued in that with this hadith. And others opposed them in that and said: The thigh is a private part, and they said: This hadith was narrated by a group of the household upon other than what those whose narration you argued with narrated."
"2546","2717 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, sought permission of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while the Messenger of Allah was wearing the cloak of the Mother of the Believers, so he permitted him and he fulfilled his need to him, then he exited. Then Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, sought permission of him while he was in that state, and he fulfilled his need to him, then he exited. Then Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, sought permission of him, so he sat upright and said to Aishah: Gather your garments upon you. When he exited, Aishah said to him: What is with you that you were not alarmed for Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, as you were alarmed for Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him? He said: Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, is a man of much modesty, and had I permitted him in that state I feared he would fall short in his need."
"2547","2718 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2548","2719 - Muhammad ibn Uzayz al-Ayli narrated to us: Salamah ibn Rawh narrated to us, he said: Uqayl narrated to us, Ibn Shihab narrated to me, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id ibn al-As informed me, that Sa'id ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with him, informed him that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, sought permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it."
"2549","2720 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, may Allah be pleased with him, from Yahya ibn Sa'id ibn al-As, that Sa'id ibn al-As informed him that Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to him that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, sought permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So this is the origin of this hadith; there is in it no mention of uncovering of the thighs at all. And there have come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) successive sound reports in which is that the thigh is of the private parts. Of what was narrated from him in that is what."
"2550","2721 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The thigh is a private part."
"2551","2722 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abi Yahya, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out and saw the thigh of a man, and said: The thigh of a man is of his private parts."
"2552","2723 - And Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Maysarah narrated to me, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Jahsh, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by Ma'mar in the courtyard of the mosque, uncovering the edge of his thigh. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Cover your thigh, O Ma'mar, the two thighs are a private part."
"2553","2724 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abu Mus'ab narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, from al-Ala, from Abi Kathir, freedman of Muhammad ibn Jahsh, from Muhammad ibn Jahsh, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2554","2725 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal, and Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abi Kathir, freedman of Muhammad ibn Abdullah, from Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Jahsh, he said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) walking in the market, and he passed by Ma'mar sitting at his door with his thigh uncovered, and he said: Cover your thigh; do you not know that it is of the private parts."
"2555","2726 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: al-Muhsin ibn Salih narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Abdullah ibn Muslim ibn Jarhad, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The thigh of a man is of his private parts, or he said: of the private parts."
"2556","2727 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Hasan, he is Ibn Salih ibn Hayy, narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Abdullah ibn Jarhad al-Aslami, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2557","2728 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik narrated to me, from Abi al-Nadr, from Zur'ah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Jarhad, from his father, and he was of the people of the Suffah, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat with me while my thigh was uncovered, and he said: Cover yourself; do you not know that the thigh is a private part."
"2558","2729 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Mis'ar, he said: Abu al-Zinad narrated to us, from his uncle Zur'ah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Jarhad, from his grandfather Jarhad, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by me while upon me was a cloak, and I had uncovered my thigh, and he said: Cover your thigh; the thigh is a private part. Abu Ja'far said: So these reports narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) inform that the thigh is a private part, and no sound report opposed them. So it is established thereby that the thigh is a private part; prayer is nullified by uncovering it, as it is nullified by uncovering what is besides it of private parts. So this is the aspect of this chapter by way of verifying the meanings of the reports. And as for its aspect by way of reasoning, we saw a man looks at a woman with whom there is no kinship between him and her, at her face and hands, and does not look at what is above that, of her head, nor at what is below it, of her belly, and back, and thighs, and legs, and we saw him regarding one of kin, there is no harm that he look at her chest, and hair, and face, and head, and leg, and not look at what is between that of her body. And likewise we saw him look at a slave woman over whom he has no ownership nor kinship between him and her, and he was prevented from looking at one of kin and at a slave woman who is not kin to him nor owned by him, at her thigh, as he was prevented from looking at her private part, so the ruling of the thigh of women became like the ruling of the private part, not like the ruling of the leg. So reasoning upon that is that it be from men also likewise, and that the ruling of the thigh of a man in looking at it be like the ruling of his private part in looking at it, not like the ruling of his leg. So when looking at his private part was forbidden, likewise looking at his thigh is forbidden, and likewise all that was forbidden for a man to look at thereof at one of kin is forbidden for men to look at, some of them at others. And all that was lawful for one of kin to look at of a woman of kin, there is no harm that men look at, some of them at others. So this is the origin of reasoning in this chapter, and that agreed with what the narrations which we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) brought. So by that we take, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, and Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"2559","71 - Chapter: what is best in voluntary prayer, is it length of standing or multitude of bowing and prostration? 2730 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas and Hudayj narrated to us, from Abi Ishaq, from al-Mukhariq, he said: We went out as pilgrims, and we passed by al-Rabadhah and found Abu Dharr standing praying, and I saw him not lengthening standing, and multiplying bowing and prostration, so I said to him about that, and he said: I did not fall short in doing well that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever bows a bow and prostrates a prostration, Allah raises him thereby a degree and removes from him thereby a sin. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to that multitude of bowing and prostration is best in voluntary prayer than length of standing and recitation, and they argued in that with this hadith. And others opposed them in that and said: Length of standing in that is best. And among the proof for them in that was what we narrated before in this book from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he was asked which prayer is best? He said: Length of standing in devotion, and in some of what we narrated in that, length of standing. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thereby preferred lengthening standing over multitude of bowing and prostration. And there is not in the hadith of Abu Dharr which we mentioned opposition to this in our view, because it is possible that the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever bows to Allah a bow and prostrates a prostration, be upon what was lengthened before it of standing. And it is also possible: Whoever bows to Allah a bow and prostrates a prostration, Allah raises him thereby a degree and removes from him thereby a sin, and if he adds with that length of standing it is best, and what Allah gives him upon that of reward is more. So this is worthiest of what the meaning of this hadith is carried upon, lest it contradict the other hadiths which we mentioned. And of those who held this other view in lengthening standing, and that it is best than multitude of bowing and prostration, is Muhammad ibn al-Hasan."
"2560","2731 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to me thereof, from Muhammad ibn Sama'ah, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, and Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"2561","2732 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from al-Ala ibn al-Harith, from Zayd ibn Artah, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, that Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, saw a youth while he was praying having lengthened his prayer, and when he finished it he said: Who knows this? A man said: I. Abdullah said: Had I known him I would have commanded him to lengthen bowing and prostration, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When the servant stands praying, his sins are brought and placed upon his head and shoulders, and whenever he bows or prostrates they fall from him. If a speaker says: So in this hadith is preference of bowing and prostration over standing. It was said to him: There is not in it what you mentioned; rather in it is what the praying one is given upon bowing and prostration of removal of sins from him, and perhaps he is given by length of standing better than that. And as for what is therein from Ibn Umar, what was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in preferring length of standing is worthier than it. The book of prayer is completed."
"2562","3 - Book of funerals. 1 - Chapter: walking in the funeral, how is it? 2733 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Mada'ini narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Uyaynah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, he said: We were in the funeral of Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah, or Uthman ibn Abi al-As, and they were walking with it a gentle walk. He said: Abu Bakrah scolded them and raised his voice upon them and said: You saw us hurrying with it with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2563","2734 - Rabi' the mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, from his father, that he said: I was sitting with Abdullah ibn Ja'far ibn Abi Talib at al-Baqi', and a funeral was brought upon us, so Ibn Ja'far turned to us marveling at their walking with it, and said: Wonder at what changed of the state of people; by Allah it was only hastening, and a man would dispute a man and say: O servant of Allah, fear Allah, for by Allah it is as though you have been hastened with."
"2564","2735 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Hasten with the funeral, for if it was righteous you bring it close to good, and if it was other than that it was an evil you place from your necks."
"2565","2736 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Zam'ah ibn Salih informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2566","2737 - Rabi' the mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2567","2738 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b informed me, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Mihran, that Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, when death came to him, said: Hasten with me, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the righteous man is placed upon his bed he says: Advance me, advance me, and when the evil man is placed upon his bed he says: O my woe, where are you going with me. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to that swiftness in walking with the funeral is best than other than that, and they argued in that with these reports. And others opposed them in that and said: Rather walk with it a gentle walk, and it is best than other than that. And they argued in that with what."
"2568","2739 - Mubashshir ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, he said: I heard Aba Burdah narrating from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a funeral while they were hastening with it, and he said: Let tranquility be upon you. So there was not in our view in this hadith a proof against the people of the first view, because it is possible that in that walking of theirs was harshness exceeding what they were commanded of swiftness in the first hadiths, so we looked into that: Do we find therein a proof indicating something of that?"
"2569","2740 - And Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh al-Basri had narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Za'idah narrated to us, from Layth, from Abi Burdah, from his father, he said: Passed by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was a funeral, they hastening with it the walk, while it was being churned like the churning of a waterskin, so he said: Upon you is moderation with your funerals. So in this hadith is that the dead was being churned for that swiftness like the churning of a waterskin. So it is possible that he commanded them with moderation because the swiftness was a swiftness feared from it that something be of the dead, so he forbade them that, and what he commanded them of swiftness in the first reports was more moderate than this swiftness. So we looked into that also: Is there narrated therein something indicating something of this meaning?"
"2570","2741 - And Abu Umayyah had narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Salih informed us, from Yahya al-Jabir, from Abi Majid, from Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We asked our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about walking with the funeral, and he said: What is below trotting; if he be a believer, what is hastened is good, and if he be a disbeliever, then away with the people of the Fire. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed in this hadith that walking with the funeral is what is below trotting. So that is in our view below what they were doing in the hadith of Abu Musa, until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded them with what he commanded them thereof, and like what he commanded them of swiftness in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him. So by this we take, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, and Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"2571","2 - Chapter: walking in the funeral, where should it be from it? 2742 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, walking in front of the funeral."
"2572","2743 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, that Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to walk in front of the funeral. He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do that, and Abu Bakr and Umar ibn al-Khattab and Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with them."
"2573","2744 - Muhammad ibn Uzayz al-Ayli narrated to us, he said: Salamah narrated to us, from Uqayl, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, that Salim informed him, then he mentioned the like of it."
"2574","2745 - Nasr ibn Marzuq, and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, he said: Uqayl ibn Khalid narrated to us. Then he mentioned the like of it with its chain."
"2575","2746 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he used to walk in front of the funeral, and that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to walk in front of the funeral, and Abu Bakr and Umar and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, and likewise is the sunnah in following the funeral."
"2576","2747 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us. 2748 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik informed him, from Ibn Shihab, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to walk in front of the funeral, and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, and the caliphs, successively, to our day this. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to that walking in front of the funeral is best than walking behind it, and they argued in that with these reports. And others opposed them in that and said: Walking behind it is best than walking in front of it. And among the proof for them against the people of the first view was that the hadith of Ibn Uyaynah which we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter, he narrated it from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, walking in front of the funeral, so it became a report from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, of what he saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr and Umar and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, doing thereof. And it is possible they were doing something, while other than it was in their view best, by way of latitude; as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ablution once once, while ablution twice twice is best than it, and ablution thrice thrice is best than all that, but he did what he did thereof by way of latitude. Then all the companions of al-Zuhri besides him opposed Ibn Uyaynah in the chain of this hadith; so Malik narrated it from al-Zuhri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to walk in front of the funeral, so he severed it. Then Uqayl and Yunus narrated it, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr and Umar and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, used to walk in front of the funeral. This is its meaning though its wording be not likewise, because the origin of his hadith is only from Salim; he said: Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to walk in front of the funeral, and likewise were the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr and Umar and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them. So all this speech in this hadith is only from Salim, not from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, so it became a severed hadith, and in the hadith of Yahya ibn Ayyub, from Uqayl: And likewise is the sunnah in following the funeral, an addition upon what is in the hadith of al-Layth and Salamah, from Uqayl; so likewise also there is no proof therein, because it is only from the speech of Salim, or from the speech of al-Zuhri. And there has been narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, of what we shall narrate in its place of this chapter, God willing. And the companions of the first view said: And there has been narrated from a group of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that they used to walk in front of the funeral. And they mentioned what."
"2577","2749 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn al-Munkadir, he heard Rabi'ah ibn Hudayr saying: I saw Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, leading the people in front of the funeral of Zaynab, may Allah be pleased with her."
"2578","2750 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me, from Ibn al-Munkadir, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2579","2751 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abd al-A'la, he said: I asked Sa'id ibn Jubayr about walking in front of the funeral. He said: Yes, I saw Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, walking in front of the funeral."
"2580","2752 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Lahi'ah informed me, from Ubaydullah ibn al-Mughirah, that Aba Rashid, freedman of Mu'ayqib ibn Abi Fatimah, informed him that he saw Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with them both, and Talhah ibn Ubaydullah, and al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam doing it."
"2581","2753 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b informed me, from Salih, freedman of al-Taw'amah, that he saw Aba Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and Abdullah ibn Umar, and Aba Usayd al-Sa'idi, and Aba Qatadah walking in front of the funeral. They said: So this proved that walking in front of the funeral is best than walking behind it. It was said to them: That did not prove anything of what you mentioned, but it permitted walking in front of the funeral, and this is of what their opponent does not deny, that walking in front of the funeral is permitted. You and he only differed in what is best thereof, and of walking behind the funeral. If you have a sound report therein that walking in front of the funeral is best than walking behind it, what you said is established thereby, otherwise his statement to now is equivalent to your statement. And if they argued in that with what."
"2582","2754 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, from Malik, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Walking behind the funeral is not of the sunnah. Ibn Shihab said: And walking behind the funeral is of error of the sunnah. It was said to them: This is the speech of Ibn Shihab, so his statement in that is like your statement, since their opponent and yours has of proof against him and you what we shall mention in this chapter, God the Exalted willing. Then we returned to what was narrated in this chapter of reports: Is therein anything permitting walking behind the funeral."
"2583","2755 - And Rabi' al-Jizi, and Ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, they said: Abu Zur'ah narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed us, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to walk in front of the funeral and behind it."
"2584","2756 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bakr al-Bursani narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Yazid, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. So in this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to walk behind the funeral, as he used to walk in front of it. If the walking of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, in front of the funeral is a proof for you that that is best than walking behind it, likewise the walking of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, behind it is a proof for your opponent against you that that is best than walking in front of it, so your disputant and you are equal in this chapter, and you have no proof therein against him."
"2585","2757 - And Abu Bakrah, and Ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, they said: Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Ubaydullah narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Jubayr, from his father, from al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The rider is behind the funeral, and the walker wherever he wills of it. So in this hadith also the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted walking behind the funeral, as he permitted walking in front of it. And there is not in anything of what we mentioned what indicates what is best thereof. And there has been narrated from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, what its meaning is close to the meaning of the hadith of al-Mughirah, and he did not mention from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2586","2758 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding a man following the funeral. He said: You are only escorting it, so walk in front of it and behind it, and on its right and its left."
"2587","2759 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Humayd, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it."
"2588","2760 - And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that also what Abd al-Ghani ibn Rifa'ah al-Lakhmi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ash'ath ibn Sulaym, he said: I heard Mu'awiyah ibn Suwayd ibn Muqarrin, he said: I heard al-Bara ibn Azib saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to follow the funeral. So in this hadith is that he commanded them to follow the funeral, and the follower in walking is the one behind it, not the one ahead in front of it. So in what we mentioned is what proved the corruption of the statement of al-Zuhri that walking behind the funeral is of error of the sunnah."
"2589","2761 - Rabi' the mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ya'la ibn Ata, from Abdullah ibn Yasar, from Amr ibn Harith, he said: I said to Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him: What do you say regarding walking in front of the funeral? So Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Walking behind it is best than walking in front of it, like the excellence of the prescribed prayer over the voluntary."
"2590","2762 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abi Farwah al-Hamdani, from Za'idah ibn Khirash, he said: Ibn Abza narrated to us, from his father, he said: I was walking in a funeral in which were Abu Bakr and Umar and Ali, may Allah be pleased with them. Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, were walking in front of it, and Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, was walking behind it, my hand in his hand. So Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The excellence of a man walking behind the funeral over one walking in front of it is like the excellence of congregational prayer over solitary prayer, and they both know thereof like what I know, but they are easy, making easy upon people. So in this hadith is the preferring by Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, of walking behind the funeral over walking in front of it. And his statement: Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, know like what I know, and they only leave that to make easy upon people, not because that is best than other than it. And this is of what is not said by opinion; rather it is said and known by what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) apprised them of and taught them thereof. So it is established by verifying what we narrated that walking behind the funeral is best than walking in front of it."
"2591","2763 - And Ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman, al-Hakam ibn Nafi' al-Bahrani narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, from Rashid ibn Sa'd, from Nafi', he said: Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, went out, and I with him, upon a funeral, and he saw women with it, so he stood then said: Send them back, for they are a trial of the living and the dead, then he went and walked behind it. I said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, how is walking in the funeral? In front of it or behind it? He said: Do you not see me walking behind it? So this Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, when asked about walking in the funeral, answered his asker that it is behind it, and he is the one we narrated from in the first chapter that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to walk in front of it. So that proved that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do that by way of lightening upon people, to teach them that walking behind the funeral, though it be best than walking in front of it, is not of what is indispensable, nor of what its leaver is burdened, but it is of what he may do and do other than it. And likewise what was narrated from Ibn Umar thereof, Salim narrated from him that he used to walk in front of the funeral. So that proved the permissibility of walking in front of it, not that that is best than walking behind it, then Nafi' narrated from him that he walked behind it. So that proved also his permitting walking behind it, not that that is best than other than it. When he asked him, he informed him of the walking which he should do in the funeral, behind it, upon that it is what is best than other than it. And we narrated in the hadith of al-Bara that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded them to follow the funeral, and the preponderant of the meaning of that is walking behind it also. So thereby became of the right of the funeral its following, and prayer upon it, if he prays upon it he is in his prayer upon it behind it. So reasoning upon that is that the follower of it in his following it be behind it. So this is the reasoning with what agreed with it of reports."
"2592","2763 - And Ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Sharik al-Amiri, he said: I heard al-Harith ibn Abi Rabi'ah ask Abdullah ibn Umar about an umm walad of his, a Christian, who died. Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said to him: Command by your command while you are far from her, then walk in front of it, for one who walks in front of the funeral is not with it. So this Ibn Umar informs that one who walks in front of the funeral is not with it. So it is impossible that that be in his view likewise, and he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) walking in front of the funeral; so it is established thereby that the origin of the hadith of Salim which we narrated at the beginning of this chapter is only as Malik narrated it from al-Zuhri, halted, or as Uqayl and Yunus narrated it, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, halted. Not as Ibn Uyaynah narrated it, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, raised."
"2593","2764 - Rabi' al-Jizi, Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, he said: Abu Yahya narrated to us, from Mujahid, he said: I was with Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, sitting, and a funeral passed, so Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, stood, then he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stand for the funeral of a Jew that passed by him. It was said: Would you like to follow it, for in following the funeral is reward? So we went walking with it, and he looked and saw people, and said: Who are those in front of the funeral? I said: They are the people of the funeral. He said: They are not with the funeral, but its sides or behind it. While he was walking he heard a wailer, so he turned me while grasping my hand, and faced it, and said evil to her: You deprived us of this funeral; go, O Mujahid, for you seek reward, and this seeks sin. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us to follow the funeral with it a wailer. If a speaker says: And how is it allowed that walking behind the funeral be best than walking in front of it? And Umar ibn al-Khattab, in the presence of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in the funeral of Zaynab, was advancing the people in front of it, so that is a proof that he did not see walking behind it at all, and were it not that, he would have permitted it to one who walked behind it. It was said to him: And how is allowed what you mentioned? And Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, said: They both, meaning Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, know that walking behind it is best than walking in front of it, then he does this meaning which you mentioned? But he did that in our view, and Allah knows best, for an incidental cause, either for women behind it, so he disliked for men to mix with them, and commanded them to advance the funeral for that incidental cause, not because it is best than walking behind it. And I heard Yunus mentioning from Ibn Wahb that he heard one saying that, and it is worthiest of what the meaning of that hadith is carried upon, so as not to contradict what Ali mentioned from Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them."
"2594","2765 - And Fahd had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: al-Aswad, when there were women with it, would take my hand and we would advance walking in front of it, and when there were no women with it, we would walk behind it. So this al-Aswad ibn Yazid, upon the length of his companionship of Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, and upon his companionship of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, his aim in walking with the funeral was to walk behind it, except that an incidental cause occur to him and he would walk in front of it for that incidental cause, not because that is best in his view than other than it. So likewise Umar, what we narrated from him of what he did in the funeral of Zaynab is upon this meaning in our view, and Allah knows best."
"2595","2766 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi al-Sari narrated to us, he said: Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us, he said: Mansur narrated to us, from Ibrahim."
"2596","2767 - And Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to dislike walking in front of the funeral. So this Ibrahim says this, and when he says they, he only means thereby the companions of Abdullah, so they used to dislike this, then they would do it for excuse, because that is best than mixing with women when they are near the funeral; as for when they are far from it, or there are no women with it, then walking behind it is best than walking in front of it, and on its right, and on its left. And this is the view of Abu Hanifah, and Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"2597","3 - Chapter: the funeral passing by people, do they stand for it or not? 2768 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Mu'alla ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, from Musa ibn Imran ibn Manah, that Aban ibn Uthman, a funeral passed by him and he stood for it."
"2598","2769 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Duhaym narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Maslamah ibn Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Except that he said: I saw Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, doing that, and he informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) does that."
"2599","2770 - Rabi' the mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from Amir ibn Rabi'ah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you see the funeral, stand for it until it is placed or passes you."
"2600","2780 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2601","2781 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Azhar ibn Sa'd al-Samman narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from Amir ibn Rabi'ah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: When you see a funeral, stand."
"2602","2782 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, he said: Salim informed me, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from Amir ibn Rabi'ah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When you see the funeral, stand for it until it is placed or passes you."
"2603","2783 - Rabi' the mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from Amir ibn Rabi'ah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2604","2784 - Yazid ibn Sinan, and Mubashshir ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, they said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us, he said: Rabi'ah ibn Sayf al-Ma'afiri narrated to me, from Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Abdullah ibn Amr, that he said: A man asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, the funeral of a disbeliever passes by us, do we stand for it? He said: Yes, for you do not stand for it; rather you stand in veneration of the One who seizes souls."
"2605","2785 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us. 2786 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Ibn Abi Layla, he said: Sahl ibn Hunayf, and Qays ibn Sa'd ibn Ubadah sat at al-Qadisiyyah, and a funeral was passed by them, and they stood. It was said to them: He is of the people of the land, that is a Magian. They said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was passed by with a funeral, and he stood, and it was said to him: He is a Jew. He said: Is he not dead? Is he not a soul?"
"2606","2787 - Rabi' the mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abi al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and those with him stood for a funeral until it disappeared."
"2607","2788 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Aban narrated to us. 2789 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Aban narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ubaydullah ibn Miqsam, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a funeral passed by him, so we stood to carry it, and it was the funeral of a Jew or Jewess, and we said: O Prophet of Allah, it is the funeral of a Jew or Jewess. He said: Death is a terror, so when you see the funeral, stand."
"2608","2790 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2609","2791 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ibn Abi al-Safar, from al-Sha'bi, from Abi Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Passed by Marwan was a funeral and he did not stand. Abu Sa'id said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was passed by with a funeral and he stood, so Marwan stood."
"2610","2792 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abi Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When you see the funeral, stand; whoever follows it, let him not sit until it is placed."
"2611","2793 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim narrated to us, he said: Aban narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2612","2794 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya. (H). 2795 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Yahya, from Abu Salamah, he said: Abu Sa'id narrated to us, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2613","2796 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Sa'id ibn Marjanah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you prays over a funeral and does not walk with it, let him stand until it disappears from him, and if he walks with it, let him not sit until it is placed down. Abu Ja'far said: A group went to these reports, followed them, made them a foundation and adhered to them, and commanded whoever a funeral passes by to stand for it until it vanishes from him, and whoever walks with it not to sit until it is placed down. Others differed with them and said: Whoever a funeral passes by is not obliged to stand for it, and whoever follows it may sit even if it has not been placed down. And they said: As for the standing of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for the funeral of the Jew in the hadith narrated by Qays ibn Sa'd and Sahl ibn Hunayf, that was not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) because standing is part of the ruling of funerals, but for another reason. And they mentioned concerning that what follows."
"2614","2797 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Umar narrating from al-Hasan, and Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them), or from one of them, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a funeral of a Jew passed by him, so he stood for it and said: Its smell harmed me. So this hadith indicates that his standing was because its smell harmed him, to move away from it, not for anything else. And as for what was narrated of his standing for a funeral, it was only to pray over it."
"2615","2798 - Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan that al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib and al-Hasan ibn Ali (may Allah be pleased with them), a funeral passed by them, so al-Abbas stood and al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with him) did not stand. Al-Abbas said to al-Hasan: Did you not know that a funeral passed by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he stood? He said: Yes. And al-Hasan said to al-Abbas: Did you not know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray over it? He said: Yes. So this hadith indicates that the standing of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was only to pray over it, not because it is its sunnah that one stands for it. And as for what was mentioned of the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to stand for the funeral, and not to sit when following it until it is placed down, that did occur, then it was abrogated."
"2616","2799 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Waqid ibn Amr, from Nafi' ibn Jubayr, from Mas'ud ibn al-Hakam, from Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood with the funeral until it was placed down, and the people stood with him, then he sat after that and commanded them to sit."
"2617","2800 - Yunus and Bahr narrated to us, they said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi informed me that Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah narrated to him, from Waqid ibn Amr ibn Sa'd ibn Mu'adh, from Mas'ud ibn al-Hakam al-Zuraqi, from Ali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2618","2801 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad informed me, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Waqid ibn Amr, from Nafi' ibn Jubayr, from Mas'ud ibn al-Hakam, that he said: I heard Ali saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to stand for the funeral, then he sat after that and commanded us to sit."
"2619","2802 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ja'far informed us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Isma'il ibn Mas'ud ibn al-Hakam al-Zuraqi, from his father, he said: I witnessed a funeral in Iraq, and I saw men standing waiting for it to be placed down, and I saw Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) gesturing to them to sit, for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to sit after standing."
"2620","2803 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Mas'ud ibn al-Hakam, from Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: We saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stand, so we stood, and we saw him sit, so we sat. So it is established by what we mentioned that standing for the funeral did occur, then it was abrogated. A group said: That was abrogated only to differ from the People of the Book, and they argued concerning that with what follows."
"2621","2804 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Safwan ibn Isa narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Rafi' narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Sulayman, from his father, from Junadah ibn Abi Umayyah, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, mentioning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) followed a funeral, he would not sit until it was placed in the grave. He said: A Jewish scholar met the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Muhammad, thus we do. He said: So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat and said: Differ from them. And this hadith, in our view, does not indicate what they held, because there is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what follows."
"2622","2805 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to let his hair hang down, and the polytheists used to part their heads, and the People of the Book used to let their heads hang down, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to love agreeing with the People of the Book in what he had not been commanded anything, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) parted his head."
"2623","2806 - Muhammad ibn Uzayr al-Ayli narrated to us, he said: Salamah narrated to us, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Ubaydullah informed me, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it. So Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) informed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to follow the People of the Book until he was commanded with the opposite of that. So it is impossible that what he was commanded of sitting in the hadith of Ubadah was to differ from the People of the Book before he was commanded to differ from them in that, because his ruling (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was to be upon the law of the prophet before him until a law would come to him abrogating what preceded it. Allah Almighty said: Those are they whom Allah guided, so by their guidance follow. But he left that, in our view, and Allah knows best, when Allah brought him a new law in that, which is sitting, abrogating what was before it, which is standing. And this view is narrated from Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"2624","2807 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Abdul-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Layth ibn Abi Sulaym narrated to us, from Mujahid, from Ibn Sakhbarah, he said: We were sitting with Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) waiting for a funeral, then another funeral passed, so we stood. He said: What is this standing? I said: What you Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bring us. Abu Musa said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you see the funeral of a Muslim or a Jew or a Christian, stand, for you do not stand for it, you stand only for those with it of the angels. Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did that only once; he was imitating the People of the Book in a matter, and when he was forbidden from it, he left it. So Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) informed in this hadith that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood only once at the beginning of his affair, imitating the People of the Book and following those before him of the prophets, until Allah Almighty brought him the opposite of that, which is sitting. So what we directed the aspect of the hadith of Ubadah to is established by that."
"2625","2808 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Abi Zar'ah, from Zayd ibn Wahb, he said: We mentioned standing for the funeral while Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) was with us. Abu Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) said: We used to stand. Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: That was while you were Jews. The meaning of this is that they used to stand upon their law, then that was abrogated by the law of Islam in it. And it is established by what we described in this chapter also the abrogation of what we narrated at its beginning of the reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about standing for the funeral, by the reports we narrated after that."
"2626","2809 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad narrated to me, from Unays ibn Abi Yahya, he said: I heard my father saying: Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) and the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would sit before the funeral was placed down. So this is Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) who used to do this, though there was narrated from Amir ibn Rabi'ah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the opposite of that. His abandoning that for what he used to do indicates established abrogation, which Amir ibn Rabi'ah introduced later."
"2627","2810 - Yunus also narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Abdur-Rahman ibn al-Qasim narrated to him that al-Qasim would sit before the funeral was placed down and would not stand for it, and he reports from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that she said: The people of ignorance used to stand for it when they saw it, saying: Among your family, what are you among your family. So this is A'ishah disapproving of standing for it entirely, and informing that that was among the deeds of the people of ignorance. And Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad (may Allah Almighty have mercy on them) held concerning all we mentioned in this chapter what we have explained of its abrogation, by what opposed it, and we adopt it."
"2628","4 - Chapter of a man praying over the dead: where should he stand from him? 2811 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abdul-Warith ibn Sa'id informed us, from Husayn ibn Dhakwan, he said: Abdullah ibn Buraydah narrated to me, from Samurah ibn Jundub, he said: I prayed behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over Umm Ka'b, who died in childbirth, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood for prayer over her at her middle."
"2629","2812 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Husayn al-Mu'allim narrated to us, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: A group went to this and said: This is the station the one praying over the funeral should take, of the woman and of the man. Others differed with them and said: As for the woman, thus he stands for prayer over her, and as for the man, at his head. And they argued concerning that with what follows."
"2630","2813 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghalib narrated to us, he said: I saw Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) pray over the funeral of a man, and he stood at his head, and a funeral of a woman was brought, and he stood at her middle. Al-Ala ibn Ziyad said to him: O Abu Hamzah, did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do thus? He said: Yes. Al-Ala ibn Ziyad turned to us and said: Memorize."
"2631","2814 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammam informed us, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it. And he added: Al-Ala ibn Ziyad said to him: O Abu Hamzah, did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stand of the woman where you stood, and of the man where you stood? He said: Yes."
"2632","2815 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Abdul-Warith ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Abu Ghalib, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to stand at the head of the man and at the hips of the woman. Abu Ja'far said: So Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) clarified in this hadith that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to stand of the man at his head and of the woman at her middle, as in the hadith of Samurah. So it agreed with the hadith of Samurah on the ruling of standing of the woman in prayer over her, how it is, and added to it the ruling of the man in standing from him for prayer over him, so it is more worthy than the hadith of Samurah."
"2633","2816 - Abu Yusuf (may Allah have mercy on him) held this view, in what Ibn Abi Imran narrated to me, he said: Muhammad ibn Shuja' narrated to me, from al-Hasan ibn Abi Malik, from Abu Yusuf (may Allah have mercy on him). As for his well-known view in that, it is like the view of Abu Hanifah and Muhammad (may Allah have mercy on them)."
"2634","2817 - Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated to me, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, from Abu Yusuf (may Allah have mercy on him), from Abu Hanifah (may Allah have mercy on him), he said: He stands of the man and the woman at the chest. Muhammad did not mention between Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf (may Allah have mercy on them) any disagreement in that. And there is narrated in that also from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i."
"2635","2818 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: The man who prays over the funeral stands at its chest. Abu Ja'far said: The first view is more beloved to us, because of what strengthened it of the reports we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2636","5 - Chapter: prayer over the funeral, should it be in the mosques or not? 2819 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Isma'il narrated to us, from al-Dahhak ibn Uthman, from Abu al-Nadr, freedman of Umar ibn Ubaydullah, from Abu Salamah ibn Abdur-Rahman, that when Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas died, A'ishah said: Bring him into the mosque that I may pray over him. The people disapproved of that from her. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over Suhayl ibn al-Bayda in the mosque."
"2637","2820 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Abu al-Nadr, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) with that."
"2638","2821 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Umar narrated to us, he said: Abdul-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Abdul-Wahid ibn Hamzah, from Abbad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr that A'ishah commanded Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas to be passed with in the mosque, then he mentioned the like of his hadith from Ya'qub. Abu Ja'far said: A group went to this hadith and said: There is no harm in prayer over the funeral in the mosques. And they argued in that also with what follows."
"2639","2822 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Umar narrated to us, he said: Abdul-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that Umar was prayed over in the mosque. Others differed with them in that, and disliked prayer over the funeral in the mosques. And they argued in that with what follows."
"2640","2823 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from Salih, freedman of al-Taw'amah. (H) 2824 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Ma'n ibn Isa narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Salih ibn Abi Salih, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever prays over a funeral in a mosque, there is nothing for him. When the narrations from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) differed in this chapter, with what we narrated in the first section being permission of prayer over funerals in the mosques, and what we narrated in the second section disapproval of that, we needed to uncover that to know the later of it, to make it abrogating what preceded. Since the hadith of A'ishah contains evidence that they had left prayer over funerals in the mosque after it used to be done in it, until that vanished from their practice and knowledge of it left most of them, that was not in her view due to a new disapproval, but in her view because they may pray in the mosque over their funerals and may pray over them elsewhere. Their praying elsewhere is not evidence of disapproval of praying in it, just as their praying in it was not evidence of disapproval of praying elsewhere. So she said after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), on the day Sa'd died, what she said for that. The people disapproved that from her, and they were the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and those who followed them. Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) had learned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the abrogation of praying over them in the mosque, by the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he heard from him in that, and that the abandonment by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of prayer over funerals in the mosque, after he used to do it in it, was an abandonment of abrogation. So that is more worthy than the hadith of A'ishah, because the hadith of A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) is information of the deed of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a state of permission not preceded by prohibition. In the hadith of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) is information of the prohibition of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preceded by permission. So the hadith of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) became more worthy than the hadith of A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), because it abrogates it. In the disapproval of those who disapproved that from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), while they were then the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), is evidence they had known in that the opposite of what she knew; otherwise they would not have disapproved it from her. And what we mentioned of prohibition of prayer over the funeral in the mosque and its disapproval is the view of Abu Hanifah, Muhammad and Abu Yusuf (may Allah be pleased with them). Except that the companions of dictation narrated from Abu Yusuf (may Allah be pleased with him) in that that he said: If there is a mosque set apart for prayer over the funeral, there is no harm in praying over funerals in it."
"2641","6 - Chapter of takbir over funerals, how many is it? 2825 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us. (H) 2826 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Ibn Abi Layla, he said: Zayd ibn Arqam used to pray over our funerals and say takbir four times. One day he said takbir five times, so he was asked about that. Abu Bakrah said in his hadith: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said takbir five times. Ibn Marzuq said in his hadith: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say it, or said it."
"2642","2827 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Isra'il ibn Yunus informed us, he said: Abdul-A'la narrated to us that he prayed behind Zayd ibn Arqam over a funeral and he said takbir five times. Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Layla asked him, taking his hand, and said: Did you forget? He said: No, but I prayed behind Abu al-Qasim, my beloved (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said takbir five times, so I will never leave it."
"2643","2828 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abdul-Aziz ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abdullah al-Taymi, he said: I prayed with Isa, freedman of Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman, over a funeral, and he said takbir over it five times, then turned to us and said: I neither erred nor forgot, but I said takbir as my master and benefactor said takbir, meaning Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman, who prayed over a funeral and said takbir over it five times, then turned to us and said: I neither erred nor forgot, but I said takbir as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said takbir. Abu Ja'far said: A group went to the view that takbir over funerals is five, and argued in that with these reports. Others differed with them and said: Rather it is four, it should not be increased nor decreased. And they argued in that with what follows."
"2644","2829 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hudbah narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, that he narrated to him from his father that he witnessed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray over a dead person, and he said takbir over him four times."
"2645","2830 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Salim ibn Hayyan, from Sa'id ibn Mina, from Jabir ibn Abdullah that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said takbir over the Negus four times."
"2646","2831 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us. (H). 2832 - And Salih ibn Abdur-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us. (H) 2833 - And Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Hakim al-Ansari, from Kharijah ibn Zayd, from Zayd ibn Thabit that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over the grave of Qilabah, and said takbir four times."
"2647","2834 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shayban narrated to us, he said: Suwayd Abu Hatim narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to me, from Ata, from Jabir ibn Abdullah that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said takbir four times."
"2648","2835 - Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Abi Zar'ah, from Abu Salman the caller to prayer, he said: Abu Surayhah died, and Zayd ibn Arqam prayed over him and said takbir over him four times. We said: What is this? He said: Thus I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do."
"2649","2836 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ayyash al-Raqqam narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Yahya al-Himyari narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Husayn narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to visit the poor of Medina, and he was informed of a woman who died, and they buried her at night. When morning came they informed him, so he walked to her grave, prayed over her and said takbir four times."
"2650","2837 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Nu'man narrating from al-Zuhri, from Abu Umamah, from one of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2651","2838 - Isma'il ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Hajari, he said: Ibn Abi Awfa led us in prayer over a daughter of his and said takbir over her four times, then he stood and we waited after the fourth for his salutation, until we thought he would say a fifth takbir, then he gave salutation, then said: I see you thought I would say a fifth takbir, and I would not do that. Thus I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do."
"2652","2839 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from al-Hajari, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"2653","2840 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hajari, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"2654","2841 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) announced to the people the Negus on the day he died, then went out to the prayer place, lined them, and said over him four takbirs."
"2655","2842 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib informed me, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2656","2843 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja' narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from one of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2657","2844 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Abdul-Warith ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Abu Ghalib, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say four takbirs over the dead. And they said concerning the hadith of Zayd ibn Arqam with which we began mention in this chapter, that he used to say takbir over funerals four times before the occasion he said it five times. It is not permissible that he would do that, having seen the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do the opposite, except for a reason he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do. It is what Abu Salman the caller to prayer narrated from him in his prayer over Abu Surayhah, in his saying takbir over him four times. His saying takbir over that funeral five times may be because the ruling of that dead person was to say takbir over him five times, because he was of the people of Badr, for they were given preference in takbir in prayer over them above what is said over others."
"2658","2845 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us. (H). And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Umar said: All of that had occurred, five and four, so Umar commanded the people with four, meaning in prayer over the funeral."
"2659","2846 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, and from Zayd, meaning Ibn Abi Unaysah, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away while the people differed in takbir over funerals; you could hardly hear a man saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say takbir seven times, and another saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say takbir five times, and another saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say takbir four times, but you would hear it. They differed in that and remained so until Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) passed away. When Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) took authority and saw the people's disagreement in that, it weighed heavily on him, so he sent to men of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: You, company of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when you differ, the people after you differ, and when you agree upon a matter, the people agree upon it, so consider a matter you agree upon, as if he awakened them. They said: What a good view, O Commander of the Faithful, so advise us. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Rather advise me, for I am only a mortal like you. They reconsidered the matter among them and agreed to make takbir over funerals like the takbir in Adha and Fitr, four takbirs, and their affair was agreed upon that. So this is Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) who returned the matter in that to four takbirs by the counsel of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to him, while they had witnessed of the deed of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what Hudhayfah and Zayd ibn Arqam narrated. What they did of that was in their view more worthy than what they had known. That is an abrogation of what they had known, because they are trusted in what they did, as they were trusted in what they narrated. This is as they agreed upon after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in fixing the hadd of wine, and leaving the sale of slave-mothers. Their agreement upon what they agreed upon of that was proof, even though they had done in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) its opposite. Likewise what they agreed upon of the number of takbir after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in prayer over the funeral is proof, even though they had known from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) its opposite. What they did of that and agreed upon after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) abrogates what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had done. If one says: How can that be abrogating, when Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) said takbir after that more than four? And they mentioned in that what follows."
"2660","2847 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, he said: Amir narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Ma'qil that Ali prayed over Sahl ibn Hunayf and said takbir over him six times."
"2661","2848 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, he said: Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Abdullah narrated to us that Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) prayed over Abu Qatadah and said takbir over him seven times. It was said to him: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) did that only because the people of Badr, such was their ruling in prayer over them, increased in takbir above what is said over others of all people."
"2662","2849 - And the evidence for that is that Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Sayrafi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Za'idah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Ma'qil, he said: I prayed with Ali over a funeral, and he said takbir over it five times, then turned and said: He is of the people of Badr. Then I prayed with Ali over funerals, and each time he would say takbir over them four times."
"2663","2850 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Jabir, from Amir, from Ibn Ma'qil, he said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) prayed over Sahl ibn Hunayf and said takbir over him six times, then turned to us and said: He is of the people of Badr."
"2664","2851 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath informed us, from Abdul-Malik ibn Sal' al-Hamdani, from Abd Khayr, he said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say takbir over the people of Badr six times, over the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) five times, and over all people four times. Thus was the ruling of prayer over the people of Badr."
"2665","2852 - Al-Qasim ibn Ja'far narrated to me, he said: Zayd ibn Akhzam al-Ta'i narrated to us, he said: Ya'la ibn Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Yasir narrated to us, he said: I prayed behind al-Aswad ibn Yazid, Hammam ibn al-Harith and Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, and they would say takbir over funerals four times. Hammam said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) gathered the people upon four, except over the people of Badr, for they would say takbir over them five, seven and nine times. So what we mentioned indicates that what they agreed upon of the number of four takbirs in the time of Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was only over other than the people of Badr, leaving the ruling of the people of Badr at above four. What was narrated from Zayd ibn Arqam of what we mentioned is only because he held this view, in our opinion, and Allah knows best."
"2666","2853 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, and he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, from Alqamah ibn Qays, he said: People of Syria came, and a dead person of theirs died, and they said takbir over him five times. I wanted not to greet them, so I informed Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), and he said: There is nothing fixed in it. This may bear what we mentioned of the difference in the ruling of prayer over the Badrites and over others. Abdullah meant by his saying there is nothing fixed in it, that there is nothing by which takbir in prayer over all people is fixed, not to be exceeded to other than it. And this hadith is narrated with other than this wording."
"2667","2854 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Abdul-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: al-Shaybani narrated to us, he said: Amir narrated to us, from Alqamah that he mentioned that to Abdullah, so Abdullah said: When the imam advances, say takbir with whatever he says takbir, for there is no fixed time nor number. And this, in our view, means also what we mentioned, because the imam might then pray over the Badrites and over others. If he prays over the Badrites and says takbir over them as is said over the Badrites, which is above four, say takbir as he said. If he prays over other than the Badrites and says four as is said over them, say as he said. There is no fixed time nor number in takbir in prayer over all people, of the Badrites and others, not to be exceeded to what is greater."
"2668","2855 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, he said: Takbir over the funeral, no fixed time nor number; if you wish five, if you wish six. The meaning of this is other than the meaning of what Amir narrated from Alqamah. What Amir narrated from Alqamah of this is more established, because Amir met Alqamah and took from him, while Abu Ishaq did not meet him nor take from him, and because there is narrated from Abdullah in takbir that it is four, from other than this channel."
"2669","2856 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ali ibn al-Aqmar, from Abu Atiyyah, he said: I heard Abdullah saying: Takbir over funerals is four, like the takbir in the two Eids."
"2670","2857 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us. (H). 2858 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ali ibn al-Aqmar, from Abu Atiyyah, from Abdullah, he said: Takbir in the two Eids is four, like prayer over the dead."
"2671","2859 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ali ibn al-Aqmar, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it. So this is Abdullah, when asked about takbir over the funeral, informing that it is four, and commanding them in the hadith of Alqamah to say takbir as their imams say. Had speech stopped at that, the aspect of his hadith in our view would be that the basis of takbir with him is four, and that whoever prays behind one who says more than four says as his imam said, because he did what some scholars said. Abu Yusuf used to hold this view. But speech did not stop at that, and he said: No fixed time nor number. So that indicates that its meaning in that is there is in my view no fixed time for takbir in prayer over funerals, nor number, upon the meaning we mentioned of the people of Badr and others. That is, no fixed time nor number in takbir in prayer over all people, but its total has no fixed time nor number: if they are people of Badr, thus is the ruling of prayer over them, and prayer over others as narrated from him by Abu Atiyyah, so that none of that contradicts. Then there is narrated from most of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in their prayer over their funerals that they said takbir in them four times. Of what was narrated from them in that is what follows."
"2672","2860 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amir ibn Shaqiq, from Abu Wa'il, that Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) gathered the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked them about takbir over the funeral. Each informed of what he saw and heard, so Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) gathered them upon four takbirs, like the longest of prayers, the noon prayer."
"2673","2861 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Isma'il narrated to us, from Amir, he said: Abdur-Rahman ibn Abza informed me, he said: We prayed with Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) over Zaynab in Medina, and he said takbir over her four times."
"2674","2862 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, he said: Umayr ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: I prayed with Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) over Yazid ibn al-Mukaffaf, and he said takbir over him four times."
"2675","2863 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Mis'ar narrated to us, from Umayr, the like of it."
"2676","2864 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, he said: I heard Umayr ibn Sa'id, and he mentioned the like of it."
"2677","2865 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Qabisah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Umayr ibn Sa'id, from Ali, the like of it."
"2678","2866 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from Musa ibn Talhah, he said: I witnessed Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) pray over funerals of men and women. He placed the men toward him and the women toward the qiblah, then said takbir over them four times."
"2679","2867 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Talhah, he said: I prayed behind Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) over a funeral, and he said takbir over it four times."
"2680","2868 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf informed me, and he was of the elders of the Ansar and their scholars, and sons of those who witnessed Badr with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that a man of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed him that the sunnah in prayer over the funeral is that the imam says takbir, then recites the Opening of the Book silently to himself, then seals the prayer in the three takbirs."
"2681","2869 - Al-Zuhri said: I mentioned what Abu Umamah informed me of that to Muhammad ibn Suwayd al-Fihri, and he said: I heard al-Dahhak ibn Qays narrating, from Habib ibn Maslamah, in prayer over the funeral, the like of what Abu Umamah narrated to you."
"2682","2870 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq that al-Hasan ibn Ali said takbir over Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) four times. This is contrary to what Umar and Ali (may Allah be pleased with them) held of the people of Badr, that takbir in prayer over them be more than four."
"2683","2871 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Mis'ar narrated to us, from Thabit ibn Ubayd, he said: I prayed behind Zayd ibn Thabit over a funeral, and he said takbir over it four times, and I prayed behind Abu Hurayrah over a funeral, and he said takbir over it four times."
"2684","2872 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, he said: Shurahbil ibn Sa'd narrated to me, he said: Abdullah ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) led us in prayer over a funeral and said four takbirs."
"2685","2873 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Muhajir Abu al-Hasan, he said: I prayed behind al-Bara ibn Azib over a funeral. He said: Have you gathered? We said: Yes, so he said takbir four times."
"2686","2874 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Abdullah ibn Mawhab, he said: I prayed behind Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) over funerals of men and women, and he treated them equally and said takbir four times. So these Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned in these reports used to say takbir in their prayer over their funerals four takbirs, then no others disapproved that from them. That indicates that that is the ruling of takbir in prayer over funerals, and that what exceeded four takbirs was only for a special reason, specified for some of the dead, of those we mentioned of the people of Badr, above all people. So it is established by what we mentioned that takbir over the funeral is four, over all people after the people of Badr until the Day of Resurrection. It was the view of Abu Hanifah, Sufyan, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan (may Allah have mercy on them) in takbir over funerals also what we mentioned. That is narrated also from Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah."
"2687","2875 - Salih narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abu Hamzah Imran ibn Abi Ata informed us, he said: I witnessed the death of Ibn Abbas in Ta'if, and Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah took charge of him and prayed over him, saying takbir four times."
"2688","2876 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Imran ibn Abi Ata, he said: I prayed behind Ibn al-Hanafiyyah over Ibn Abbas, and he said takbir four times."
"2689","7 - Chapter of prayer over the martyrs. 2877 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd informed me, from Ibn Shihab, narrating to him, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, that Jabir ibn Abdullah informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the slain of Uhud to be buried in their blood, and he did not pray over them, nor were they washed. Abu Ja'far said: A group went to this hadith and said: One does not pray over whoever is killed of the martyrs in battle, nor over whoever of them is wounded and dies before being carried from his place, just as he is not washed. Of those who said that are the people of Medina. Others differed with them and said: Rather prayer is said over the martyr. Of the proof for them against their opponents was that what is in the hadith of Jabir is only that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not pray over them. It may be that he left that because their sunnah is not to pray over them, as it was of their sunnah not to wash them. Or it may be that he did not pray over them, while others prayed over them, due to what he then had of the pain of wounds, the breaking of his front tooth, and what befell him that day from the polytheists."
"2690","2878 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Hazim and Sa'id ibn Abdur-Rahman al-Jumahi informed me, from Abu Hazim. Sa'id said in his hadith: I heard Sahl ibn Sa'd. Ibn Abi Hazim said from Sahl: He was asked about the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Uhud, with what was it treated? Sahl said: The helmet was broken on his head, his front tooth was broken, and his face was wounded. Fatimah (may Allah be pleased with her) would wash him, and Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) would pour water with the shield. When Fatimah (may Allah be pleased with her) saw that water only increased the blood, she took a piece of mat, burned it and stuck it on his wound, so the blood stopped. The wording of Ibn Abi Hazim and Sa'id differs in this hadith, and the meaning is one."
"2691","2879 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Nafi' narrated to us, from Hisham, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was struck on the day of Uhud in his face and wounded, and Fatimah (may Allah be pleased with her), his daughter, burned a piece of mat, made it ash and stuck it on his face. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The anger of Allah Almighty intensified against a people who bloodied the face of the Messenger of Allah."
"2692","2880 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan informed us, he said: Abu Hazim narrated to me, from Sahl ibn Sa'd, he said: The helmet was crushed on the head of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Uhud, his front tooth was broken, and his face was wounded."
"2693","2881 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Awn narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah informed us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The anger of Allah Almighty intensified against a people who bloodied the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). They had bloodied his face that day, crushed the helmet on him, and broken his front tooth."
"2694","2882 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his front tooth was broken on the day of Uhud and his face was cut. He began wiping blood on his face, saying: How can a people prosper who cut the face of their prophet, broke his front tooth, while he calls them to Allah Almighty. So Allah Almighty revealed: You have nothing of the affair. So it may be that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stayed back from prayer over them due to the pain of what befell him, while others prayed over them."
"2695","2883 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi narrated to us that Ibn Shihab narrated to him that Anas ibn Malik narrated to him that the martyrs of Uhud were not washed, were buried in their blood, and prayer was not said over them. So in this hadith is what negates prayer over them from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and from others. So we looked into this hadith, how is it, and was anything added over Ibn Wahb in it?"
"2696","2884 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, he said: Usamah informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed on the day of Uhud by Hamzah, who had been mutilated and disfigured, and said: Were it not that Safiyyah would grieve, I would have left him until Allah gathers him from the bellies of birds and beasts. So he shrouded him in a striped cloak; when his head was covered his feet showed, and when his feet were covered his head showed, so he covered his head. He did not pray over any of the martyrs other than him, and said: I am a witness over you on the Day of Resurrection. So in this hadith, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not pray that day over any of the martyrs except Hamzah, for he prayed over him, and he is the best of the martyrs of Uhud. Had it been of the sunnah of martyrs not to pray over them, he would not have prayed over Hamzah, just as he did not wash him, since it is of the sunnah of martyrs not to be washed. What is in this hadith became that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over Hamzah and did not pray over others. This may be that he did not pray over others due to the severity of what he had of what we mentioned, while others of the people prayed over them. And there came in other than this hadith that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed that day over Hamzah and over all the martyrs."
"2697","2885 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have ten placed before him on the day of Uhud, and he would pray over them and over Hamzah, then the ten would be lifted while Hamzah remained placed, then ten would be placed, and he would pray over them and over Hamzah with them."
"2698","2886 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded on the day of Uhud concerning the slain. Nine and Hamzah would be placed, and he would say takbir over them seven takbirs, then they would be lifted and Hamzah left, then nine would be brought, and he would say takbir over them seven times, until he finished from them."
"2699","2887 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, he said: Yahya ibn Abbad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me, from his father, meaning from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with them), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded on the day of Uhud concerning Hamzah, so he was covered with his cloak, then he prayed over him and said nine takbirs, then the slain were brought lined up, and he prayed over them and over him with them. So this is Ibn Abbas and Ibn al-Zubayr, who differed with Anas ibn Malik in what we narrated from him before this. And the like of this is narrated also from Abu Malik al-Ghifari."
"2700","2888 - Bakr ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Iyas narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Husayn ibn Abdur-Rahman, he said: I heard Abu Malik al-Ghifari, he said: The slain of Uhud would be brought nine, the tenth of them Hamzah, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray over them, then they would be carried, then nine would be brought, and he would pray over them while Hamzah in his place, until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over them. And it is narrated also from Uqbah ibn Amir that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over the slain of Uhud after their killing by eight years."
"2701","2889 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr and Ibn Lahi'ah informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, that Abu al-Khayr informed him that he heard Uqbah ibn Amir saying: The last of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us is that he prayed over the martyrs of Uhud, then ascended the pulpit, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: I am your forerunner and I am a witness over you."
"2702","2890 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Khayr, from Uqbah ibn Amir that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out one day and prayed over the people of Uhud his prayer over the dead. So in the hadith of Uqbah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over the slain of Uhud after their killing by eight years. His prayer over them at that time cannot be free of one of three meanings: either their sunnah was not to pray over them, then that ruling was abrogated afterward to pray over them; or that prayer he prayed over them was voluntary, with no basis for prayer over them in sunnah and obligation; or it is of their sunnah not to pray over them at the time of burial, but to pray over them after this long period. His deed (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cannot be free of these three meanings. We considered that and found the affair of prayer over all dead is to pray over them before their burial. Then people spoke about volunteering over them before burial and after burial; a group permitted that and others disliked it. The affair of sunnah in it is more emphatic than volunteering, due to their agreement on sunnah and disagreement on volunteering. If the slain of Uhud were of those he volunteered prayer over, in establishing that is establishing the sunnah in prayer over them before the time of volunteering it over them, and every volunteering has a basis in obligation. If it is established that that prayer was from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a volunteering he volunteered, it cannot be except with prayer over them being sunnah, like prayer over others. If his prayer over them was due to abrogation of his first deed and leaving prayer over them, then this prayer of his over them necessitates that of their sunnah is prayer over them, and that his leaving prayer over them at their burial is abrogated. If his prayer over them was only because thus is their sunnah, not to pray over them except after this period, and they were specified with that, it may be that likewise is the ruling of all martyrs, not to pray over them except after a like period passes. Or all martyrs may have prayer hastened over them, other than the martyrs of Uhud, whose sunnah was delaying prayer over them. It is established by all these meanings that of their sunnah is establishing prayer over them, either after a while or before burial. Then the speech between disputants in our time is only about establishing prayer over them before burial or leaving it entirely. When prayer over them after burial is established in this hadith, prayer over them before burial is more proper and worthier. Then there is narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning other than the martyrs of Uhud that he prayed over them."
"2703","2891 - Of that is what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak informed us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ikrimah ibn Khalid informed me that Ibn Abi Ammar informed him, from Shaddad ibn al-Had, that a Bedouin man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), believed in him and followed him, and said: I will emigrate with you. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entrusted him to some of his Companions. When there was a battle in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gained spoils, he divided and divided for him. His companions gave him what he divided for him, while he was watching their backs. When he came they handed it to him. He said: What is this? They said: A share the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided for you. He took it and brought it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Muhammad, what is this? He said: I divided it for you. He said: I did not follow you for this, but I followed you to be shot here, and he pointed to his throat, with an arrow, then die and enter Paradise. He said: If you are true to Allah, He will be true to you. They stayed a little, then rose to the enemy. He was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) carried, an arrow having struck him where he pointed. The Prophet said: Is it he? They said: Yes. He said: He was true to Allah, so He was true to him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shrouded him in the cloak of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then put him forward and prayed over him. Of what appeared of his prayer over him was: O Allah, this is Your servant, who went out emigrating in Your path, killed a martyr; I am a witness over him. So in this hadith is establishing prayer over martyrs who are not washed, because the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this hadith did not wash the man and prayed over him. So it is established by this hadith that likewise is the ruling of the martyr killed in the path of Allah in battle: prayed over and not washed. This is the ruling of this chapter by way of verifying the meanings of the reports. As for reasoning in that, we saw the dead of natural death washed and prayed over, and we saw him when prayed over and not washed to be in the ruling of one not prayed over. So prayer over him was guaranteed by the washing preceding it. If washing had occurred, prayer over him is permitted; if no washing, prayer over him is not permitted. Then we saw the martyr exempted from washing, so reasoning upon that would exempt what is guaranteed by the ruling of washing. In this is what necessitates leaving prayer over him, except that in that is a point: we saw one other than the martyr washed to be purified, and before washing in the ruling of the impure, prayer over him and burial in that state not proper, until transferred from it by washing. Then we saw the martyr, no harm in burying him in that state before washing, and he is in the ruling of all dead who were washed. So reasoning upon that is that prayer over them be in the ruling of all dead who were washed. This is the reasoning in this chapter with what witnessed to it of the reports, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad (may Allah Almighty have mercy on them)."
"2704","2892 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Khattab ibn Uthman al-Fawzi narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abdullah, he said: I heard Makhul asking Ubadah ibn Awfa al-Numayri about martyrs, are they prayed over? Ubadah said: Yes. So this is Ubadah ibn Awfa saying this, while the campaigns of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were mostly there toward Syria. It would not be hidden from its people what they did with their martyrs, of washing, prayer and other than that."
"2705","8 - Chapter of the child who dies: is he prayed over or not? 2893 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Amrah, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) buried his son Ibrahim (may Allah be pleased with him) and did not pray over him."
"2706","2894 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Naysaburi narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub narrated to us, and he mentioned the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: A group went to the view that the child is not prayed over, and argued in that with this hadith. And they narrated in that also from Samurah ibn Jundub what follows."
"2707","2895 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'mar narrated to us, he said: Abdul-Warith narrated to us, he said: Uqbah ibn Yasar narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Jahhash narrated to me, and he was the nephew of Samurah ibn Jundub, he said: A son of Samurah died, who had been given drink. He heard weeping and said: What is this? They said: Over so-and-so, he died. He forbade that, then called for a basin and a pick, so he was washed before him and shrouded before him. Then he said to his freedman so-and-so: Go with him to his pit; when you place him in his grave, say: In the name of Allah and upon the sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then loosen the knots of his head and the knots of his feet, and say: O Allah, do not deprive us of his reward, nor test us after him. He said: And he did not pray over him."
"2708","2896 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, meaning from Jallas, from Ibn Jihash, from Samurah ibn Jundub, that a boy of his died, and he said: Bury him and do not pray over him, for there is no sin upon him. Then call upon Allah for his parents to make him for them a forerunner and predecessor. Others differed with them in that and said: Rather the child is prayed over. And they argued in that with what follows."
"2709","2897 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Talhah ibn Yahya ibn Talhah, from his paternal aunt A'ishah bint Talhah, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: The Ansar brought a boy to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to pray over him. I said: And it was said to him: Glad tidings to him, O Messenger of Allah, he never did evil nor reached it, a sparrow of the sparrows of Paradise. He said: Or other than that? Allah Almighty, when He created Paradise, created for it people while they were in the loins of their fathers, and created Hell and created for it people while they were in the loins of their fathers."
"2710","2898 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Harmalah ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah, from his father that Abu Talhah called the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Umayr ibn Abi Talhah when he died. He came to them and prayed over him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went forward; Abu Talhah was behind him, and Umm Sulaym behind Abu Talhah; none other was with them. The marriage of Abu Talhah and Umm Sulaym was only after the coming of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Medina by a period, and Umayr is his son from her in that marriage. He died as a child. So this is his brother Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah mentioning that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over him."
"2711","2899 - Abdul-Aziz ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Sa'id al-Jubayri narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Jubayr ibn Hayyah, from his father, in what Abdul-Aziz thinks, doubting his father especially, from al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The child is prayed over."
"2712","2900 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Abdus-Salam narrated to us, from Layth, from Amir, from al-Bara, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Most deserving of those you pray over are your children. Amir al-Sha'bi said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had prayed over his son Ibrahim, and he would not have said that except that it was established with him."
"2713","2901 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Ibrahim son of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died at sixteen months, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over him."
"2714","2902 - al-Hasan ibn Abdullah ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to me, from Jabir, and he mentioned the like of it with its chain. Except that he said: At sixteen months, or eighteen months. So in these reports is establishing prayer over children. When the reports contradicted in that, we must look at the practice of Muslims upon which their customs run, act upon that, and it abrogates what opposes it. The custom of Muslims was prayer over their children, so what agreed of the reports is established, and what opposed is negated. This is the aspect of this chapter by way of reports. As for its aspect by way of reasoning, we saw children washed by agreement of Muslims upon that. We saw adults, everyone washed of them is prayed over, and whoever unwashed of martyrs, therein is disagreement. Some people pray over him, some do not pray over him. So washing occurs only with prayer after it, while prayer may occur with no washing before it. Since children are washed as adults are washed, it is established they are prayed over as adults are prayed over. This is the reasoning in this chapter, agreeing with the custom of Muslims in prayer over children. It is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad (may Allah Almighty have mercy on them), and that is narrated from a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2715","2903 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, from Yunus, from Nafi', that he narrated to him that Abdullah ibn Umar prayed in the house over a newborn of his, then commanded concerning him, so he was carried and buried."
"2716","2904 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Rashid informed us, from Ata, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: When the boy cries at birth, he is inherited from and prayed over."
"2717","2905 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, from Ibn Lahi'ah, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Mansur ibn Abi Mansur, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that he was asked for a ruling about a newborn boy who died: is he prayed over? He said: Yes."
"2718","2906 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, he said: I saw Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) pray over a newborn who never did a sin, and I heard him saying: O Allah, protect him from the punishment of the grave."
"2719","9 - Chapter of walking between graves with sandals. 2907 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: al-Aswad ibn Shayban narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Shumayr narrated to us, he said: Bashir ibn Nahik narrated to me, from Bashir ibn al-Khasasiyyah that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a man walking between graves in two sandals, and said: Woe to you, O wearer of the two sibtiyyah, cast off your two sibtiyyah."
"2720","2908 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Waki' narrated to us, from al-Aswad, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: A group went to this hadith and disliked walking with sandals between graves. Others differed with them and said: It may be that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded that man to remove the sandals, not because he disliked walking between graves with sandals, but for another reason, of filth he saw in them defiling the graves. We saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray with his sandals on, then he was commanded to remove them and removed them while praying. That was not upon dislike of prayer in sandals, but for the filth in them. And there is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates permission of walking between graves with sandals."
"2721","2909 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, and he mentioned a long hadith about the believer when buried in his grave: By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, he hears the striking of your sandals when you turn away from him going back."
"2722","2910 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Abdul-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr informed us, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"2723","2911 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Waki' narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Suddi, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), raised. The like of it. So this opposes the first hadith, if its meaning is upon what the people of the first view carried it. But we do not carry it upon opposition; we make both hadiths sound. We make the prohibition in the hadith of Bashir for the impurity in the sandals, lest he defile the graves, just as it is forbidden to defecate or urinate upon them. The hadith of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) indicates permission of walking with sandals in which there is no filth between graves. This is the aspect of this chapter by way of verifying the meanings of the reports. Reports are successive from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what we mentioned from him: his praying in his sandals, his removing them when he removed them for the impurity in them, and the permission of people praying in sandals."
"2724","2912 - Of that is what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Hamzah narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) removed his sandals while praying, so those behind him removed theirs. He said: What carried you to remove your sandals? They said: We saw you remove, so we removed. He said: Jibril (peace be upon him) informed me that in one of them is filth, so I removed them for that. Do not remove your sandals."
"2725","2913 - Ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, he said: Abdur-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Maslamah Sa'id ibn Yazid al-Azdi, he said: I asked Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him): Did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray in sandals? He said: Yes."
"2726","2914 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Alqamah ibn Qays, and he did not hear it from him, that Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) came to Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, and prayer came. Abu Musa said: Advance, O Abu Abdur-Rahman, for you are older and more knowledgeable. He said: Advance you, for we came to you in your house and your mosque, so you are more deserving. Abu Musa advanced and removed his sandals. When he gave salutation he said: What did you intend by removing them, are you at the sacred valley Tuwa? We saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray in leather socks and sandals."
"2727","2915 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abu Na'amah, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you comes to the mosque, let him look in his sandals; if in them is harm or filth, let him wipe them, then pray in them."
"2728","2916 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abdul-Malik ibn Umayr, from a man of Banu al-Harith ibn Ka'b, he said: I was sitting with Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), and a man said: O Abu Hurayrah, did you forbid people to pray in their sandals? He said: I did not do that, except that I, by the Lord of this sanctuary, saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray in this place with his sandals on him."
"2729","2917 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abdul-Malik, he said: One who heard Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) saying informed me: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in his sandals."
"2730","2918 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Abdul-Malik ibn Umayr, from Ziyad al-Harithi, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), and he mentioned the like of it."
"2731","2919 - Rabi' al-Jizi and Salih ibn Abdur-Rahman narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Maslamah narrated to us, he said: Mujammi' ibn Ya'qub al-Ansari narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Isma'il, he said: It was said to Abdullah ibn Abi Habibah: What do you remember of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray in his sandals."
"2732","2920 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Husayn al-Mu'allim, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed barefoot and shod."
"2733","2921 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from al-Suddi, he said: One who heard Ibn Hurayth saying informed me: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray in two patched sandals."
"2734","2922 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb and Abu al-Walid narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Nu'man ibn Salim, in the hadith of Wahb, from Ibn Amr ibn Aws, and in the hadith of Abu al-Walid, he said: I heard a man whose grandfather is Aws ibn Abi Aws, he said: My grandfather would pray and command me to hand him his sandals, and he would wear them and say: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray in his sandals."
"2735","2923 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, and he mentioned the like of what Abu Bakrah mentioned from Wahb."
"2736","2924 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from Umayr ibn Abdullah, from Abdul-Malik, meaning Ibn al-Mughirah al-Ta'ifi, from Aws ibn Aws, or Aws ibn Uways, he said: I stayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) half a month, and I saw him pray with two strapped sandals on him."
"2737","2925 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Rabi'ah narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Abdul-Malik, from Sa'id ibn Fayruz, from his father that the delegation of Thaqif came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). They said: We saw him pray with two strapped sandals on him. Since entering mosques with sandals is not disliked, and praying in them is also not disliked, walking in them between graves is more fitting not to be disliked. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad (may Allah Almighty have mercy on them)."
"2738","10 - Chapter of burial at night. 2926 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Mubarak ibn Fadalah narrated to us, he said: Nasr ibn Rashid narrated to us, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, that a man of Banu Udhrah was buried at night, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not pray over him, so he forbade burial at night."
"2739","2927 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Imran narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: Ibn Abi Layla narrated to me, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not bury your dead at night. Abu Ja'far said: A group disliked burying the dead at night, and argued in that with this hadith. Others differed with them and saw no harm in burial at night. And they argued in that with what follows."
"2740","2928 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir, he said: A fire was seen in the graveyard at night, and behold the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a grave, saying: Hand me your companion."
"2741","2929 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Ta'ifi narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: Jabir ibn Abdullah informed me, or he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah, the like of it. And he added: He is the man who used to raise his voice with the Qur'an. So in this hadith is permission of burial at night. It may be that the prohibition we mentioned in the first chapter is not by way of dislike of burial at night, but due to the desire of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to pray over all the dead of Muslims, for what they have in that of favor and good by his prayer over them."
"2742","2930 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Hakim al-Ansari, from Kharijah ibn Zayd, from Yazid ibn Thabit that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let me not know that one of the believers died but you inform me to pray over him, for my prayer over them is mercy."
"2743","2931 - As Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Thabit, from Abu Rafi', from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he entered the graveyard and prayed over a man after he was buried, and said: This graveyard was filled with light after it was dark over him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) may have intended by his prohibition of burying the dead at night that he himself pray over them, so they attain by his prayer what we described of favor. It is said: He forbade that only for another reason."
"2744","2932 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Humran narrated to us, from Ash'ath, from al-Hasan, that people used to make poor shrouds of their dead and bury them at night, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade night burial. So al-Hasan informed that the prohibition of burial at night was only for this cause, not because night burial is disliked. And there is narrated from Jabir ibn Abdullah the like of that."
"2745","2933 - Rawh, he is Ibn al-Faraj, narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Ja'far, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed one day and mentioned a man of his Companions who died, shrouded poorly, and buried at night. He rebuked that a man be buried at night, so that he pray over him, except compelled to that, and said: When one of you takes charge of his brother, let him make his shroud good. So he combined in this, meaning the hadith, the two causes said to be the reason of the prohibition. There is no harm in prayer over the dead at night and burying them in it also. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad (may Allah Almighty have mercy on them). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was so done with, buried at night."
"2746","2934 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, he said: Abdah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Fatimah bint Muhammad, from Amrah bint Abdur-Rahman, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: We did not know of the burial of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until we heard the sound of shovels at the end of the night, the night of Wednesday. This in the presence of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), none of them disapproving. That indicates that the prohibition of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of burial at night was only for an incidental cause, not because night burial is disliked when there is no incidental cause. Uqbah ibn Amir said: Three hours the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to forbid us to pray in them and to bury our dead in them: when the sun rises until it ascends, when the midday stander stands until it declines, and when the sun inclines to setting until it sets. We mentioned that with its chain in what preceded of this book of ours. That indicates that what is besides these times is opposite to them in prayer over the dead and burying them in disapproval."
"2747","2935 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Uqayl. (H). 2936 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn al-Dayf narrated to us, he said: Abdur-Razzaq narrated to us, from Ma'mar, both together, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: Ali ibn Abi Talib buried Fatimah (may Allah Almighty be pleased with them) at night."
"2748","2937 - Nasr ibn Marzuq and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Abu Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Uqayl, from al-Zuhri, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it. So this is Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) who saw no harm in burial at night, and Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) nor any of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disapproved that."
"2749","2938 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was buried at night."
"2750","2939 - Bakr ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Abu Abdur-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ulayy narrated to us, he said: I heard my father, from Uqbah, that a man asked him: Is burial at night? He said: Yes, Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was buried at night. So we see no harm in burial at night. It is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad (may Allah Almighty have mercy on them)."
"2751","11 - Chapter of sitting on graves. 2940 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Sadaqah ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, from Busr ibn Ubaydullah, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Wathilah ibn al-Asqa', from Abu Marthad al-Ghanawi, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Do not pray toward graves, nor sit upon them."
"2752","2941 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Hamid ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Abdur-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir narrated to us, that he heard Busr ibn Ubaydullah al-Hadrami, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"2753","2942 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Busr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: Abdur-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir narrated to me, from Bishr that he heard Wathilah, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"2754","2943 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak saying: Abdur-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir narrated to us, he said: I heard Busr ibn Ubaydullah saying: I heard Abu Idris al-Khawlani saying: I heard Wathilah ibn al-Asqa' saying: I heard Abu Marthad al-Ghanawi saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying that."
"2755","2944 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Ibn Lahi'ah, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from al-Nadr ibn Ubaydullah al-Sulami, then al-Ansari, from Amr ibn Hazm, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw me upon a grave and said: Come down from the grave, do not harm the occupant of the grave, lest he harm you."
"2756","Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Khazim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade plastering graves, writing on them, sitting on them, and building over them."
"2757","Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Hafs narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, and he mentioned the same with its chain of narration."
"2758","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim narrated to us, he said: Mubarak ibn Fadalah narrated to us, from Nasr ibn Rashid, from Jabir ibn 'Abdillah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us to sit on graves."
"2759","Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih. And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For one of you to sit on a live coal until it burns his clothes and reaches his skin is better for him than to sit on a grave. Abu Ja'far said: A group adopted these reports and followed them, and disliked sitting on graves because of them. Others disagreed with them and said: He did not forbid that out of dislike for sitting on the grave itself; rather what was meant is sitting to relieve oneself, to defecate or urinate, and that is valid usage in the language, as one says: so-and-so sat to relieve himself, so-and-so sat to urinate. And they argued for that with what follows."
"2760","Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn 'Ali narrated to us, he said: 'Uthman ibn Hakim narrated to us, from Abu Umamah, that Zayd ibn Thabit said: Come here, O son of my brother, let me tell you: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only forbade sitting on graves for passing stool or urine. So Zayd clarified in this hadith what the prohibited sitting in the earlier reports is. And something similar has been reported from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him."
"2761","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd informed me that Muhammad ibn Ka'b al-Qurazi informed them, he said: Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, only said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever sits on a grave urinating on it or defecating, it is as if he sat on a coal of fire."
"2762","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ka'b, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever squats on a grave and defecates on it or urinates, it is as if he squatted on a live coal. Thus it is established that the prohibited sitting in the earlier reports is this sitting; as for sitting for other than that, it is not included in that prohibition. And this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them. And that has been reported from 'Ali and Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them."
"2763","'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to me, from 'Amr ibn al-Harith, from Bukayr, that Yahya ibn Abi Muhammad narrated to him that a freedman of the family of 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to him that 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, used to sit on graves. And the freedman said: I used to spread for him in the graveyard, and he would pillow his head on a grave, then lie down."
"2764","'Ali narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Bakr narrated to me, from 'Amr, from Bukayr, that Nafi' narrated to him that 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to sit on graves."
"2765","Book of Zakat. Chapter on charity to Banu Hashim. Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: A caravan arrived in Madinah, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bought goods from it and sold them for a profit of silver ounces, then gave it in charity to the widows of Banu 'Abd al-Muttalib, then said: I will never again buy anything after this while its price is not with me. Abu Ja'far said: A group adopted this hadith and permitted charity to Banu Hashim. Others disagreed with them and said: Charity is not permissible for Banu Hashim, neither from obligatory alms nor voluntary charity nor anything else; they are like the rich: whatever charity is forbidden to the rich is forbidden to Banu Hashim, whether poor or rich. And everything permissible for rich people other than Banu Hashim is permissible for Banu Hashim, poor and rich alike. In our view, those who hold this position have no proof in the first hadith, because it is possible that what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave in charity to the widows of Banu 'Abd al-Muttalib was not of the kind of charity that is forbidden to Banu Hashim in the view of those who forbid it to them, but rather of the kind of charity that is permissible for them. For we see that a man may give one of the rich, other than Banu Hashim, his house or his slave in charity, and that is valid and lawful, and his wealth does not forbid it to him. So what his wealth forbids him of charities is the obligatory alms, expiations, and charities by which closeness to Allah the Exalted is sought. As for charities intended as gifts even if called charities, they are not forbidden. Likewise Banu Hashim: what was forbidden to them due to their kinship of charities is like what was forbidden to the rich due to their wealth. Whatever was not forbidden to the rich due to their wealth is not forbidden to Banu Hashim due to their kinship. Therefore we regard what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave in charity to their widows as being in the category of gifts even if it was called charity, and this is how the interpretation of that first hadith should properly be understood, because there has been reported from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, what follows."
"2766","Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Sa'id and Hammad, the two sons of Zayd, narrated to us, from Abu Jahdam Musa ibn Salim, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Ubaydillah ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: We entered upon Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not single us out with anything apart from the people except three things: performing ablution thoroughly, not eating charity, and not breeding donkeys upon horses."
"2767","Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Abu Jahdam, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"2768","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Murajja ibn Raja' narrated to us, from Abu Jahdam, and he mentioned the same with its chain. Abu Ja'far said: So this Ibn 'Abbas reports in this hadith that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) singled them out with not eating charity. So the first hadith must either be as we mentioned in the first discussion, so that what was permitted to them in it is other than what was forbidden to them in this second hadith, with each one's meaning as we mentioned; or the first hadith permits what this second hadith prohibits, in which case this second hadith abrogates it, because 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas reports in it, after the death of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that they are singled out with it apart from the people, so it can only be that it remained in force at that time of his. And if someone argues for the permissibility of charity to them from the charities of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he mentioned what follows."
"2769","Fahd narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn Musafir narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, that 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, informed him that Fatimah, daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), sent to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, asking him for her inheritance from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what Allah had granted as fay' to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Fatimah at that time was seeking the charity of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Madinah and Fadak, and what remained of the Khaybar fifth. So Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We are not inherited from; what we leave is charity; the family of Muhammad only eat from this wealth. And by Allah, I will not change anything of the charity of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from the state it was in during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I will act in it as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) acted."
"2770","Nasr ibn Marzuq and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us. And Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abdillah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, they both said: al-Layth narrated to us, he said: 'Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"2771","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan al-Nadari informed me, he said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, sent for me and said: Some households of your people have arrived in Madinah, and I have ordered a grant for them, so distribute it among them. While I was thus engaged, Yarfa' came to him and said: Here are 'Uthman, 'Abd al-Rahman, Sa'd, and al-Zubayr, and I do not know whether he mentioned Talhah or not, seeking permission to enter upon you. He said: Admit them. He said: Then we waited a while, and he said: Here are al-'Abbas and 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, seeking permission. He said: Admit them. When al-'Abbas entered, he said: O Commander of the Believers, judge between me and this man; they were then disputing over what Allah had granted as fay' to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from the properties of Banu al-Nadir. The people said: Judge between them, O Commander of the Believers, and relieve each one of them of the other. So 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I adjure you by Allah, by Whose permission the heavens and the earth stand, do you know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We are not inherited from; what we leave is charity? They said: He did say that. Then he said the same to the two of them, and they said: Yes. He said: I will tell you about this fay'. Allah the Almighty singled out His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with something He did not give anyone else, and said: Whatever Allah granted as fay' to His Messenger from them, you did not spur toward it any horses or camels. So this belonged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) exclusively; then, by Allah, he did not withhold it from you nor favor himself with it over you; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) distributed it among you and dispersed it among you until there remained of it this wealth. He used to spend from it on his family a year's provision, then gather what remained of it as Allah's wealth. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, Abu Bakr said: I am the guardian of the Messenger of Allah after him; I act in it as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to act. Then he mentioned the rest of the hadith."
"2772","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned the same with its chain, and he affirmed that Talhah was among the people, and he did not say: and dispersed it among you."
"2773","Yazid ibn Sinan and Abu Umayyah narrated to us, they said: Bishr ibn 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned the same with its chain, and he said: He used to spend from it on his family."
"2774","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from Sufyan and Warqa', from Abu al-Zinad, from 'Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: My heirs do not divide a dinar; what I leave after the maintenance of my family and the provision of my servant is charity. They said: In this hadith of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, is evidence that it was charity during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), because of his words: after the provision of my servant, and his servant could only exist while he was alive. They said: In these reports is evidence that charity is permissible for Banu Hashim, because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his family, among them Fatimah his daughter, used to eat from this charity during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So that indicates the permissibility of all charities to them. The counter-argument against them is that that charity is like the charities of endowments, and we see that lawful for the rich. Do you not see that if a man endows his house upon a rich man, that is valid and his wealth does not prevent it, and its ruling differs from the ruling of other charities of obligatory alms, expiations, and what is used to seek closeness to Allah the Almighty? So likewise for whoever is of Banu Hashim, that is lawful for them, and its ruling differs from the ruling of the other charities we mentioned. Then there came after these reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), consecutively transmitted, prohibiting charity to Banu Hashim. Among what came in that is what follows."
"2775","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Burayd ibn Abi Maryam, from Abu al-Hawra' al-Sa'di, who said: I said to al-Hasan ibn 'Ali: What do you remember from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: I remember that I took a date from the dates of charity and put it in my mouth, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it out with its saliva and threw it back among the dates. A man said: O Messenger of Allah, what harm is this date to this boy? He said: We, the family of Muhammad, charity is not lawful for us."
"2776","Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Thabit ibn 'Umarah, from Rabi'ah ibn Shayban, who said: I said to al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned something similar, except that he said at its end: nor to anyone of his family."
"2777","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Arqam ibn Abi al-Arqam al-Zuhri was employed over the charities, and he engaged Abu Rafi' to follow him. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him, and he said: O Abu Rafi', charity is forbidden to Muhammad and to the family of Muhammad, and the freedman of a people is of themselves."
"2778","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma' narrated to us, he said: Juwayriyah ibn Asma' narrated to us, from Malik, from al-Zuhri, that 'Abdullah ibn 'Abdillah ibn Nawfal ibn al-Harith ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib narrated to him, that 'Abd al-Muttalib ibn Rabi'ah ibn al-Harith narrated to him, he said: Rabi'ah ibn al-Harith and al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib met and said: If only we sent these two boys, meaning himself and al-Fadl ibn al-'Abbas, over the charities, so they would deliver what the people deliver and receive what the people receive. He said: While they were discussing that, 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, came and stood over them, and they mentioned that to him. So 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Do not do it, for by Allah he will not do it. So Rabi'ah ibn al-Harith said: Nothing keeps you from this but envy of us; by Allah, you attained kinship by marriage to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and we did not envy you for it. So 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I am Abu Hasan; send them both. So they went, and he lay down. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Zuhr, we raced him to the chamber and stood at its door until he came; he took hold of our ears and said: Spit out what you are keeping secret. Then he entered and we entered upon him, and he was that day with Zaynab bint Jahsh. We left the talking to one another, then one of us spoke, saying: O Messenger of Allah, you are the kindest of people and the most joining of kinship; we have reached marriageable age, and we have come to you to appoint us over some of the charities, so we deliver to you as they deliver and receive as they receive. He remained silent until we were about to speak to him, and Zaynab kept signaling to us from behind the curtain not to speak to him. Then he said: Charity is not befitting for the family of Muhammad; it is only the filth of the people. Call Mahmiyah for me, and he was in charge of the fifth, and Nawfal ibn al-Harith ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib. So they both came to him. He said to Mahmiyah: Marry this boy to your daughter, meaning al-Fadl ibn al-'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, so he married him. And he said to Nawfal ibn al-Harith: Marry this boy to your daughter, so he married me. And he said to Mahmiyah: Pay the dowry on behalf of both of them from the fifth, such-and-such. If someone says: He paid the dowry on their behalf from the fifth, and its ruling is the ruling of charities, it is said to him: It is possible that was from the share of the near relatives in the fifth, and that is outside the charities forbidden to them, because He only forbade them the filth of the people, and the fifth is not so."
"2779","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from 'Ubayd al-Mukattib, from Abu al-Tufayl, from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I brought the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) charity and he returned it, and I brought him a gift and he accepted it."
"2780","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from 'Asim ibn 'Umar ibn Qatadah, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: Salman al-Farisi, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me a long hadith, in which he mentioned that he was a slave. He said: In the evening I gathered what I had, then went out until I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was at Quba'. I entered upon him with a group of his Companions, and I said: It has reached me that you have nothing in hand and that you have companions with you, and you are people of need and strangeness, and I had something I had set aside for charity; when your place was mentioned to me, I deemed you most deserving of it, then I presented it to him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Eat it, or keep it. Then I came to him after he had moved to Madinah, having gathered something, and said: I saw that you do not eat charity, and I had something with which I wished to honor you, an honor that is not charity, so he ate, and his Companions ate."
"2781","Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibn Abi Rafi', the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a man of Banu Makhzum over the charity. He said to Abu Rafi': Accompany me so that you may get some of it. He said: Until I ask permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him, and he said: The family of Muhammad, charity is not lawful for them, and the freedman of a people is of themselves."
"2782","Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Warqa' ibn 'Umar narrated to us, from 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, who said: I entered upon Umm Kulthum bint 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, and she said: A freedman of ours called Hurmuz, or Kaysan, informed me that he passed by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: He called me and I came. He said: O Abu so-and-so, we, the household, have been forbidden to eat charity, and the freedman of a people is of themselves, so do not eat charity."
"2783","Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Shababab ibn Sawwar narrated to us. And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us. And Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, they all said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ziyad, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: al-Hasan ibn 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, took a date from the dates of charity and put it in his mouth, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Kakh, kakh, throw it, throw it; do you not know that we do not eat charity?"
"2784","Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Bahz ibn Hakim narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought something, he would ask: Is it a gift or charity? If they said: A gift, he would extend his hands; and if they said: Charity, he would say to his Companions: Eat."
"2785","Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: 'Abdullah ibn Bakr narrated to us, from Bahz ibn Hakim, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say concerning grazing camels: For every forty, a bint labun; whoever gives it seeking its reward, he has its reward, and whoever withholds it, we shall take it from him along with half his camels as a binding decree of our Lord's decrees; none of it is lawful for any of us."
"2786","Ibn Marzuq and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pass by a date on the road, and nothing would prevent him from taking it except fear that it might be charity."
"2787","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, he said: Mansur narrated to us, from Talhah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a date and said: Were it not that I fear it might be charity, I would have eaten it."
"2788","'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: al-Hakam ibn Marwan al-Darir narrated to us. And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ma'ruf ibn Wasil al-Sa'di narrated to us, he said: Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated to us in the year ninety; Ibn Abi Dawud said in his hadith: daughter of Talq, saying: Rashid ibn Malik Abu 'Umayr narrated to us, he said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a tray with dates was brought. He said: Is it charity or a gift? He said: Rather charity. So he placed it before the people, while al-Hasan was crawling before him. The boy took a date and put it in his mouth. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) inserted his finger and dealt gently with him, took it out and threw it away, then said: We, the family of Muhammad, do not eat charity."
"2789","'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Hakim al-Awdi narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Isa, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from his father, who said: I entered with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the charity storehouse, and al-Hasan took a date, so he took it out of his mouth and said: We, the household, charity is not lawful for us, or: we do not eat charity."
"2790","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, and he mentioned the same with its chain, except that he said: We, the household, charity is not lawful for us, without doubt."
"2791","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Ma'mar informed us, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I return to my family and find a date fallen on my bed in my house, so I lift it to eat it, then I fear it might be charity and so I throw it away."
"2792","Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Mu'min al-Khurasani narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn Waqid narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Buraydah narrated to us, he said: I heard my father say: Salman al-Farisi came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he arrived in Madinah with a tray bearing fresh dates. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is this, O Salman? He said: Charity for you and your Companions. He said: Take it away, for we do not eat charity, so he took it away. The next day he came to him with the like of it and placed it before him, and he said: What is this, O Salman? He said: A gift. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his Companions: Help yourselves. Abu Ja'far said: All these reports have come prohibiting charity to Banu Hashim, and we know of nothing that abrogated them or opposed them except what we have mentioned in this chapter, in which there is no evidence contradicting them. If someone says: That charity is only zakat specifically, while other charities are unobjectionable, it is said to him: In these reports is what refutes what you held, namely what is in the hadith of Bahz ibn Hakim that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when something was brought to him, would ask: Is it a gift or charity? If they said: Charity, he would say to his Companions: Eat, contenting himself with the answer that it is charity without asking whether it is charity from zakat or otherwise. That indicates that the ruling of all charities in that is the same. And in the hadith of Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, he came and he said: Is it a gift or charity? I said: Rather charity, because it reached me that you are poor people, so he refrained from eating it for that reason, though Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, was at that time a slave, of those upon whom no zakat is due. That indicates that all charities, voluntary and otherwise, were forbidden to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and to all Banu Hashim. Reasoned analogy also indicates the equality of the ruling of obligatory and voluntary charity in that: we see that non-Hashimis, rich and poor, are alike regarding obligatory and voluntary charities: whoever is forbidden to take an obligatory charity is forbidden to take a non-obligatory charity. Since Banu Hashim were forbidden to take obligatory charities, they are forbidden to take non-obligatory charities. This is the reasoned view in this chapter, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. But there is disagreement reported from Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, in that: it is reported from him that he said: There is no harm in all charities to Banu Hashim. In our view he held that because charities were only forbidden to them on account of what was assigned to them in the fifth, the share of the near relatives. When that ceased from them and reverted to others with the death of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what had been forbidden to them became lawful to them on account of what had been made lawful to them. And Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to me, from his father, from Muhammad, from Abu Yusuf, from Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on them, in that the like of Abu Yusuf's view, may Allah have mercy on him, and by this we take. If someone asks: Do you dislike it for their freedmen? I say: Yes, because of the hadith of Abu Rafi' which we have mentioned in this chapter; Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, said that in the Book of Dictation, and I know of none of our companions who disagreed with him in that. If someone asks: Do you dislike for the Hashimi to work over the charity? I say: No. If he says: Why, when in the hadith of Ibn Rabi'ah ibn al-Harith and al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas which you mentioned, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prevented them from that? I say: There is no prevention in it from that, because they asked him to employ them over the charity so as to relieve their poverty thereby, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) relieved their poverty by other than that. It is also possible that by preventing them he meant sparing them the task of working over the filth of the people, not that it is forbidden to them to take their wage for working over it. And we have found what indicates this."
"2793","Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn 'Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Musa ibn Abi 'A'ishah, from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Razin, from Abu Razin, from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I said to al-'Abbas: Ask the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to employ you over the charities. So he asked him, and he said: I would never employ you over the washings of people's sins. Do you not see that he only disliked for him employment over the washings of people's sins, not that he forbade it to him due to the unlawfulness of taking wages from it. Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, used to dislike for Banu Hashim to work over charity if their wage came from it. He said: Because charity passes from the giver's wealth to the categories Allah the Exalted named, so the collector would own part of it, and it is not lawful for him. He also argued in that with the hadith of Abu Rafi' when the Makhzumi asked him to go out with him to get some of it, and it is inconceivable that he would get any of it except by his work over it and taking wages from it. Others disagreed with Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, in that and said: There is no harm in the Hashimi taking wages from it, because he only takes wages for his work, and that is lawful for the rich. Since this is not forbidden to the rich, whose wealth forbids them charity, likewise by analogy it is not forbidden to Banu Hashim, whose lineage forbids them taking charity. And there has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning what was given in charity to Barirah, that he ate of it and said: It is charity for her, and a gift for us."
"2794","Fahd narrated to us of that, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from 'A'ishah, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me while a sheep's leg was hanging in the house. He said: What is this? I said: It was given in charity to Barirah, and she gifted it to us. He said: It is charity for her, and a gift for us, then he ordered it and it was roasted."
"2795","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Rabi'ah ibn Abi 'Abd al-Rahman, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered while the pot was boiling with meat, a stew of the household's condiments. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did I not see a pot with meat? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah, but that is meat given in charity to Barirah, and you do not eat charity. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is charity for her, and a gift for us."
"2796","'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Maslamah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Rabi'ah, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"2797","'Ali narrated to us, he said: 'Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Charity was given to Barirah, and she gifted some of it to 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: It is a gift for us, and charity for her."
"2798","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ubayd ibn al-Sabiq, from Juwayriyah bint al-Harith, who said: Charity of a piece of meat was given to a freedwoman of mine. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me and said: Do you have any supper? I said: O Messenger of Allah, charity of a piece of meat was given to my freedwoman so-and-so, and she gifted it to me, and you do not eat charity. He said: It has reached its proper place, so bring it; that is, hand it to me. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate of it."
"2799","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: 'Ubayd ibn al-Sabbaq informed me, from Juwayriyah, the same."
"2800","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Khalid al-Hadhdha' narrated to us, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm 'Atiyyah, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and said: Do you have anything? She said: No, except something Nusaybah sent us of the sheep that was sent to her from charity. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It has reached its proper place."
"2801","Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, from Abu Ma'n ibn Yazid ibn Yasar, from Ya'qub ibn 'Abdillah ibn al-Ashajj, from 'Abdullah ibn Wahb, from Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) distributed sheep from charity and sent to Zaynab al-Thaqafiyyah a sheep of it. Zaynab gifted us some of its meat. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon us and said: Do you have anything to feed us? We said: No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Did I not just see meat brought in to you? We said: O Messenger of Allah, that is from the sheep you sent to Zaynab from charity, and you do not eat charity, so we did not wish to keep what you would not eat of. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had I found it I would have eaten of it. Since what was given in charity to Barirah was lawful for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to eat because he owned it by gift, it is likewise lawful for the Hashimi to take wages from charity, because he owns it by his work, not by charity. This is the reasoned view, and it is sounder than what Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, held in that."
"2802","Chapter: The able-bodied poor man: is charity lawful for him or not? Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Sa'd ibn Ibrahim informed me, he said: I heard Rayhan ibn Yazid, and he was a truthful Bedouin, say: 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr said: Charity is not lawful for a rich man, nor for one of sound strength."
"2803","Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sa'd, from a man of Banu 'Amir, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, saying that."
"2804","Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us. And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, they both said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Rayhan ibn Yazid, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"2805","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: 'Ikrimah ibn 'Ammar al-Yamami narrated to us, from Simak Abu Zumayl, from a man of Banu Hilal, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the same."
"2806","'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Mu'alla ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"2807","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash, from Abu Husayn, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"2808","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, and he mentioned the same with its chain. Abu Ja'far said: A group held that charity is not lawful for one of sound strength, and regarded him in it like the rich, arguing with these reports. Others disagreed with them and said: Every poor person, able-bodied or disabled, charity is lawful for him. They interpreted the earlier reports to mean that the Prophet's words (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Charity is not lawful for one of sound strength, mean that it is not lawful for him as it is lawful for the disabled poor man who cannot do other than it, taking it out of necessity and need, from every direction of his need for it. One of sound strength, able to earn other than it, is not like him in its lawfulness for him, because the disabled poor man is lawful for him on account of disability and his inability to do other than it. And one of sound strength is only lawful for him on account of poverty specifically, though both may lawfully take it; yet it is better for the able-bodied to leave it and eat from earning by his work. A thing may be spoken of emphatically and said: It is not lawful, or it is not so, meaning that the causes by which that matter becomes lawful are not fully present, though that matter may be lawful with less than the fullness of those causes. An example is what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: The poor man is not the one who goes around, nor the one whom a date or two, or a morsel or two, turn away; rather the poor man is the one who does not ask and is not noticed so that charity is given to him. The poor man who asks is not thereby outside the causes of poverty and its rulings so that taking charity is unlawful for him and one who gives him of it has not discharged his duty; rather that means he is not a poor man with fully present causes of poverty. Likewise his words: Charity is not lawful for one of sound strength, mean that it is not lawful for him with all the causes by which charity becomes lawful, though it may be lawful for him with some of those causes. The adherents of the first view also argued for their school with what follows."
"2809","Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ja'far ibn 'Awn narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn 'Urwah narrated to us, from his father, from 'Ubaydillah ibn 'Adi ibn al-Khiyar, who said: Two men of my people narrated to me that they came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was distributing charity and asked him of it. He raised his gaze and lowered it, and saw them to be strong, sturdy men, and said: If you wish, I shall do it, but there is no share in it for a rich man, nor for a strong earner."
"2810","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith and al-Layth ibn Sa'd informed me, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"2811","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah and Hammam narrated to us, from Hisham, and he mentioned the same with its chain. They said: He said to them: There is no share in it for a strong earner, which indicates that the strong earner has no portion in charity, and one who gives him of it has not discharged his duty. The counter-argument of the others against them is that his words: If you wish, I shall do it, and there is no share in it for a rich man, mean that your wealth is hidden from me: if you are rich, you have no share in it, and if you wish, I shall do it, because I do not know of your wealth, so giving you is permissible for me, while taking what I give you is forbidden for you if you know of the reality of your affairs regarding wealth contrary to the outward appearance by which I inferred your poverty. This is the meaning of his words: If you wish, I shall do it, and there is no share in it for a rich man. As for his words: nor for a strong earner, that means he has no share from all the directions by which a share becomes due, so its meaning returns to the meaning we mentioned of his words: nor for one of sound strength. It may be said: So-and-so is truly learned, when the causes by which a man is learned are fully present in him, and it is not said: He is truly learned, when he is below that, though he is learned. Likewise it is not said: Truly poor, except of one in whom the causes by which a poor man is poor are fully present, though he is poor. Therefore he said to them: nor any share in it for a strong earner, that is, he has no share in it so as to be of its people truly while being a strong earner. Had it not been permissible for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to give to the strong earner if poor, he would not have said to them: If you wish, I shall do it. And this is the most proper interpretation of these reports, because if they are interpreted as the adherents of the first view interpreted them, they would contradict others reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Among that is what follows."
"2812","Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn 'Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Hamzah, from Hilal ibn Hisn, who said: I stayed at the house of Abu Sa'id al-Khudri in Madinah, and the sitting brought us together, and he said: They woke one morning having bound a stone over his belly from hunger. His wife or his mother said to him: If only you went to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him, for so-and-so came to him and asked him and he gave him, and so-and-so came to him and asked him and he gave him. I said: No, by Allah, until I seek work. So I sought work and found nothing, then I hastened to him while he was delivering a sermon, saying: Whoever seeks independence, Allah will make him independent; whoever seeks chastity, Allah will make him chaste; whoever asks us, we either give him generously or console him; and whoever remains chaste from us and independent is dearer to us than one who asks us. He said: So I returned and never asked anyone after that, and Allah kept providing for us until I knew no household in Madinah of greater means than us."
"2813","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Hilal ibn Murrah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, who said: We were once needy, so I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever seeks chastity, Allah will make him chaste; whoever seeks independence, Allah will make him independent; and whoever asks us, we shall give him. He said: I said: I shall seek chastity so Allah makes me chaste, and seek independence so Allah makes me independent. He said: By Allah, only days passed until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) distributed raisins and sent us some of them, then distributed barley and sent us some of it, then worldly wealth flowed upon us and overwhelmed us except whom Allah protected."
"2814","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Hilal ibn Husayn, brother of Banu Murrah ibn 'Abbad, from Abu Sa'id, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Ibn Abi Dawud said: This is the correct one. Abu Ja'far said: This is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever asks us, we shall give him, addressing thereby his Companions, most of whom were healthy, with no disability, except that he was poor; he did not bar them from it due to their health. That indicates what we mentioned, and he preferred one who is chaste and does not ask over one who asks. Abu Sa'id did not ask him because of that, though had he asked him he would have given him, since he had offered that to him and to his likes among his Companions. And there has also been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) through another channel what indicates what we mentioned."
"2815","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn An'um informed me, from Ziyad ibn Nu'aym, that he heard Ziyad ibn al-Harith al-Suda'i say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) put me in charge of my people. I said: O Messenger of Allah, give me of their charities. So he did, and wrote me a document to that effect. Then a man came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, give me of the charity. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah the Almighty was not content with the judgment of a prophet or anyone else concerning charities until He Himself judged concerning them from heaven, dividing them into eight shares; if you are of those shares, I shall give you of it. Abu Ja'far said: This al-Suda'i, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had put him in charge of his people, and it is inconceivable that he put him in charge while he had a disability. Then he asked him of the charity of his people, which is their zakat, and he gave him of it, not barring him due to soundness of body. Then the other man asked him after that, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: If you are of the shares into which Allah the Almighty divided charity, I shall give you of it. Thus the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) referred the ruling of charities to what Allah the Almighty referred it in His words: Charities are only for the poor and the needy, to the end of the verse. So whoever falls under the name of one of those categories is of the people of charity whom Allah the Almighty assigned to them in His Book, and His Messenger in his Sunnah, whether disabled or healthy. The most proper interpretation for us of the reports we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the first discussion, of his words: Charity is not lawful for one of sound strength, is what we interpreted them as, so that their meaning does not depart from the decisive verse we mentioned, nor from these other hadiths we narrated, and the meaning of all of that is one, each confirming the other. Then Qabisah ibn al-Mukhariq reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what also indicates that."
"2816","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Harun ibn Ri'ab, from Kinanah ibn Nu'aym, from Qabisah ibn al-Mukhariq, that he undertook a surety obligation, then came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him concerning it. He said: We shall discharge it for you from the camels of charity, or the livestock of charity. O Qabisah, asking has been forbidden except in three cases: a man who undertook a surety obligation, for whom asking is lawful until he discharges it, then he refrains; a man struck by a calamity that swept away his wealth, for whom asking is lawful until he attains sufficiency of livelihood, or a prop of livelihood, then he refrains; and a man struck by need until three judicious men of his people testify that asking has become lawful for him, until he attains sufficiency of livelihood, or a prop of livelihood, then he refrains. Whatever asking besides that is ill-gotten gain."
"2817","Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Harun ibn Ri'ab, from Kinanah ibn Nu'aym al-'Adawi, from Qabisah ibn al-Mukhariq, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar."
"2818","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Harun ibn Ri'ab, and he mentioned the same with its chain, adding: a man who undertook a surety obligation on behalf of his people intending reconciliation thereby. So in this hadith the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the needy to ask for his need until he attains sufficiency of livelihood, or a prop of livelihood. That indicates that charity is not forbidden by health when the one to whom it is given intends thereby to close his poverty; it is only forbidden to him when he intends thereby other than that, such as accumulation and the like. Whoever intends thereby that is of those who seek it for other than the three meanings the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned in the hadith of Qabisah ibn al-Mukhariq which we mentioned, and it is ill-gotten gain for him. And Samurah also reported the like of that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2819","Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from Zayd ibn 'Uqbah, who said: I heard Samurah ibn Jundub, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), say: The asker is scratches with which a man scratches his face; whoever wishes keeps his face intact, and whoever wishes leaves off, except that a man asks one in authority, or asks in a matter from which he finds no escape."
"2820","Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"2821","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from Zayd ibn 'Uqbah, from Samurah ibn Jundub, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith permitted asking in every matter in which asking is unavoidable, so included in that is what asking was permitted for in the hadith of Qabisah, and this hadith adds to it other matters that are unavoidable. In that is the permission of asking due to need specifically, not due to disability. And there has been reported from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this sense, what follows."
"2822","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: al-Akhdar ibn 'Ajlan narrated to me, from Abu Bakr al-Hanafi, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man of the Ansar came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him. He said: Asking is only proper for three: a painful debt, a shocking blood-money, or crushing poverty. Abu Ja'far said: All these matters are of what is unavoidable, so that too enters into the meaning of the hadith of Samurah. And there has also been reported from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that, what Fahd, that is Ibn Sulayman, narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Sufyan, from 'Imran al-Bariqi, from 'Atiyyah ibn Sa'd, from Abu Sa'id, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Charity is not lawful for a rich man, except if he is in the path of Allah, or a wayfarer, or he has a neighbor to whom charity is given and who gifts to him, or invites him."
"2823","'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Jarud narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydillah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Layla informed us, from 'Atiyyah, from Abu Sa'id, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted charity to a man if he is in the path of Allah or a wayfarer, and that combines the healthy and the unhealthy. That also indicates that charity becomes lawful through poverty, whether disability accompanies it or not. And there has been reported from Wahb ibn Khanbash, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what follows."
"2824","Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: al-Mu'alla ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id informed me, he said: Mujalid informed me, from al-Sha'bi, from Wahb, who said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was standing at 'Arafah and asked him for his cloak, so he gave it to him and he went off with it. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Asking is only lawful due to crushing poverty or shocking debt, and whoever asks people to enrich his wealth thereby, it is scratches on his face, and embers he eats from Hell; if little, then little, and if much, then much. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also informed in this hadith that asking becomes lawful through poverty and debt, and that is evidence that it becomes lawful through these two meanings specifically, with no difference in that between the state of the disabled and others."
"2825","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Mukhawwal ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Hubshi ibn Junadah, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever asks without poverty only eats live coals."
"2826","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, and he mentioned the same with its chain. So this is Hubshi, who narrated this from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), agreeing with what the others narrated, that asking is only lawful through poverty. And reports have also come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to that effect consecutively transmitted."
"2827","al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us. And Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, they both together said: from Sufyan, from Hakim ibn Jubayr, from Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid al-Nakha'i, from his father, from Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No servant asks for something while having what suffices him, except it comes as disgrace, or scratches, or lacerations on his face on the Day of Resurrection. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, and what is his sufficiency? He said: Fifty dirhams, or its equivalent in gold."
"2828","Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Abu Hisham al-Rifa'i narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, and he mentioned the same with its chain, except that he said: scratches on his face without doubt, adding: It was said to Sufyan: What if it were from other than Hakim? He said: Zubayd narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, the same."
"2829","Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Suwayd narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: Rabi'ah ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Abu Kabshah al-Saluli, he said: Sahl ibn al-Hanzaliyyah narrated to me, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever asks people while outwardly rich only seeks to increase thereby the coals of Hell. I said: O Messenger of Allah, and what is outward richness? He said: That he knows that with his family is what gives them lunch, or what gives them supper."
"2830","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah, from Qatadah, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Ma'dan ibn Abi Talhah, from Thawban, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever asks while having what suffices him, it comes as disgrace on his face on the Day of Resurrection."
"2831","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Rijal narrated to us, from 'Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sa'id al-Khudri, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever asks while having the value of an ounce has asked importunately."
"2832","Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Fudayl narrated to us, from 'Umarah al-Qa'qa', from Abu Zur'ah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever asks people for their wealth to increase thereby, it is but live coal, so let him take little of it or much."
"2833","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, from a man of Banu Asad, who said: I settled with my family at Baqi' al-Gharqad, and my family said to me: Go to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and ask him for something for us to eat, and they kept mentioning their need. So I went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and found with him a man asking him, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was saying: I do not find what to give you. The man turned away angry, saying: By my life, you favor whom you wish. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He angers me because I do not find what to give him; whoever of you asks while having an ounce or its equivalent has asked importunately. The Asadi said: I said: A milch camel of ours is better than an ounce. He said: And the ounce is forty dirhams. He said: So I returned and did not ask him. After that, barley, raisins, and butter were brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he distributed to us of it until Allah enriched us."
"2834","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Hajari, from Abi al-Ahwas, from 'Abdullah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Hands are three: Allah's hand is the highest, the giver's hand is next to it, and the asker's hand is the lowest until the Day of Resurrection. So remain chaste as much as you can, do not neglect yourself, there is no blame for sufficiency, and when Allah brings you good, let it be seen upon you. Abu Ja'far said: The asking which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted in all these reports is for poverty and nothing else. Correcting the meanings of these reports, in our view, requires that the one meant by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his words: Charity is not lawful for one of sound strength, is other than the one he excepted in the hadith of Wahb ibn Khanbash with his words: except for crushing poverty or shocking debt, and that he is the one who intends by his asking to increase his wealth and become rich from charity wealth, so that these reports are sound and their meanings agree and do not contradict. This meaning we placed upon these reports is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them. If someone asks about the meaning of the hadith of 'Umar reported from him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in something like this, which is what follows."
"2835","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid narrated to us that Huwaytib ibn 'Abd al-'Uzza informed him that 'Abdullah ibn al-Sa'di informed him that he came to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, during his caliphate, and 'Umar said to him: Have I not been told that you undertake tasks of the people, and when you are given the wage you dislike it? He said: Yes. So 'Umar said: What do you want by that? I said: I have horses and slaves, and I trade, and I want my wage to be charity upon the Muslims. So 'Umar said: Do not do it, for I too wanted what you wanted, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to give me gifts, and I would say: Give it to one more in need of it than I, until he once gave me wealth and I said that to him. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take it and make it wealth; whatever comes to you of this wealth while you are neither aspiring nor asking, take it, and what is not, do not follow it with your soul. He said: In this hadith is also the prohibition of asking. It is said to him: This is not about charity wealth; it is about the wealth which the ruler distributes among the people, distributing it to their rich and poor, as 'Umar assigned stipends to the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he registered the registers, assigning to their rich and poor. Those wealths are given to people not on account of poverty, but for their rights in them. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked for 'Umar, when he gave him of it, his saying: Give it to one more in need of it than I, that is: I did not give you that because you are poor; I gave it to you for another meaning besides poverty. Then he said to him: Take it and make it wealth. That also indicates it is not of charity wealth, because the poor man should not take of charities what he makes into wealth, whether after asking or without asking. Then he said: Whatever comes to you of this wealth whose ruling is this, while you are not aspiring, that is, taking it without aspiration; aspiration is that you intend by it what you have been forbidden. His words: nor aspiring, may also mean: do not take of the Muslims' wealth more than what is due to you in it, for that would be excess in it, nor asking, that is, nor asking of it what is not due to you. This is the purport of this chapter, in our view, and Allah knows best. As for what came about charity wealth, we have given its meanings in what preceded of this chapter."
"2836","Chapter: A woman: is it permissible for her to give her husband of her wealth's zakat or not? Fahd narrated to us, he said: 'Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from al-A'mash, he said: Shaqiq narrated to me, from 'Amr ibn al-Harith, from Zaynab, wife of 'Abdullah, he said: I mentioned it to Ibrahim, and Ibrahim narrated to me, from Abu 'Ubaydah, from 'Amr ibn al-Harith, from Zaynab, wife of 'Abdullah, the same exactly. She said: I was in the mosque, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw me in the mosque and said: Give charity even from your jewelry. Zaynab used to spend on 'Abdullah and orphans in her care, so she said to 'Abdullah: Ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) whether it suffices me if I spend on you and on orphans in my care from charity? He said: You ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So I went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and found a woman of the Ansar at the door with a need like mine. Bilal passed by us, so I said: Ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for us: does it suffice me to give charity to my husband and orphans in my care from charity? And we said: Do not inform about us. She said: He entered and asked him, and he said: Who are they? He said: Zaynab. He said: Which Zaynab is she? He said: The wife of 'Abdullah? He said: Yes; she shall have the reward of kinship and the reward of charity. Abu Ja'far said: A group held that it is permissible for a woman to give her husband of her wealth's zakat, arguing with this hadith; among those who held that are Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them both. Others disagreed with them, among them Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, saying: It is not permissible for a woman to give her husband of her wealth's zakat, just as it is not permissible for him to give her of his wealth's zakat. Part of the proof for them against the adherents of the first view, in the hadith of Zaynab with which they argued against them, is that the charity to which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) urged in that hadith was of other than zakat. That is clarified by what follows."
"2837","Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from 'Ubaydillah ibn 'Abdillah, from Ra'itah bint 'Abdillah, wife of 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, and she was a skilled craftswoman, while 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, had no wealth, so she used to spend on him and on his child from it. She said: By Allah, you and your child have kept me from charity, so I cannot give charity along with you of anything. He said: I would not like you to do it if you have no reward in that. So she asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she and he, saying: O Messenger of Allah, I am a woman of craft, I sell of it, while my child and my husband have nothing; they have kept me busy so I do not give charity. Do I have reward for them? He said: You have in that the reward of what you spend on them, so spend on them. In this hadith, that charity is of what has no zakat in it. This Ra'itah is Zaynab, wife of 'Abdullah; we do not know that 'Abdullah had any wife other than her in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The proof that that charity was voluntary, as we mentioned, is her words: I was a skilled woman, working with my hand and selling of that, spending on 'Abdullah. So the words of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this hadith and in the first hadith were an answer to this question of hers. And in this hadith of Ra'itah: I used to spend of that on 'Abdullah and on his child from me. They have agreed that it is not permissible for a woman to spend on her child of her zakat. Since what she spent on her child is not of zakat, likewise what she spent on her husband is not of zakat either. And there has also been reported from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what indicates that the charity whose spending on her husband the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted her was of other than zakat."
"2838","Fahd narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Kathir al-Ansari narrated to us, from 'Umar ibn Nubayh al-Ka'bi, from al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left the dawn prayer one day and came upon the women in the mosque and said: O assembly of women, I have never seen any deficient in mind and religion taking away the minds of the judicious more than you, and I have seen that you are the majority of the people of Hell on the Day of Resurrection, so draw near to Allah with what you can. Among the women was the wife of 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with her. She went back to 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, informed him of what she heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and took jewelry of hers. Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Where are you going with this jewelry? She said: I draw near with it to Allah and to His Messenger, hoping Allah will not make me of the people of Hell. He said: Bring that here; woe to you, give it in charity to me and to my child. She said: No, by Allah, until I take it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). She went to seek permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). They said: O Messenger of Allah, here is Zaynab seeking permission. He said: Which Zaynab is she? They said: The wife of 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud. She entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I heard from you a statement, so I went back to Ibn Mas'ud and told him, and I took my jewelry to draw near with it to Allah the Almighty and to you, hoping Allah will not make me of the people of Hell. Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Give it in charity to me and to my sons, for we are a proper place for it. I said to him: Until I ask permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Give it in charity to him and to his sons, for they are a proper place for it."
"2839","al-Husayn ibn al-Hakam al-Hibari narrated to us, he said: 'Asim ibn 'Ali narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi 'Amr informed me, from Abu Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Abu Ja'far said: Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, clarified in this hadith that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only meant by his words: give charity, the voluntary charity that expiates sins. And in his hadith he said: She brought jewelry of hers to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, take this, I draw near with it to Allah the Almighty and to His Messenger. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Give it in charity to 'Abdullah and to his sons, for they are a proper place for it. That was upon charity of all the jewelry, and that is of voluntary charity, not of zakat, because zakat does not obligate charity of all wealth, but only charity of a part of it. This too is evidence of the invalidity of Abu Yusuf's interpretation, may Allah have mercy on him, and those who held his view, of the first hadith. It is thus invalidated, by what we mentioned, that there is in the hadith of Zaynab anything indicating that a woman gives her husband of her wealth's zakat if he is poor. Rather we seek its ruling thereafter through reasoned analogy and the evidences of the principles: we considered that and found that the woman, by their agreement, her husband does not give her of his wealth's zakat even if she is poor, and she is not in that like others, because we see a sister, her brother gives her of his zakat if she is poor, even though her brother must spend on her, and she is not thereby removed from the ruling of those given zakat. Thus it is established that what prevents the husband from giving his wife of his wealth's zakat is not the obligation of maintenance for her upon him, but the bond between him and her; that became like the kinship between him and his parents in preventing him from giving them of zakat. Since it is established by what we mentioned that the woman's bond which prevented her husband from giving her of his wealth's zakat even if she is poor is like the bond between him and his parents which prevents him from giving them of his zakat even if they are poor, and we see the parents also do not give him of their zakat if he is poor, so what is between him and his parents of kinship prevents him from giving them of zakat and prevents them from giving him of zakat. Likewise the bond between husband and wife: since it prevented him from giving her of zakat, it also prevents her from giving him of zakat. We also see this bond between husband and wife preventing acceptance of the testimony of each one of them for the other, so they are made in that like kin of forbidden marriage, whose testimony each for the other is not permitted. We also see each one of them does not take back what he gifted to the other, in the view of one who permits taking back gifts between relatives. Since the spouses, in what we mentioned, have been made like kin of forbidden marriage in what is barred of accepting testimony and taking back gifts, they are by analogy likewise in each one's giving the other of zakat. This is the reasoned view in this chapter, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on him."
"2840","Chapter: Pasturing horses: is there charity in them or not? Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Mu'alla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned horses and said: They are for three: for a man a reward, for a man a cover, and upon a man a burden. As for the one for whom it is a cover, it is the man who takes them for honor and beauty, without forgetting the right of their backs and bellies in hardship and ease."
"2841","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abu Salih al-Samman, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, except that he said: And he did not forget Allah's right in their necks nor in their backs, only."
"2842","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Aslam, and he mentioned the same with its chain. Abu Ja'far said: A group held zakat obligatory in horses if they are males and females whose owner seeks their offspring. They argued for obligating zakat in them with the words of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And he did not forget Allah's right in them. They said: In this is evidence that Allah has a right in them, like His right in other wealth in which zakat is due. They argued in that with what was reported from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him."
"2843","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma' narrated to us, he said: Juwayriyah narrated to us, from Malik, from al-Zuhri, that al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid informed him, he said: I saw my father value horses and pay its charity to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him."
"2844","Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, that 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, used to take from a horse ten, and from a mule five."
"2845","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar and al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, and he mentioned the same with its chain. Among those who held this view also are Abu Hanifah and Zufar, may Allah have mercy on them both. Others disagreed with them, among them Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on them both, saying: There is no charity at all in pasturing horses. Part of the proof for them against the adherents of the first view, in what they argued with for their view, of the words of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And he did not forget Allah's right in them, is that it is possible that right is a right other than zakat. For there has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what follows."
"2846","Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn 'Abdillah narrated to us, from Abu Hamzah, from 'Amir, from Fatimah bint Qays, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: In wealth there is a right besides zakat, and he recited this verse: Righteousness is not that you turn your faces, to the end of the verse. Since we see wealth assigned in it a right besides zakat, it is possible that the right which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned concerning horses is that right too. Another proof is that the zakat in the hadith we narrated from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, is only about tied horses, not pasturing horses. Another proof is that we see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also mentioned pasturing camels and said: Therein is a right. He was asked about that right: what is it? He said: Lending its stallion, lending its bucket, and gifting its fat milch animal."
"2847","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us of that, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Since camels too have in them a right besides zakat, it is possible that horses are likewise. As for what they argued with of what we narrated from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, they have no proof in it either, in our view, because 'Umar did not take that from them as obligatory upon them. The reason for which 'Umar ibn al-Khattab took that has been clarified by Harithah ibn Mudarrib."
"2848","Fahd narrated to us of that, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Qasim, known as Suhaym al-Harrani, narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Harithah ibn Mudarrib, who said: I performed hajj with 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with them both. Nobles of the nobles of Syria came to him and said: O Commander of the Believers, we have acquired riding animals and wealth; take of our wealth charity by which you purify us, and which shall be zakat for us. He said: This is something the two before me did not do, but wait until I ask the Muslims. So he asked the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), among them 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him. They said: It is good, while 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, remained silent and did not speak with them. He said: What is with you, O Abu al-Hasan, that you do not speak? He said: They have advised you, and there is no harm in what they said, if it is not an obligatory command nor a fixed tribute taken from them. He said: So he took from every slave ten, from every horse ten, from every crossbreed eight, and from every mule or donkey five dirhams in the year, and he provided for them every month: for a horse ten dirhams, for a crossbreed eight, for a mule five and five, and for a slave two jaribs every month. This hadith indicates that what 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, took from them for that reason, what he took from them therein, was not zakat but charity other than zakat. 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to them: This was not done by the two before me, meaning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. That indicates that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, did not take, of what was in their presence, of horses any charity, and none of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) denied 'Umar what he said of that. The words of 'Ali to 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both: They have advised you, if it is not a standing tribute nor an obligatory tax, and 'Umar's acceptance of that from him, indicate that 'Umar only took from them at their asking him to take from them and disburse it in charities, and that they may withhold that from him whenever they wish. Then 'Umar treated slaves in that like horses, yet that was no proof that slaves not for trade incur charity; it was only voluntary giving by their masters. And there has been reported from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: I have pardoned you the charity of horses and slaves."
"2849","Fahd narrated to us of that, he said: 'Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from al-A'mash, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from 'Asim ibn Damrah, from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2850","'Ali ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sufyan and Sharik informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"2851","Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ishaq ibn Abi 'Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. That too negates there being charity in horses. If someone says: Along with that he coupled slaves, and since that does not negate charity being obligatory in slaves if they are for trade, likewise that does not negate zakat being obligatory in horses if pasturing; and as his words: I have pardoned you the charity of slaves, is only upon slaves for service specifically, likewise his words: I have pardoned you the charity of horses, is only upon riding horses specifically, it is said to him: This admits what you mentioned. And when zakat is not negated by this hadith, it is negated by what we mentioned before it, of what is in the hadith of Harithah, because in it 'Ali said to 'Umar what we mentioned, indicating that the meaning of the words of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in 'Ali's view, may Allah be pleased with him, was upon negating zakat from them even if pasturing. And there has been reported from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what is close in meaning to the meaning of the hadith of 'Asim and al-Harith from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him."
"2852","Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Sulayman ibn Yasar narrating, from 'Irak ibn Malik, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: There is no charity upon the Muslim in his slave nor in his horse."
"2853","Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb and Sa'id ibn 'Amir narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Sulayman, from 'Irak, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"2854","Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"2855","Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"2856","Muhammad ibn 'Isa ibn Fulayh narrated to me, he said: Abu al-Aswad al-Nadr ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us, from Sulayman, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Isa ibn Fulayh, that is Ibn Bilal, from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"2857","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi informed me, from Makhul, from 'Irak, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"2858","Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Khuthaym ibn 'Irak, from his father, and he mentioned the same with its chain. Since there is in nothing we mentioned of these reports evidence for the obligatoriness of zakat in pasturing horses, and there is in them what negates zakat from them, it is established by authenticating these reports that the view of those who see no zakat in them holds. This is the purport of this chapter by way of reports. As for its purport by way of reasoned analogy: we see that those who obligate zakat in them only obligate it when they are males and females whose owner seeks their offspring; zakat is not due in their males alone, nor in their females alone. The agreed-upon zakats in pasturing livestock are due in camels, cattle, and sheep, whether all males or females. Since the ruling of males alone in that, the ruling of females alone, and the ruling of males and females together are alike, and the males of horses alone and the females alone incur no zakat, likewise by analogy the females and males together incur no zakat. Another proof: we see mules and donkeys bear no zakat even if pasturing, while camels, cattle, and sheep bear zakat if pasturing; the disagreement is only about horses. So we sought to see which of the two categories it most resembles, to attach its ruling to its ruling. We saw horses are hoofed, and likewise donkeys and mules are hoofed, while livestock of cattle, sheep, and camels are cloven/soft-footed. So the hoofed resembles the hoofed more than the cloven-footed. Thus it is established that there is no zakat in horses, as there is no zakat in donkeys and mules. This is the view of Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them both, and it is the dearer of the two views to us. And that has been reported from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab."
"2859","Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, and he said: I said to Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab: Is there charity upon mules? He said: Is there charity upon horses?"
"2860","Chapter: Zakat: does the ruler take it or not? Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Humayd, from al-Hasan, from 'Uthman ibn Abi al-'As, that the delegation of Thaqif came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said to them: You shall not be mustered nor tithed."
"2861","Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, from Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir al-Bajali, from 'Amr ibn Hurayth, from Sa'id ibn Zayd ibn 'Amr ibn Nufayl, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O assembly of Arabs, praise Allah, since He has lifted the tithes from you."
"2862","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, from a man who narrated to him, from 'Amr ibn Hurayth, from Sa'id ibn Zayd, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say, and he mentioned the same."
"2863","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad and al-Himmani narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, from Harb ibn 'Ubaydillah, from his grandfather Abu Umayyah, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There are no tithes upon the Muslims; tithes are only upon the people of dhimmah. Abu Ja'far said: A group held that the ruler has no right to send over the Muslims one to take charge of their charities; rather the Muslims have the choice: if they wish they deliver them to the ruler who disburses them in their places which Allah the Almighty commanded him, and if they wish they distribute them in those places themselves. The ruler may not take them from them without their willing consent. They argued in that with these reports we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and with what was reported from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him."
"2864","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from 'Amr, from Muslim ibn Yasar, who said: I said to Ibn 'Umar: Did 'Umar tithe the Muslims? He said: No. Others disagreed with them in that and said: The ruler may appoint owners of wealth over the charities of their wealth until they place them in their places, and the ruler may also send collectors over them until they tithe them and take zakat from them. Part of the proof against the adherents of the first view is that the tithe which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lifted from the Muslims is the tithe that was taken in the pre-Islamic age, which is other than zakat; they used to call it maks, and it is that concerning which 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what follows."
"2865","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Shimasah, from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The taker of maks shall not enter Paradise, meaning the tithe-collector. So this is the tithe lifted from the Muslims; as for zakat, it is not. That is also clarified by what follows."
"2866","Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, from Harb ibn 'Ubaydillah, from a man of his maternal uncles, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) employed him over charity, taught him Islam, and informed him of what to take. He said: O Messenger of Allah, all of Islam I have learned except charity: shall I tithe the Muslims? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Only Jews and Christians are tithed. In this hadith, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him over charity and ordered him not to tithe the Muslims, saying to him: Tithes are only upon Jews and Christians. That indicates that the tithes lifted from the Muslims are other than zakat. Among what also clarifies that is that."
"2867","Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, from Harb ibn 'Ubaydillah al-Thaqafi, from an uncle of his of Bakr ibn Wa'il, who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him about camels and sheep: shall I tithe them? He said: Tithes are only upon Jews and Christians, not upon the Muslims. This indicates that the tithe which is not upon the Muslims, taken from Jews and Christians, is other than zakat, because what is taken from Christians and Jews of that is a right of the Muslims due upon them, like the tribute due to them upon them, while zakat is not so, because it is only taken as purification for the owner of wealth, and he is rewarded for paying it. The Jews and Christians: what is taken from them of the tithe is not purification for them, nor are they rewarded for it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lifted from the Muslims what is taken from them of what brings them no reward, and affirmed that upon Jews and Christians."
"2868","Abu Bakrah and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu 'Amir narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mihran, that 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz wrote to Ayyub ibn Shurahbil: Take from the Muslims, of every forty dinars a dinar, and from the People of the Book of every twenty dinars a dinar, when they intend them, then take nothing from them until the year's end, for I heard that from one who heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say that. In this hadith: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the collectors to take of the Muslims' wealth what we mentioned, and of the dhimmis' wealth what we described. And there has been reported from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, what agrees with this."
"2869","Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Mu'adh ibn Mu'adh al-'Anbari narrated to us, from Ibn 'Awn, from Anas ibn Sirin, who said: Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, sent for me, and I delayed upon him, then he sent for me and I came to him. He said: I deemed that had I ordered you to bite upon such-and-such stone seeking my pleasure, you would have done it. I chose a task for you and you disliked it; or shall I write for you the practice of 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him? He said: I said: Write for me the practice of 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: So he wrote: Take from the Muslims, of forty dirhams a dirham, and from the people of dhimmah of every twenty dirhams a dirham, and from one with no dhimmah, of every ten dirhams a dirham. He said: I said: Who has no dhimmah? He said: The Romans who used to come from Syria. Since 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, did this in the presence of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and no denier among them denied it to him, that was proof and consensus of them upon it. This is the purport of this chapter by way of reports. As for its purport by way of reasoned analogy: we see that they do not disagree that the ruler may send to owners of pasturing livestock to take from them the charity of their livestock when charity becomes due in them, and likewise he does with their fruits, then places that in the places of zakats as He the Almighty commanded him, with none of the Muslims refusing that. By analogy, the rest of wealths, gold, silver, and trade goods, are likewise. As for the meaning of the words of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): There are no tithes upon the Muslims; tithes are only upon Jews and Christians, it is upon what I have explained above in this chapter; I heard Abu Bakrah narrate that from Abu 'Umar al-Darir. All this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. And there has been reported from Yahya ibn Adam in explaining the Prophet's words (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): There are no tithes upon the Muslims; tithes are only upon Jews and Christians, a meaning other than the meaning we mentioned: that the Muslims incur nothing, by their passing with their wealth before the tithe-collector, which was not already due upon them had they not passed it before him, because zakat is upon them in whatever state they are. The Jews and Christians, had they not passed with their wealth before the tithe-collector, nothing would be due upon them in it. So what was lifted from the Muslims is what passing with wealth before the tithe-collector imposes, and that was not lifted from Jews and Christians."
"2870","Chapter: Animals with defects: are they taken in livestock charities or not? Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd ibn Kasib narrated to us, he said: 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from 'A'ishah, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a collector at the beginning of Islam and said: Take the old and the young camel, and those with defects, and do not take the choicest of people's property. Hisham said: I deem that to conciliate them, then the Sunnah proceeded after that."
"2871","Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub narrated to us, he said: Waki' narrated to us, from Hisham, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar. Abu Ja'far said: A group adopted this report and said: Thus should the collector take. Others disagreed with them in that and said: He does not take one with defect in charities; rather he takes a just average of the property. They argued in that with what follows."
"2872","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Thumamah ibn 'Abdillah, from Anas, that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, when he was made caliph, directed Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, to Bahrayn and wrote him this letter. This is the obligation, meaning the charity which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated upon the Muslims, which Allah the Almighty commanded His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with. Whoever of the believers is asked it in its proper manner, let him give it; whoever is asked more than it, let him not give it. Then he mentioned the obligations of charity, saying: Neither an old nor a defective animal is taken in charity, nor the he-goat of sheep."
"2873","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Hakam ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to me, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote a letter to the people of Yemen containing the obligations and practices, writing in it: Neither an old nor a defective animal is taken in charity, nor the he-goat of sheep. Thus ran the letters of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and of Abu Bakr and 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, after him; and 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote after that. What we mentioned indicates the abrogation of what is in the hadith of 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, with which we began this chapter. In it too is what indicates its precedence, by what we narrated after it, namely the words of 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to send a collector at the beginning of Islam and ordered him with that; and that was abrogated by what we mentioned in the letter of Abu Bakr to Anas and in the letter of 'Amr ibn Hazm. All this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"2874","Chapter: Zakat of what comes out of the earth. Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini, from his father, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no charity in less than five wasqs, no charity in less than five dhawds, and no charity in less than five ounces."
"2875","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn 'Amir narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from 'Amr ibn Yahya, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"2876","'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id informed us, from 'Amr, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"2877","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abdillah ibn Salim, Malik, Sufyan al-Thawri, and 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar informed me that 'Amr ibn Yahya narrated to them, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"2878","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Yahya, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"2879","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Yahya ibn 'Umarah, from Abu Sa'id, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"2880","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sa'sa'ah al-Mazini, from his father, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"2881","Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Dinar informed us, from Jabir ibn 'Abdillah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no charity in anything of crops or vines until it is five wasqs, nor in silver until it reaches two hundred dirhams."
"2882","Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no charity in less than five wasqs."
"2883","'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Musa al-Ashyab narrated to us, he said: Shayban ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no charity in less than five camels, nor five ounces, nor five wasqs of charity."
"2884","Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'mar narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Layth narrated to us, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"2885","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, similar, but he did not raise it."
"2886","Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"2887","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Hakam ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Dawud, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to me, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to the people of Yemen a letter containing the obligations and practices, writing in it: What the sky waters, or is rain-fed or spring-irrigated, therein is the tenth when it reaches five wasqs; and what is watered by bucket or water-wheel, therein is half the tenth when it reaches five wasqs. Abu Ja'far said: A group adopted these reports and said: No charity is due in anything of wheat, barley, dates, and raisins until it is five wasqs. Likewise everything the earth produces, such as chickpeas, lentils, mash, and the like, there is no charity in any of it until it reaches this measure too. Among those who held that are Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them both, and the people of Madinah. Others disagreed with them and obligated charity in little or much of that. They argued in that with what follows."
"2888","Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, he said: 'Asim ibn Abi al-Najud narrated to me, from Abu Wa'il, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to Yemen and ordered me to take of what the sky waters and what is watered by seepage the tenth, and of what is watered by buckets half the tenth."
"2889","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Hamid ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"2890","Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: My uncle 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In what the sky waters is the tenth, and in what is watered by beasts half the tenth."
"2891","Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated, in what rivers and springs water, or is rain-sustained watered by the sky, the tenth, and in what is watered by the camel half the tenth."
"2892","Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"2893","Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"2894","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to me that Abu al-Zubayr narrated to him that he heard Jabir ibn 'Abdillah mention from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: In what rivers and clouds water is the tenth, and in what is watered by beasts half the tenth. Abu Ja'far said: In these reports, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) assigned in what the sky waters what is mentioned therein, without fixing a measure in that. In that is evidence for the obligatoriness of zakat in all that comes out of the earth, little or much. If someone of those who hold the view of the people of Madinah says: These reports you narrated in this section do not contradict the reports you narrated in the first section, except that the first are elaborated and these are summary, so the elaborated thereof is worthier than the summary, it is said to him: This is impossible, because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed in these reports that that due of the tenth or half-tenth is in what is watered by rivers, springs, buckets, or water-wheels; the wording is upon all that comes out of what is watered thereby. You yourselves reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he turned Ma'iz away when he came and confessed adultery before him four times, then stoned him after that. And you reported that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Unays: Go to this man's wife; if she confesses, stone her. So you made this evidence that the consideration is confession of adultery once, because that is the outward meaning of the Messenger of Allah's words (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): If she confesses, stone her, and you did not make the elaborated hadith of Ma'iz decisive over the summary hadith of Unays, so that the confession mentioned in the summary hadith of Unays is the confession mentioned in the elaborated hadith of Ma'iz. Since you did this in what we mentioned, what do you deny against one who does in the zakat hadiths what we described? Rather the hadith of Unays is worthier to be conjoined to the hadith of Ma'iz, because in it confession is mentioned, and his confessing once is not confession of adultery that obligates the hadd upon him in your opponent's view. While the hadith of Mu'adh, Ibn 'Umar, and Jabir, may Allah be pleased with them, in zakat only mentions obligating it in what is watered by such-and-such and what is watered by such-and-such. That is worthier to be contradictory to what mentions wasqs than the hadith of Unays to the hadith of Ma'iz. The hadith of Mu'adh, Jabir, and Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, has been interpreted upon what we mentioned, and its meaning held to what we described: Ibrahim al-Nakha'i and Mujahid."
"2895","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: In everything the earth produces there is charity."
"2896","Muhammad ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn A'yan narrated to us, from Khusayf, from Mujahid, who said: I asked him about the zakat of food, and he said: In little or much of it, the tenth and half the tenth. Sound reasoned analogy also indicates that: we see zakats due in wealths and livestock in a known measure thereof, after a known time, which is the year. Those things become due with a known measure and a known time. Then we see what the earth produces, zakat is taken of it at the time it produces, without waiting for a time. Since it lapses that it have a time in which zakat becomes due by its arrival, it lapses that it have a measure in which zakat becomes due by reaching it. So the ruling of measure and appointed time in this is alike: when one of them lapses the other lapses, just as in the wealths we mentioned they were alike: when one of them is established the other is established. This is the reasoned view, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on him."
"2897","Chapter of estimation. Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Nafi' narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Farms used to be leased in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on condition that the landowner gets what is above the water-channel of the crop, and a portion of the straw, I do not know how much. Nafi' said: Rafi' ibn Khadij came, and I was with him, and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave Khaybar to the Jews on condition that they work it and cultivate it, on condition that they get half of what comes out of it of fruit or crops, on condition that we keep you in it as long as we wish. He said: 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah estimated it upon them, and they complained to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his estimation. So 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah said to them: You have the choice: if you wish it is yours, and if you wish it is ours; we estimate it and pay you half of it. They said: By this the heavens and the earth stand."
"2898","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awn al-Ziyadi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Allah granted Khaybar as fay', and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept them as they were, making it between him and them. He sent 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah, who estimated it upon them, then said: O assembly of Jews, you are the most hateful of creation to me; you killed Allah's prophets, lied about Allah, yet my hatred of you does not carry me to wrong you. I have estimated upon you twenty thousand wasqs of dates: if you wish it is yours, and if you wish it is mine."
"2899","Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Nafi' narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Salih narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, from 'Attab ibn Asid, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to estimate grapes as raisins as he estimates fresh dates. Abu Ja'far said: A group held that the fruit in which the tenth is due, such is its ruling: it is estimated while fresh dates as dry dates, its measure known, handed over to its owner, who thereby owns Allah the Exalted's right in it, with the like of it in measure of dry dates due upon him; likewise is done with grapes. They argued in that with these reports. Others disagreed with them, disliking that, saying: There is in none of these reports that the dates were fresh at the time they were estimated in the hadith of Ibn 'Umar and Jabir, may Allah be pleased with them both. How could they have been fresh then, made over to their owner with Allah's right in them for a measure of dry dates due upon him on credit? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade selling dates on palm-tops for dates by measure, and forbade selling fresh dates for dry dates on credit, and authentic reported reports came of that from him; we have mentioned that elsewhere in this book of ours, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made no exception in that. So the purport of what we narrated about estimation, in our view, is not upon what you mentioned; rather its purport, in our view, and Allah knows best, is that the estimation of Ibn Rawahah was only intended to know thereby the measure of what was in each people's hands of fruits, so the like of it in measure is taken at harvest time, not that they own thereby anything of what is due to Allah in it for a substitute that does not leave them. How could that be, when after that a blight may strike and destroy them, or fire burn them, so what is taken from their owner as substitute for Allah the Exalted's right in them is taken from him as substitute for what was not delivered to him? Rather only what we mentioned was intended by that estimation, and likewise in the hadith of 'Attab ibn Asid, it is upon what we described of that too. That is also indicated by what follows."
"2900","Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Khubayb ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mas'ud ibn Niyar, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you estimate, take, and leave a third; if you do not leave a third, leave a fourth. We know that cannot be at the time zakat is taken, because if its fruit reached the measure in which zakat becomes due, nothing of what became due upon him in it would be dropped from him; what became due upon him in it would be taken from him in full; this is of what the Muslims agreed upon. Rather the reduction mentioned in this hadith is only before that, at the time its people eat of the fruit, before the time of taking zakat from it. So he ordered the estimators to drop, of what they estimate, the measure mentioned in this hadith, so it is not counted against the owners of fruits at the time of taking zakat from them. And there has been reported from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that he too used to order the estimators with that."
"2901","3098 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, sent Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah to estimate (the dates on the palms) for the people, and he ordered him - when he found the people among their palm trees - not to estimate against them what they eat. So this too is a proof of what we have mentioned. And there has also been reported from Abu Humayd al-Saidi regarding the description of the estimation of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates what we have mentioned."
"2902","3099 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud and Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us, they said: al-Wuhazi narrated to us. (H). 3100 - And Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman and Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, they said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, they said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini narrated to us, from Abbas ibn Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi, from Abu Humayd al-Saidi, he said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the expedition of Tabuk, and we came to Wadi al-Qura at the garden of a woman, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Estimate it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) estimated it, and we estimated it as ten awsuq, and he said: Count it until I return to you, if Allah the Exalted wills. When we came to it, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked her about her garden: How much did its dates reach? She said: Ten awsuq. So in this hadith also is that they estimated it and ordered her to count it until they returned to her. That is a proof that she was not made owner of it by their estimating it beyond what she already owned before that. They only intended by that to know the amount of what was in her palm trees in particular, then to take zakat from her at the time of cutting, according to what is due in it. This is the meaning of these reports according to us, and Allah knows best. Some people have said regarding estimation other than this saying; they said: In the earliest time, what the people of the first view said was done - that the estimators gave the owners of the fruits Allah's right in them, while they were fresh dates, in exchange, taking dried dates from them - then that was abrogated by the abrogation of riba, and matters were returned to taking in zakats only what is permissible in sales. And they mentioned regarding that what:"
"2903","3101 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade estimation (khars), and said: What do you think - if the fruit perishes, would any one of you like to consume his brother's wealth falsely? So this is the aspect of this chapter by way of reports. As for its aspect by way of reasoning: we have seen that zakat is due on various things, among them gold, silver, the fruits the earth produces, palm trees and trees, and pasturing livestock. All have agreed that if a man owed zakat on his wealth, whether gold or silver or pasturing livestock, and the collector handed it over to him in a manner upon which sales are not permissible, that is not permissible for him. Do you not see that if a man owed zakat on his dirhams and the collector sold it to him for gold on credit, that is not permissible; likewise if he sold it to him for gold and then parted before taking possession, that is not permissible; likewise if zakat was due on his livestock and then the collector handed it over to him for an unknown exchange, or for a known exchange to an unknown term, all of that is unlawful and impermissible. So everything forbidden in sales among people selling to one another, the ruling of the collector in selling it to the owner of the wealth in which zakat is due, which the collector undertakes to take from him, is included in it. Since what we mentioned is so regarding the properties we described, reasoning likewise requires that the ruling of fruits be so. Just as selling fresh dates for dried dates on credit is impermissible in matters other than charities, likewise it is impermissible in matters of charities, between the collector and the owner of the wealth. This is also the reasoning in this chapter, and it returns to what we directed the reports narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), which we mentioned earlier, to. We take this position, and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"2904","9 - Chapter on the amount of Sadaqat al-Fitr. 3102 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Iyad ibn Abd Allah ibn Said ibn Abi Sarh, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: We used to give Zakat al-Fitr of Ramadan a sa of food, or a sa of dates, or a sa of barley, or a sa of dried yogurt (aqit)."
"2905","3103 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Iyad ibn Abd Allah, that he heard Abu Said saying: We used to pay Sadaqat al-Fitr a sa of food, or a sa of barley, or a sa of dates, or a sa of dried yogurt, or a sa of raisins."
"2906","3104 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Qays narrated to us, from Iyad ibn Abd Allah ibn Sad, from Abu Said, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was among us, we used to pay Sadaqat al-Fitr either a sa of food, or a sa of dates, or a sa of barley, or a sa of raisins, or a sa of dried yogurt. We continued to pay it until Muawiyah came performing Hajj or Umrah, and among what he addressed the people with, he said: Give two mudds of the wheat of Sham, which equals a sa of barley."
"2907","3105 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Nafi informed me, from Dawud ibn Qays, from Iyad, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2908","3106 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar informed us, he said: Dawud narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. And he added: Abu Said said: As for me, I shall continue to pay as I used to pay."
"2909","3107 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Iyad, from Abu Said, he said: Regarding the charity of Ramadan, whoever brought a sa of barley it was accepted from him, and whoever brought a sa of dried yogurt it was accepted from him, and whoever brought a sa of dates it was accepted from him, and whoever brought a sa of raisins it was accepted from him."
"2910","3108 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us. (H). 3109 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, they said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abd Allah ibn Uthman, that Iyad ibn Abd Allah narrated to him that Abu Said said: We only used to pay in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a sa of dates, or a sa of barley, or a sa of dried yogurt; we would not pay other than it. When food became abundant in the time of Muawiyah, they made it two mudds of wheat."
"2911","3110 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Uthman, from Iyad ibn Abd Allah, he said: I heard Abu Said, while he was asked about Sadaqat al-Fitr, saying: I shall pay only what I used to pay in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): a sa of dates, or a sa of barley, or a sa of raisins, or a sa of dried yogurt. A man said to him: Or two mudds of wheat? He said: No; that is the valuation of Muawiyah; I do not accept it nor act upon it. Abu Jafar said: So some people went to these reports, and said regarding Sadaqat al-Fitr: whoever wishes to give it from wheat gives it a sa, and likewise if he wishes to give it from barley, dates, or raisins. Others differed with them in that, and said: He gives Sadaqat al-Fitr from wheat half a sa, and from other than wheat of the kinds we mentioned, a sa. Among the proof for them against the people of the first view was that the hadith of Abu Said which they used as proof against them only contains information about what they used to give. And it is possible that they were giving from that what was due upon them, plus extra as a surplus not due upon them. And there has been reported from other than Abu Said regarding wheat contrary to what was reported from Abu Said. Among that is what:"
"2912","3111 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us. (H). 3112 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us. And Ibn Abi Maryam said: Ibn Lahiah informed us, from Abu al-Aswad, from Fatimah bint al-Mundhir, from Asma bint Abi Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them both, she said: We used to pay Zakat al-Fitr in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) two mudds of wheat."
"2913","3113 - Fahd and Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me that Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to him, from his father, that Asma bint Abi Bakr informed him that she used to pay in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on behalf of her household, the free among them and the slave, two mudds of wheat, or a sa of dates by the mudd or by the sa with which they trade."
"2914","3114 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Uzayz narrated to us, he said: Salamah narrated to us, from Uqayl, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Asma, she said: We used to pay Zakat al-Fitr in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) two mudds. So this Asma informs that they used to pay Zakat al-Fitr in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) two mudds of wheat. And it is impossible that they would do this except by the order of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), because this would not be taken at that time except through his directing them to what is due upon them of that. So the reconciliation of what was reported from Asma and what was reported from Abu Said is to make what they used to pay according to what she - meaning Asma - mentioned the obligation, and what they used to pay according to what Abu Said mentioned an addition to that, which is voluntary."
"2915","3115 - And the proof of the correctness of what we mentioned of this is that Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, that Marwan sent to Abu Said: Send me the zakat of your slaves. Abu Said said to the messenger: Marwan does not know; we are only required to give for each head, at every Fitr, a sa of dates, or half a sa of wheat. So this Abu Said has informed in this of what is due upon him in Zakat al-Fitr on behalf of his slaves, and that indicates what we mentioned, and that what was reported from him of more than that was his choice, not an obligation. And reports have come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he obligated in Zakat al-Fitr, agreeing with this also."
"2916","3116 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Arim narrated to us. (H). 3117 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Sadaqat al-Fitr, for every young and old, free and slave, a sa of barley or a sa of dates. He said: Then the people made it equal to two mudds of wheat."
"2917","3118 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2918","3119 - Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Isa narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, the like of it."
"2919","3120 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi and Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, they said: Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it, except that he did not mention the equalization."
"2920","3121 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him. (H). 3122 - And Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Maslamah narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Except that he said: For every free and slave, male and female, among the Muslims."
"2921","3123 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Tariq narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, from Yunus ibn Yazid, that Nafi informed him, he said: Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated Zakat al-Fitr, a sa of dates or a sa of barley, upon every person, free male or slave, among the Muslims. He said: And Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to say: Then the people made its equal two mudds of wheat. So the saying of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both: Then the people made its equal two mudds of wheat, he means thereby the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), whose equalization is permissible and whose saying must be adhered to. For there has been reported from Umar the like of that regarding the expiation of oaths, that he said to Yasar ibn Numayr: I swear that I shall not give some people anything, then it appears best to me so I do it; when you see me doing that, feed on my behalf ten poor persons, each poor person half a sa of wheat, or a sa of dates or barley. And the like of that was reported from Ali, and we shall mention that in its place in this book of ours, if Allah the Exalted wills, although there has been reported from Umar, and from Abu Bakr also, and from Uthman ibn Affan, regarding Sadaqat al-Fitr, that it is from wheat half a sa, and we shall mention that also in this chapter, if Allah the Exalted wills. That indicates that they are the ones who equalized what we mentioned of wheat with the amount of barley and dates which we mentioned, and they would not do that except by consultation of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and their agreement with them upon that. If nothing had been reported to us regarding the amount given from wheat in Zakat al-Fitr except this equalization, that would be - according to us - a great proof establishing that amount of wheat, that it is half a sa. How much more so since there has also been reported - along with that - from Asma that she used to pay that amount in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also. Then there has been reported in other than these reports which we mentioned from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what agrees with that also. Among that is what:"
"2922","3124 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from al-Numan ibn Rashid, from al-Zuhri, from Thalabah ibn Abi Suayr, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A sa of wheat, or grain, for every two persons, free or slave, male or female; as for your rich, Allah will purify him, and as for your poor, there will be returned to him more than what he gave."
"2923","3125 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from al-Numan ibn Rashid, from al-Zuhri, from Thalabah ibn Abi Suayr, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pay Zakat al-Fitr a sa of dates, or a sa of barley, or half a sa of wheat - or he said: grain - for every person, young or old, male or female, free or owned, rich or poor."
"2924","3126 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Zakat al-Fitr for every free and slave, male or female, young or old, rich or poor - a sa of dates, or half a sa of wheat. Mamar said: And it reached me from al-Zuhri that he used to raise it (to the Prophet)."
"2925","3127 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid and Uqayl ibn Khalid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Said ibn al-Musayyib: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated Zakat al-Fitr two mudds of wheat."
"2926","3128 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2927","3129 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu Zurah narrated to us, he said: Haywah informed us, he said: Uqayl informed us, from Ibn Shihab, that he heard Said ibn al-Musayyib, Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, and Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Zakat al-Fitr, with a sa of dates, or with two mudds of wheat."
"2928","3130 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah, al-Qasim, and Salim, they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded regarding Sadaqat al-Fitr, with a sa of barley, or two mudds of wheat."
"2929","3131 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, from Said, Ubayd Allah, al-Qasim, and Salim, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2930","3132 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Abd al-Khaliq al-Shaybani, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Charity used to be given in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, half a sa of wheat. So these reports which we mentioned from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding wheat have come with the like of what the people equalized after him, and Abu Said; and there has been reported from him from his opinion what agrees with that. And what was reported from him of what Iyad ibn Abd Allah mentioned about him in his saying: That is the valuation of Muawiyah; I do not accept it nor act upon it, does not contradict what was reported from him; because in that he did not deny the valuation, he only denied the valuer. This is what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding Sadaqat al-Fitr, and we have mentioned some of what was reported from Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, regarding that. And there has also been reported regarding that from Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, what agrees with that."
"2931","3133 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar and Hilal ibn Yahya narrated to us, they said: Abu Awanah informed us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Abu Qilabah, he said: One who handed to Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, a sa of wheat between two persons, informed me."
"2932","3134 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar narrated to us, he said: Hammad informed us, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, he said: I and al-Hakam ibn Utaybah went to Ziyad ibn al-Nadr, and he narrated to us from Abd Allah ibn Nafi that his father asked Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: I am an owned man, so is there zakat in my wealth? Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Your zakat is only upon your master, to pay on your behalf at every Fitr a sa of barley or dates, or half a sa of wheat."
"2933","3135 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Nuaym narrated to us, from Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Abi Suayr, he said: We used to pay Zakat al-Fitr in the time of Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, half a sa."
"2934","3136 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Ashath, he said: Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed us and said in his sermon: Pay Zakat al-Fitr a sa of dates, or a sa of barley, for every young and old, free and owned, male and female."
"2935","3137 - Abu Zurah Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: al-Qawariri narrated to us. And he mentioned with his chain from Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, that he addressed them and said: Pay Zakat al-Fitr two mudds of wheat, and he did not mention other than that of what Ibn Abi Dawud mentioned. So these Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, have agreed upon that of what we mentioned. And the like of that has also been reported from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"2936","3138 - Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Isa narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: I commanded the people of Basra, when I was among them, to give on behalf of the young and old, free and owned, two mudds of wheat. And the like of that has also been reported from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz and others among the Successors."
"2937","3139 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Humran narrated to us, he said: Awf narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to Adi ibn Artah a letter, and he read it upon the pulpit of Basra, while I heard: To proceed: order those under you among the Muslims to pay Zakat al-Fitr a sa of dates or half a sa of wheat."
"2938","3140 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah informed us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim and Mujahid, the like of it."
"2939","3141 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Mansur, from Mujahid: Regarding Zakat al-Fitr - a sa of everything other than wheat, and wheat half a sa."
"2940","3142 - Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from Said ibn al-Musayyib regarding the zakat of Ramadan, he said: A sa of dates, or half a sa of wheat."
"2941","3143 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: I think Affan narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: I asked al-Hakam, Hammad, and Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim about Sadaqat al-Fitr and they said: Half a sa of wheat. So this is all that we have narrated in this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from his Companions after him, and from their Successors after them; all of it is that Sadaqat al-Fitr from wheat is half a sa, and from other than wheat a sa. And we do not know of anyone among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) nor among the Successors from whom contrary to that was reported, so no one should oppose that, since it has become consensus in the time of Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman, and Ali, may Allah be pleased with them, up to the time of those we mentioned among the Successors. Then reasoning also indicates that, for we have seen them agree that it is from barley and dates a sa. So we looked at the ruling of wheat among the things paid on their behalf - dates and barley - how is it? We found that the expiations of oaths are agreed to be feeding from these kinds also, then there was disagreement about its amount from them. Some people said: its amount from dates and barley is half a sa, and from wheat a mudd, like half of that. Others said: rather it is from wheat half a sa, and from other than that a sa. And all of them have equated wheat with twice it of dates and barley, so reasoning required thereby, since Sadaqat al-Fitr is a sa of dates and barley, that it be from wheat like half of that, which is half a sa. This is also the reasoning in this chapter, and it has agreed with what the reports we mentioned brought, so we take this position, and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"2942","10 - Chapter: How much is the weight of the sa? 3144 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Shuja, Sulayman ibn Bakkar, and Ahmad ibn Mansur al-Ramadi narrated to us, they said: Yala ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from Musa al-Juhani, from Mujahid, he said: We entered upon Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and one of us asked for drink, so a large cup (uss) was brought. Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to bathe with the like of this. Mujahid said: So I estimated it as I estimate: eight ritls, nine ritls, ten ritls. Abu Jafar said: So some went to the view that the weight of the sa is eight ritls, and they argued with this hadith, and said: Mujahid did not doubt about the eight, he only doubted about what is above it, so the eight is established by this hadith, and what is above it is negated. Among those who held this saying is Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him. Others differed with them in that, and said: Its weight is five ritls and a third of a ritl, and among those who said that is Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, and they said: This with which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to bathe is a sa and a half. And they mentioned regarding that what:"
"2943","3145 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Zaidah narrated to us, from Jafar ibn Burqan, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I used to bathe, I and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from one vessel, and it is the faraq."
"2944","3146 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, she said: I used to bathe, I and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from one vessel, from one cup, called the faraq."
"2945","3147 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, and he mentioned with his chain similar to it. They said: Since it is established by this hadith reported from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to bathe, he and she, from the faraq, and the faraq is three sas, what each one of them bathed with was a sa and a half. So when that is eight ritls, the sa is two-thirds of it, which is five ritls and a third of a ritl, and this is also the saying of the people of Madinah. Among the proof against them for the people of the first view was that the hadith of Urwah from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, only mentions the faraq from which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and she used to bathe; she did not mention the amount of water in it - whether it was its fill or less than that. For it is possible that he and she bathed with its fill, and it is possible that he and she bathed with less than its fill, of what is two sas, so each one of them bathed with a sa of water, and the meaning of this hadith would agree with the meanings of the hadiths reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he used to bathe with a sa. For there has been reported from him regarding that what:"
"2946","3148 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman informed us, from Hajjaj, from Ibrahim, from Safiyyah bint Shaybah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform ablution with a mudd and bathe with a sa."
"2947","3149 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2948","3150 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Muslim, meaning al-Mulai, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to bathe with a sa."
"2949","3151 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Safiyyah bint Shaybah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to bathe with the measure of a sa, and perform ablution with the measure of a mudd."
"2950","3152 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim narrated to us, he said: Aban narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Safiyyah bint Shaybah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to bathe with a sa and perform ablution with a mudd."
"2951","3153 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, from Said, from Qatadah, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he said: with a mudd and the like of it."
"2952","3154 - Muhammad ibn al-Abbas ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: al-Mubarak ibn Fadalah narrated to us, he said: My mother narrated to me, from Muadhah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform ablution with a mudd and bathe with a sa."
"2953","3155 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, he said: Baqiyyah narrated to us, from Utbah ibn Abi Hakim, he said: Abd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Jabr ibn Atik narrated to me, he said: We asked Anas about the ablution - what suffices a man of water. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform ablution from a mudd and perform it thoroughly, and some of it might remain. He said: We asked him about bathing from janabah: how much water suffices? He said: The sa. So I asked about it: Is the mention of the sa from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes, along with the mudd."
"2954","3156 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform ablution with a mudd and bathe with a sa."
"2955","3157 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Bishr narrated to us, he said: Abu Rayhanah narrated to us, from Safinah, the freed slave of Umm Salamah, he said: A sa of water sufficed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for washing, and a mudd of water sufficed him for ablution. So in these reports: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to bathe with a sa, but there is no mention of the weight of the sa, how much it is. And in the hadith of Mujahid from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, is mention of the weight of what he used to bathe with, which is eight ritls. And in the hadith of Urwah from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her: that she and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to bathe from one vessel, which is the faraq. So in this hadith is mention of what they bathed from in particular, but no mention of the amount of water they bathed with. And in the other reports is mention of the amount of water he bathed with, that it was a sa. So it is established thereby, when these reports are authenticated, combined, and their meanings uncovered - that he used to bathe from a vessel which is the faraq, and with a sa whose weight is eight ritls. Thereby is established what Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, held. Muhammad ibn al-Hasan also held that. And there has also been reported from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, what indicates this meaning."
"2956","3158 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Isa, from Ibn Jubayr, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform ablution with a mudd, which is two ritls."
"2957","3159 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Isa, from Abd Allah, meaning Ibn Jabr, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform ablution with two ritls, and bathe with a sa. So this Anas has informed that the mudd of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is two ritls, and the sa is four mudds. So when it is established that the mudd is two ritls, it is established that the sa is eight ritls. If one says: Anas ibn Malik has had reported from him contrary to this, and he mentioned what:"
"2958","3160 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Jubayr informed us, he heard Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform ablution with a makkuk, and bathe with five makakik. He said: So this hadith contradicts the first hadith. It was said to him: There is in this - according to us - no contradiction to it; because the hadith of Sharik only contains that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform ablution with a mudd, and Utbah ibn Abi Hakim agreed with him upon that, reporting from Abd Allah ibn Jubayr similar to that. Since Shubah reported what we mentioned from Abd Allah ibn Jubayr, it is possible that he meant by makkuk the mudd, because they used to call the mudd a makkuk, so what he performed ablution with would be a mudd, and what he bathed with would be five makakik - he bathed with four of them, which are four mudds, which is a sa, and performed ablution with another, which is a mudd. So he combined in this hadith what he performed ablution with for janabah and what he bathed with for it. And he singled out in the hadith of Utbah what he bathed with for it in particular, apart from what he performed ablution with, and that that ablution is for it also. And I heard Ibn Abi Imran saying: I heard Ibn al-Thalji saying: The measure of the sa is only upon the weight of what is balanced in measure and weight of mung beans, raisins, and lentils, for it is said that the measure and weight of that are equal."
"2959","3161 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Salih and Bishr ibn al-Walid both informed us, from Abu Yusuf, he said: I came to Madinah, and one whom I trust brought out to me a sa and said: This is the sa of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So I measured it and found it five ritls and a third of a ritl. And I heard Ibn Abi Imran saying: It is said that the one who brought this out to Abu Yusuf is Malik ibn Anas. And I heard Abu Hazim mentioning that Malik was asked about that and said: It is the determination of Abd al-Malik for the sa of Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him. So as if Malik, when it was established with him that Abd al-Malik determined that from the sa of Umar, and the sa of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, is the sa of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the sa of Umar had been measured upon contrary to that."
"2960","3162 - So Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, from Ali ibn Salih, from Abu Ishaq, from Musa ibn Talhah: He - al-Hajjaji - said: The sa of Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him."
"2961","3163 - Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Yaqub narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, from his father, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: We measured the sa of Umar and found it Hajjaji, and the Hajjaji according to them is eight ritls by the Baghdadi measure."
"2962","3164 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Bishr al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Mughirah and Ubaydah, from Ibrahim, he said: al-Hajjaj placed his qafiz upon the sa of Umar. So this is more worthy than what Malik mentioned of the determination of Abd al-Malik, because determination is not accompanied by reality, while what Ibrahim and Musa ibn Talhah mentioned of measuring is accompanied by reality. So this is more worthy, and with Allah is success. The Book of Zakat is completed."
"2963","5 - Book of Fasting. 1 - Chapter on the time in which food becomes unlawful for the fasting person. 3165 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, he said: I ate suhur, then set out to the mosque, and passed by the house of Hudhayfah, so I entered upon him, and he ordered a milch camel so it was milked, and a pot so it was heated, then he said: Eat. I said: I intend to fast. He said: And I intend to fast. He said: So we ate, then we drank, then we came to the mosque, and the prayer was established. He said: This is what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did with me - or: I did with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I said: After dawn? He said: After dawn, except that the sun had not risen. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith from Hudhayfah is that he ate after the rising of dawn while intending to fast, and the like of that is related from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And there has come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) contrary to that, which is what we have reported from him of what we mentioned earlier in this book of ours: that he said: Bilal calls at night, so eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktum calls. And that he said: Let not the adhan of Bilal prevent any of you from his suhur, for he only calls so that your sleeper may wake and your standing one may return - then he described dawn with what he described it. That indicates that it is what prohibits food, drink, and other than that of what the fasting person is prohibited from. These reports we mentioned contradict the hadith of Hudhayfah. And the hadith of Hudhayfah may bear - according to us, and Allah knows best - that it was before the revelation of His Exalted saying: And eat and drink until the white thread becomes distinct to you from the black thread of dawn, then complete the fast until night. For indeed."
"2964","3166 - Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Salim narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Husayn and Mujalid informed us, from al-Shabi, he said: Adi ibn Hatim informed us, he said: When this verse was revealed: And eat and drink until the white thread becomes distinct to you from the black thread, I took two cords, one black and the other white, and kept looking at them, but the white would not become distinct to me from the black. When morning came, I went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the morning and informed him of what I had done, so he said: Your pillow is indeed wide; that is only the whiteness of day and the blackness of night."
"2965","3167 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from al-Shabi, from Adi, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2966","3168 - Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Idris al-Awdi narrated to us, from Husayn, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2967","3169 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: al-Fudayl ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi, he said: When: And eat and drink until the white thread becomes distinct to you from the black thread was revealed, a man would take a white thread and a black thread and place them under a pillow, and look when he could distinguish them, then leave food. He said: So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, clarified that, and: of dawn was revealed. So since the ruling of this verse had been obscure to the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until Allah, Mighty and Majestic, clarified for them of that what He clarified, and until: of dawn was sent down after what had been sent down: until the white thread becomes distinct to you from the black thread - the ruling was that they eat and drink until that becomes distinct to them, until Allah, Mighty and Majestic, abrogated by His saying: of dawn, upon what we mentioned, what Sahl clarified in his hadith. And it is possible that what Hudhayfah reported of that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was before the revelation of that verse; when Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down that verse, He made that firm and returned the ruling to what He clarified in it. And there has also been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding that what:"
"2968","3170 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym and al-Khadir ibn Muhammad ibn Shuja narrated to us, they said: Mulazim ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Badr al-Suhaymi narrated to us, he said: My grandfather Qays ibn Talq narrated to me, he said: My father narrated to me that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Eat and drink, and let not the ascending shining (dawn) alarm you; eat and drink until the redness spreads for you - and he gestured with his hand and spread it. So a verse of the Book of Allah the Exalted in text, and successive hadiths from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which the ummah has accepted and acted upon from the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until today, must not be abandoned for a hadith which may be abrogated by what we mentioned in this chapter. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"2969","2 - Chapter: A man intending fasting after dawn has risen. 3171 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah and Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, from Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever does not resolve fasting before dawn, there is no fast for him."
"2970","3172 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2971","Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ruayni narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me from Yahya ibn Ayyub, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So some people went to the view that when a man does not intend to enter fasting before the rising of dawn, it does not suffice him to fast that day of his with an intention occurring to him after that, and they argued with this hadith. Others differed with them in that, and said: This hadith is not raised (to the Prophet) by the memorizers who narrate it from Ibn Shihab, and they differ from him in it with a difference that necessitates disturbance of the hadith by less than it. But - along with that - we affirm it, and place it upon a specific kind of fast, which is the obligatory fast which is not in particular days, like fasting in expiations, making up Ramadan, and what resembles that."
"2972","As for what we mentioned of the narration of the memorizers of this hadith from al-Zuhri, and of their differing from him in it: 3173 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Aishah and Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with them both, with that which we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter."
"2973","3174 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Hamzah ibn Abd Allah, from his father, from Hafsah, Mother of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with her, with that, and he did not raise it."
"2974","3175 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, he said: Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with her, with that, and he did not raise it. So these Malik, Mamar, and Ibn Uyaynah - and they are the proof from al-Zuhri - have differed in the chain of this hadith as we mentioned. And it has also been narrated from al-Zuhri by others than these, contrary to what Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr also narrated."
"2975","3176 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, he narrated to him from Salim, from his father with that, and he did not mention Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with her, nor raise it."
"2976","3177 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to us, from al-Saib ibn Yazid, from al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wadaah, from Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with her, with that, and he did not raise it. Then Nafi also narrated it from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, with that, and he did not mention Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with her, also, nor raise it."
"2977","3178 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us. (H) And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad informed me, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, the like of it. So this is the origin of this hadith, and there has also been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the permissibility of entering fasting after the rising of dawn."
"2978","3179 - Abu Bakrah, Ibrahim ibn Marzuq, and Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, they said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Talhah ibn Yahya, from Aishah bint Talhah, from Aishah, Mother of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to like food, so he came one day and said: Do you have any of that food? I said: No. He said: Then I am fasting."
"2979","3180 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: al-Thawri narrated to us, from Talhah, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. So that, according to us, is upon a specific kind of fast also, which is voluntary fasting which a man intends after morning in the first part of the day. And a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after him acted upon that."
"2980","3181 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb and Rawh narrated to us, they said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: When one of you wakes in the morning then wishes to fast after the morning, then he is at one of two considerations."
"2981","3182 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: Whenever you wake in the morning of a day, you are at one of two considerations, so long as you have not eaten or drunk: if you wish, fast, and if you wish, break fast."
"2982","3183 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Harith al-Awar, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it."
"2983","3184 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Talhah ibn Musarrif, from Sad ibn Ubaydah, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, that Hudhayfah decided to fast after the sun had passed the meridian, and he fasted."
"2984","3185 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from al-Mustawrid, a man from Banu Asad, from a man among them, that he stayed with a debtor of his, so he came to Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: I stayed with a debtor of mine from Murad until near midday, and I neither fasted nor broke fast. He said: If you wish, fast, and if you wish, break fast."
"2985","3186 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, he said: A man said to Anas ibn Malik: I ate suhur, then it occurred to me to break fast. He said: If you wish, break fast. Abu Talhah used to come and say: Do you have any food? If they said: No. He said: I am fasting."
"2986","3187 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yazid al-Rahabi narrated to us, from Sahm ibn Hubaysh - and none remained of those who witnessed the killing of Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, other than him - that Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, woke in the morning of the day in which he was killed, and said: Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, came to me in this night and said to me: O Uthman, you will break fast with us tonight. And I call you to witness that I have made fasting obligatory (upon myself)."
"2987","3188 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Abi Amr narrated to me, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, that he would wake in the morning until forenoon, then say: By Allah, I woke in the morning not intending to fast, and I have not eaten any food nor drink since today, yet I shall fast this day of mine."
"2988","3189 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, that Abu Talhah used to come to his family in the forenoon and say: Do you have lunch? If they said: No, he fasted that day."
"2989","3190 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu al-Fayd, he said: I heard Abd Allah ibn Sayyar al-Dimashqi, he said: Abu al-Darda bargained with a man over a horse, and the man swore not to sell it. When he had gone, he said: Come, I dislike making you sinful; I have not visited a sick person today, nor fed a poor person, nor prayed Duha, but I am fasting the rest of my day."
"2990","3191 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Ayyub informed us, from Abu Qilabah, he said: Umm al-Darda narrated to us that Abu al-Darda used to come and say: Do you have any food? If they said: No. He said: I am fasting."
"2991","3192 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Thabit, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Utbah, that Abu Ayyub used to do that also."
"2992","3193 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata claimed that he used to do that. So this fasting in which intention after the rising of dawn suffices, in which has come the hadith which we mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and which those we mentioned among his Companions after him acted upon, is voluntary fasting. And there has also been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he ordered the people on the day of Ashura after they had woken in the morning to fast, while at that time fasting it was obligatory upon them, just as fasting Ramadan after that became obligatory upon the people; and reports were narrated from him regarding that which we shall mention in the chapter on fasting the day of Ashura, after this chapter of this book, if Allah the Exalted wills. Since these reports have come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon what we mentioned, it is not permissible to make some of them contradict others, so they negate one another and repel one another, so long as we find a way to authenticate them and explain their aspect. So the hadith of Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, which we mentioned from her in this chapter, is regarding voluntary fasting; such is its aspect according to us. And what was reported regarding Ashura is regarding fasting made obligatory on the particular day. Likewise the ruling of obligatory fasting on that day: it is permissible to contract the intention for it after the rising of dawn. And among that is the month of Ramadan, for it is obligatory in particular days, like the day of Ashura when it was obligatory on a particular day. Just as the day of Ashura sufficed whoever intended its fast after the morning, likewise the month of Ramadan suffices whoever intends the fast of a day of it likewise. And there remains after this what we reported in the hadith of Hafsah from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); it is - according to us - regarding fasting which is other than these two fasts, of fasting expiations and making up the month of Ramadan, so that it does not contradict anything of what we mentioned in this chapter and others. And the ruling of the intention with which one enters fasting is upon three aspects. Whatever of it is obligatory on a particular day, that intention suffices before the entry of that day in the night, and in that day also. And whatever of it is obligatory not on a particular day, the intention with which one enters it is in the night before it, and it does not suffice after the entry of the day. And whatever of it is voluntary, the intention with which one enters it is in the night before it, and in the day after that. This is the aspect upon which the reports we mentioned are explained, without contradicting one another; it is the most worthy of what they are carried upon. And to that went Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. Except that they used to say: Whatever of what we mentioned the intention after the rising of dawn suffices in, it suffices in the first part of the day, and does not suffice in what is after that."
"2993","3 - Chapter: The meaning of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Two months of festival do not decrease: Ramadan and Dhu al-Hijjah. 3194 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq and Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, they said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Hammad informed us, from Salim ibn Abd Allah ibn Salim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two months of festival do not decrease: Ramadan and Dhu al-Hijjah."
"2994","3195 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith is that these two months do not decrease, and the people spoke about its meaning. Some people said: They do not decrease, meaning their decrease does not coincide in one year. And it is possible that one of them decreases. And this is a saying which observation has refuted, for we have found them decreasing in years, and that may be combined in each one of them. Some people refuted that, by this and by the hadith of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which we mentioned in other than this place, that he said regarding the month of Ramadan: Fast upon seeing it, and break fast upon seeing it; if it is obscured to you, count thirty. And by his saying: The month may be twenty-nine, and may be thirty. So he informed that that is permissible in every month of the months, and we shall mention that with its chain in its place in this book of ours, if Allah wills. And others went to authenticating all these reports, and said: As for his saying: Fast upon seeing it and break fast upon seeing it, for the month may be twenty-nine and may be thirty - all of that is as he said, and it exists in all the months. As for his saying: Two months of festival do not decrease, Ramadan and Dhu al-Hijjah - that is not - according to us - upon decrease of number, but they contain what other than them of the months does not contain: in one of them fasting, and in the other Hajj. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed them that they do not decrease, even if they are twenty-nine; and they are two complete months, whether they are thirty thirty or twenty-nine twenty-nine, so that it be known thereby that the rulings in them, even if they are twenty-nine twenty-nine, are complete in them, not deficient from their ruling when they are thirty thirty. This is the aspect of authenticating these reports which we mentioned in this chapter, and Allah knows best."
"2995","4 - Chapter: The ruling regarding one who has intercourse with his wife in Ramadan deliberately. 3196 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said informed us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn al-Zubayr, from Abbad ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned to him that he was burnt, so he asked him about his matter, and he said: I fell upon my wife in Ramadan. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a basket called the araq, containing dates, and said: Where is the burnt one? The man stood. He said: Give this in charity. Abu Jafar said: So some people went to the view that whoever falls upon his wife in Ramadan must give charity, and nothing of expiation besides charity is due upon him. And they argued with this hadith in that. Others differed with them in that, and said: Rather he must free a slave, or fast two consecutive months, or feed sixty poor persons - whichever of that he wishes, he does. And they argued in that with what:"
"2996","3197 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man broke fast in Ramadan in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to expiate by freeing a slave, or fasting two consecutive months, or feeding sixty poor persons. He said: I do not find. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought an araq containing dates, and said: Take this and give it in charity. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I do not find anyone more needy of it than me. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed until his eyeteeth appeared, then said: Eat it."
"2997","3198 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to him that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered a man who broke fast in the month of Ramadan to free a slave or fast two consecutive months, or feed sixty poor persons. They said: The Messenger only gave him what he gave him of what he ordered him to give in charity after he had informed him of what was due upon him in that, of what Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, clarified in this hadith of his. And others also differed with them in that, and said: Rather by freeing a slave if he finds one, or he fasts two consecutive months if he does not find a slave; if he is unable to do that, he feeds sixty poor persons. Among the proof for them in that was that the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, which we mentioned in the section before this section, has included in it the hadith of Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, as they mentioned. And the origin of that hadith of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, in it is beginning with the slave if the one who had intercourse finds one, then fasting after it if the one who had intercourse does not find a slave, then feeding after them both if the one who had intercourse does not find them - such is the origin of the hadith which al-Zuhri narrated regarding that. And likewise the rest of the people narrated it from him, other than Malik and Ibn Jurayj, and they clarified therein the story at length how it was, and how the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered expiation in that."
"2998","3199 - Fahd narrated to me, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn Musafir narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a man came and said: O Messenger of Allah, I am ruined. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Woe to you, what is the matter with you? He said: I fell upon my wife while fasting in Ramadan. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Can you find a slave to free? He said: No. He said: Can you fast two consecutive months? He said: No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Can you find food for sixty poor persons? He said: No, O Messenger of Allah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell silent. While we were upon that, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought an araq containing dates - and the araq is the basket. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Where is the questioner just now? Take this and give it in charity. The man said: Upon a household poorer than me, O Messenger of Allah? By Allah, there is no household between its two lava plains - meaning the two Harras - poorer than my household. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed until his eyeteeth appeared, then said: Feed it to your household. He said: So the expiation became freeing a slave, or fasting two consecutive months, or feeding sixty poor persons."
"2999","3200 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shuayb informed us, from al-Zuhri, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. So this is the hadith upon its proper aspect; the hadith of Malik and Ibn Jurayj regarding that from al-Zuhri only came upon the wording of the saying of al-Zuhri in this hadith: So the expiation became freeing a slave, or fasting two consecutive months, or feeding sixty poor persons. So the choice is the speech of al-Zuhri upon what was imagined by one who did not narrate it in his hadith from Humayd, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3000","3201 - Ismail ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he did not mention his saying: So it became a sunnah, to the end of the hadith."
